For databases that restrict access to VPC-only modes, the consuming Lambda function may need VPC placement. See the [Lambda function](/resources/compute/lambda-function) and [relational database](/resources/databases/relational-database) pages for VPC and accessibility mode configuration.

## Using injected variables in code

Access the `STP_*` variables through `process.env` in your application code:

```typescript
import { Client } from 'pg';

export const handler = async () => {
  const client = new Client({
    connectionString: process.env.STP_MY_DATABASE_CONNECTION_STRING
  });

  await client.connect();
  const result = await client.query('SELECT NOW()');
  await client.end();

  return { statusCode: 200, body: JSON.stringify(result.rows) };
};
```

For S3 operations, use the injected bucket name:

```typescript
import { S3Client, PutObjectCommand } from '@aws-sdk/client-s3';

const s3 = new S3Client({});

export const handler = async () => {
  await s3.send(
    new PutObjectCommand({
      Bucket: process.env.STP_UPLOADS_NAME,
      Key: 'hello.txt',
      Body: 'Hello, world!'
    })
  );

  return { statusCode: 200, body: 'Uploaded' };
};
```


> **Tip:** IAM permissions from `connectTo` are applied to the resource's execution role automatically. AWS SDK calls work without explicit credentials: the SDK uses the role's temporary credentials from the environment.

## Custom permissions with iamRoleStatements

When you need access to an AWS service not covered by `connectTo`, or need different permissions than `connectTo` provides, use `iamRoleStatements`:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, Bucket, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const uploads = new Bucket({});

  const reader = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/reader.ts'
    }),
    environment: { BUCKET_NAME: "$ResourceParam('uploads', 'name')" },
    iamRoleStatements: [
      {
        Resource: ["$ResourceParam('uploads', 'bucketArn')/*"],
        Action: ['s3:GetObject']
      }
    ]
  });

  return {
    resources: { uploads, reader }
  };
});
```


In this example, the reader function gets read-only access to the bucket via `iamRoleStatements` instead of the broad read/write/delete access that `connectTo` would grant. The `$ResourceParam('uploads', 'bucketArn')` directive resolves to the bucket's ARN at deploy time. The `/*` suffix targets objects inside the bucket (`s3:GetObject` acts on individual objects, so the IAM resource must be `arn:aws:s3:::bucket-name/*` rather than the bucket ARN alone. The bucket name is injected manually via `$ResourceParam()`. Always verify the exact parameter key for each resource type on the [referenceable parameters](/configuration/referenceable-parameters) page before using `$ResourceParam` in IAM resource ARNs) parameter keys vary by resource type and may differ from the `connectTo` environment variable names.

The `iamRoleStatements` property adds a separate IAM policy alongside any auto-generated policies. If you use both `connectTo` and `iamRoleStatements` targeting the same resource, both policies apply: `iamRoleStatements` does not override or narrow `connectTo` permissions.

## Scripts with connectTo

[Scripts](/configuration/hooks-and-scripts) support `connectTo` for running local commands that need access to deployed resources: database migrations, seed scripts, or health checks:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, RelationalDatabase, RdsEnginePostgres, Bastion } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myDatabase = new RelationalDatabase({
    credentials: {
      masterUserPassword: "$Secret('database', 'password')"
    },
    engine: new RdsEnginePostgres({
      version: '16',
      primaryInstance: { instanceSize: 'db.t4g.micro' }
    })
  });

  const bastion = new Bastion({});

  return {
    resources: { myDatabase, bastion },
    scripts: {
      migrate: {
        type: 'local-script-with-bastion-tunneling',
        properties: {
          bastionResource: 'bastion',
          executeCommand: 'npx prisma migrate deploy',
          connectTo: ['myDatabase']
        }
      }
    }
  };
});
```


Run the script with [`stacktape script run`](/cli/script-run):

```bash
stacktape script run migrate --stage production --region eu-west-1
```

The `local-script-with-bastion-tunneling` type tunnels connections through a [bastion host](/resources/security/bastion-host), giving your local machine secure access to VPC-only databases. The injected environment variables are automatically adjusted to route through the tunnel. Use `local-script` for commands that can reach their targets from the machine running Stacktape; use `local-script-with-bastion-tunneling` when the target must be reached through a bastion tunnel. Three script types support `connectTo`: `local-script`, `local-script-with-bastion-tunneling`, and `bastion-script`. The example uses `executeCommand`; local scripts can also use `executeCommands`, `executeScript`, or `executeScripts`, while bastion scripts support `executeCommand` and `executeCommands`.

For `local-script-with-bastion-tunneling`, `properties.bastionResource` names the [bastion host](/resources/security/bastion-host) resource used for the tunnel. For `bastion-script`, `properties.bastionResource` names the bastion server where the commands are executed. Script execution can also set `cwd` to control the working directory; local scripts can set `stdioMode` (`capture` by default, `inherit` for interactive prompts, `ignore` to discard output). See [hooks and scripts](/configuration/hooks-and-scripts) for details.

Scripts can also define `properties.environment` for static values, secrets, custom directives, or `$ResourceParam()` values when a tool expects a specific variable name that differs from the `STP_*` convention. Use `connectTo` when the script needs connection variables for specific targets. Use `assumeRoleOfResource` when the script should run with the same IAM permissions as a deployed workload: such as a function, batch job, web service, private service, worker service, multi-container workload, or SSR frontend.

## Using directives in TypeScript

In TypeScript configuration, Stacktape exposes runtime directive helpers as functions you import from the `stacktape` package. For local operations like variable access, string interpolation, and file loading, you can use native JavaScript instead: template literals, `import`, and `fs.readFileSync` cover most of what `$Var`, `$Format`, and `$File` do in YAML configs.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  LambdaFunction,
  RelationalDatabase,
  RdsEnginePostgres,
  $Secret,
  $ResourceParam,
  $Stage,
  $Region,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(({ stage }) => {
  const database = new RelationalDatabase({
    engine: new RdsEnginePostgres({
      primaryInstance: { instanceSize: 'db.t4g.micro' },
      version: '16'
    }),
    credentials: {
      masterUserPassword: $Secret('db-password')
    }
  });

  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts' }),
    environment: {
      DB_CONNECTION_STRING: $ResourceParam('database', 'connectionString'),
      STAGE: $Stage(),
      REGION: $Region()
    },
    connectTo: [database]
  });

  return { resources: { database, api } };
});
```


The examples below import runtime directive helpers like `$Secret`, `$ResourceParam`, `$CfFormat`, `$GitInfo`, `$Stage`, and `$Region` from the `stacktape` package. For most local operations in TypeScript configs, prefer native JavaScript: template literals instead of `$Format`, variables instead of `$Var`, and `fs`/`import` instead of `$File`.

## Pass deployment context to your app

The most common use of directives is to give your code values that depend on the deploy: the stage, the region, the commit, or a value you pass on the command line. They resolve when you run [`stacktape deploy`](/cli/deploy), so one config works for every stage.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/api.ts'
    }),
    environment: {
      STAGE: '$Stage()',
      REGION: '$Region()',
      GIT_COMMIT: "$GitInfo('commit')",
      LOG_LEVEL: "$CliArgs('logLevel', 'info')"
    }
  });

  return { resources: { api } };
});
```


Your code reads them as `process.env.STAGE`, `process.env.GIT_COMMIT` and so on. `LOG_LEVEL` is `info` unless you deploy with `-- --logLevel debug`. For values that differ more between stages, such as instance sizes or whole resources, see [Stages and environments](/configuration/stages-and-environments).

### $Stage()

Returns the current target stage name. Use it to differentiate resource naming, domain prefixes, or feature flags across stages.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, $Stage, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const handler = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts' }),
    environment: { STAGE: $Stage() }
  });

  return { resources: { handler } };
});
```


In TypeScript, the `stage` value is also available directly from the `defineConfig` callback parameter: `defineConfig(({ stage }) => { ... })`. Use the callback parameter when you need native JavaScript operations like template literals for domain names (e.g., `` `${stage}-api.example.com` ``).

### $Region()

Returns the AWS region where the stack is being deployed (e.g. `us-east-1`, `eu-west-1`).


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  LambdaFunction,
  $Region,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const handler = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts' }),
    environment: { DEPLOY_REGION: $Region() }
  });

  return { resources: { handler } };
});
```

### $File()

Loads and parses a file in one of the supported formats, resolved using Stacktape's configured working directory. Supported formats:

| Format | Returns |
|--------|---------|
| `.env` | Array of `{ name, value }` objects |
| `.json` | Parsed JSON object |
| `.yml` / `.yaml` | Parsed YAML object |
| `.ini` | Parsed INI object |

Access nested properties with dot notation: `$File('config.json').database.host`. If the file format is not one of the supported types, Stacktape throws a directive error suggesting you use `$FileRaw` instead.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
import { readFileSync } from 'fs';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const envVars: Record<string, string> = {};
  readFileSync('.env', 'utf-8')
    .split('\n')
    .filter(line => line.includes('='))
    .forEach(line => {
      const [name, ...rest] = line.split('=');
      envVars[name.trim()] = rest.join('=').trim();
    });

  const handler = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts' }),
    environment: envVars
  });

  return { resources: { handler } };
});
```


> **Tip:** In TypeScript configs, use native `import` or `readFileSync` for file loading. The example above parses `.env` into a `Record<string, string>` for the `environment` property's object form. In YAML configs, `$File('.env')` returns an array of `{ name, value }` objects matching the `environment` array form. The `$File()` directive is most useful in YAML configs where file loading isn't built in.

### $GitInfo()

Returns information about the current git repository. Pass the property name as a required string argument: `$GitInfo('commit')`, `$GitInfo('branch')`, `$GitInfo('username')`, or `$GitInfo('gitUrl')`.

Available properties:

| Property | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| `commit` | Current commit SHA |
| `branch` | Current branch name |
| `username` | Git username |
| `gitUrl` | Repository URL |


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  LambdaFunction,
  $GitInfo,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const handler = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts' }),
    environment: {
      GIT_COMMIT: $GitInfo('commit'),
      GIT_BRANCH: $GitInfo('branch')
    }
  });

  return { resources: { handler } };
});
```

### $Secret()

References a secret stored in AWS Secrets Manager. The named secret must be readable by Stacktape during deployment. Create secrets using [`stacktape secret set`](/cli/secret-set) or through the [Stacktape Console](/configuration/secrets).


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, RelationalDatabase, RdsEnginePostgres, $Secret } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const database = new RelationalDatabase({
    engine: new RdsEnginePostgres({
      primaryInstance: { instanceSize: 'db.t4g.micro' },
      version: '16'
    }),
    credentials: {
      masterUserPassword: $Secret('db-password')
    }
  });

  return { resources: { database } };
});
```


Deployment fails with an error if Stacktape cannot fetch the named secret, with a hint to create it using [`stacktape secret set`](/cli/secret-set).


> **Warning:** The secret's `VersionId` is baked into the CloudFormation template at deploy time. If you update a secret in AWS Secrets Manager after deployment, the running stack continues using the old version until you redeploy. This means secret rotation requires a redeployment to take effect.


When the CLI resolves `$Secret()` locally, including in `stacktape dev` and scripts, it reads the current secret value (`AWSCURRENT`), just as `$SsmParam()` reads the current parameter value. A prior deployment does not pin local directive resolution. See [Secrets](/configuration/secrets#update-and-rotate) for the distinction between local directives and deployed database credentials.

For a secret that stores a JSON object, pass the top-level key as the second argument: `$Secret('secret-name', 'key')`. The first argument is always the exact secret name, so it can contain dots. Omit the key, or pass `''`, to use the whole secret.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  LambdaFunction,
  $Secret,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const handler = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts' }),
    environment: {
      STRIPE_KEY: $Secret('api-keys', 'stripe'),
      SENDGRID_KEY: $Secret('api-keys', 'sendgrid')
    }
  });

  return { resources: { handler } };
});
```


The Stacktape 3 form `$Secret('secret-name.key')` still works and reads the same secret as before: the text before the first `.` is the secret name, and the next segment is the JSON key. Use the two-argument form in new configurations, and always for a secret whose name contains a dot.

Create a JSON secret with the CLI:

```bash
stacktape secret set --region us-east-1
```

When prompted, provide a name (e.g., `api-keys`) and a JSON value (e.g., `{"stripe": "sk_...", "sendgrid": "SG..."}`).

### $SsmParam()

References a parameter from AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store. `$SsmParam()` reads the parameter and uses the secure dynamic reference form (`ssm-secure`) when AWS reports the parameter type as `SecureString`; otherwise it uses the standard SSM dynamic reference form (`ssm`). During deployment, `$SsmParam()` reads the current parameter version and injects a CloudFormation dynamic reference pinned to that version: updating the SSM parameter requires a redeployment to pick up the new value. Use SSM parameters when integrating with other AWS tools that write to Parameter Store or when managing configuration values outside your Stacktape config.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  LambdaFunction,
  $SsmParam,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const handler = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts' }),
    environment: {
      FEATURE_FLAGS: $SsmParam('/myapp/feature-flags'),
      DB_HOST: $SsmParam('/prod/database/host')
    }
  });

  return { resources: { handler } };
});
```


SSM parameters support hierarchical paths (e.g., `/myapp/prod/database/host`). The referenced parameter must exist before deployment: if missing, deployment fails with a directive error.


> **Info:** `$Secret` resolves values from AWS Secrets Manager. `$SsmParam` resolves values from SSM Parameter Store. Use `$Secret` for credentials and sensitive values. Use `$SsmParam` for configuration parameters, feature flags, and values managed outside Stacktape.

### $ResourceParam()

References a parameter from another Stacktape resource in the same stack. The value resolves at deployment time because it depends on CloudFormation-provisioned outputs like URLs, ARNs, and connection strings. See [Referenceable parameters](/configuration/referenceable-parameters) for the full list of parameters available per resource type.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  HttpApiGateway,
  LambdaFunction,
  $ResourceParam,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new HttpApiGateway({});

  const handler = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts' }),
    environment: { API_URL: $ResourceParam('api', 'url') }
  });

  return { resources: { api, handler } };
});
```


The first argument is the resource name (as defined in the `resources` object you return from `defineConfig`). The second argument is the parameter name. If the resource or parameter doesn't exist, Stacktape lists the available options in the error message.


> **Tip:** For most resource-to-resource communication, prefer [`connectTo`](/configuration/connecting-resources) which automatically injects environment variables and grants IAM permissions. Use `$ResourceParam` when you need a value in a non-standard location or when `connectTo` doesn't cover your use case.

### $CfResourceParam()

References a parameter from a raw CloudFormation resource defined in the `cloudformationResources` section. Use this when integrating with [raw CloudFormation resources](/resources/advanced/raw-cloudformation-resources) that aren't managed by Stacktape's resource abstraction.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  LambdaFunction,
  $CfResourceParam,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const handler = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts' }),
    environment: { TOPIC_ARN: $CfResourceParam('mySnsTopic', 'TopicArn') }
  });

  return {
    resources: { handler },
    cloudformationResources: {
      mySnsTopic: {
        Type: 'AWS::SNS::Topic',
        Properties: { TopicName: 'my-notifications' }
      }
    }
  };
});
```


The first argument is the logical ID of the CloudFormation resource. The second argument is the attribute name: a `GetAtt` attribute such as `TopicArn`, `Arn` or `DomainName`, or a `Ref` attribute, which returns the resource's primary identifier. For `AWS::CloudFormation::CustomResource` types, any attribute name is accepted. Available attributes for standard types depend on the CloudFormation resource type; see the [AWS CloudFormation documentation](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSCloudFormation/latest/UserGuide/aws-template-resource-type-ref.html) for each type's return values.

### $CfFormat()

Performs string interpolation at CloudFormation deployment time, similar to `$Format()` but capable of including runtime directive values as arguments. Use `$CfFormat` when you need to combine multiple runtime values into a single string. Under the hood, `$CfFormat` produces a CloudFormation `Fn::Sub` intrinsic function.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  LambdaFunction,
  RelationalDatabase,
  RdsEnginePostgres,
  $Secret,
  $CfFormat,
  $ResourceParam,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const database = new RelationalDatabase({
    engine: new RdsEnginePostgres({
      primaryInstance: { instanceSize: 'db.t4g.micro' },
      version: '16'
    }),
    credentials: {
      masterUserPassword: $Secret('db-password')
    }
  });

  const handler = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts' }),
    environment: {
      JDBC_URL: $CfFormat(
        'jdbc:postgresql://{}:{}/{}',
        $ResourceParam('database', 'host'),
        $ResourceParam('database', 'port'),
        $ResourceParam('database', 'dbName')
      )
    }
  });

  return { resources: { database, handler } };
});
```


The key difference from `$Format()`: `$CfFormat()` accepts other runtime directives as arguments. `$Format()` only works with locally-resolved values.

### $CfStackOutput()

References an exported output from another deployed stack, resolved at CloudFormation deployment time. Unlike `$StackOutput()` (which fetches the value locally), `$CfStackOutput()` creates a CloudFormation `ImportValue` dependency: this means **the referenced stack cannot be deleted** while this stack exists, protecting shared infrastructure from accidental removal.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  LambdaFunction,
  $CfStackOutput,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const handler = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts' }),
    environment: {
      SHARED_QUEUE_URL: $CfStackOutput('shared-infra-production', 'QueueQueueUrl')
    }
  });

  return { resources: { handler } };
});
```


Arguments: `$CfStackOutput('stackName', 'outputName', region?)`. The `stackName` is the full CloudFormation stack name. The `outputName` must match the CloudFormation output key of the referenced stack. Both `$StackOutput` and `$CfStackOutput` accept an optional `region` argument: Stacktape uses it to look up the referenced stack and validate the export exists. The key difference: `$StackOutput` returns the resolved value at config-parse time, so cross-region lookups work directly. `$CfStackOutput` returns a CloudFormation `ImportValue` intrinsic, which only resolves within the deployment region. Use `$StackOutput` when you need a value from a stack in another region.


> **Warning:** The referenced output must be exported from its stack. Unexported outputs cannot be referenced and will cause a deployment error.


| Directive | Resolution time | Deletion protection | Use when |
|-----------|----------------|---------------------|----------|
| `$StackOutput()` | Config parse time (local) | None | You want a snapshot of the value; referenced stack can be deleted independently |
| `$CfStackOutput()` | CloudFormation deploy time | Referenced stack is locked | You need a live dependency that prevents accidental deletion of shared resources |

## Directive nesting

Directives can be nested up to two levels deep. A valid 2-level nesting is a directive inside another directive: for example, `$CfFormat('{}/api', $ResourceParam('api', 'url'))`. Three levels of nesting (a directive inside a directive inside a directive) causes an error. In YAML configs, nesting a runtime directive inside a local directive is also not possible because local directives resolve before deployment begins.

If you need what would be three levels, break the innermost level into a `variables` entry so it resolves first. In YAML, define a variable like `stagePrefix: $Format('{}-', $Stage())` and then reference it as `$Var().stagePrefix` inside the outer directive: this reduces the effective nesting depth to two. In TypeScript, this limit rarely matters because you use plain JavaScript variables for local operations, avoiding the YAML directive nesting chain entirely.

Example: valid 2-level nesting of `$ResourceParam` inside `$CfFormat`:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  LambdaFunction,
  $CfFormat,
  $ResourceParam,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const handler = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts' }),
    environment: {
      API_ENDPOINT: $CfFormat('{}/v1', $ResourceParam('api', 'url'))
    }
  });

  return { resources: { handler } };
});
```


In TypeScript configs, nesting constraints rarely apply because you use native JavaScript for local operations. The nesting rules matter primarily in YAML configs where you compose directives inside directive arguments.

### Local script

A local script runs wherever the Stacktape CLI runs: your laptop during development, or the CI runner in a pipeline. This is the most common script type, covering builds, database migrations, linting, and data seeding.

Scripts can run shell commands or JS/TS/Python files. The examples below use `executeCommand`; see the configuration reference for the full set of execution properties.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, Bucket, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const uploads = new Bucket({});

  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts'
    }),
    connectTo: [uploads]
  });

  return {
    resources: { api, uploads },
    scripts: {
      seedBucket: {
        type: 'local-script',
        properties: {
          executeCommand: 'node scripts/seed-data.js',
          connectTo: ['uploads']
        }
      },
      buildFrontend: {
        type: 'local-script',
        properties: {
          executeCommand: 'npm run build',
          cwd: './frontend'
        }
      }
    }
  };
});
```


The `seedBucket` script uses `connectTo` to receive the same bucket connection details as the `api` function: injected as environment variables like `STP_UPLOADS_NAME`. See [connecting resources](/configuration/connecting-resources) for the full list of variables injected per resource type. The `buildFrontend` script runs in a subdirectory using `cwd`. A local script's `stdioMode` controls its terminal: `capture` (the default) shows its output in Stacktape's progress output, `inherit` hands it the terminal, including input, for interactive programs, and `ignore` discards its output. In captured output and in a failed script's error, Stacktape replaces the secret values it injected into the script, from `$Secret`, `$SsmParam` or the secret parameters of `connectTo` resources, with `[redacted]`.

### Local script with bastion tunneling

When your database or Redis cache has no public endpoint, `local-script-with-bastion-tunneling` runs locally and tunnels connections to VPC-only resources through a [bastion host](/resources/security/bastion-host). This lets you keep using local CLI tools (Prisma, `psql`, `redis-cli`) against resources that aren't publicly reachable.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Redis, Bastion } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const cache = new Redis({
    defaultUserPassword: "$Secret('redis', 'password')",
    engine: { type: 'valkey-serverless' }
  });
  const bastion = new Bastion({});

  return {
    resources: { cache, bastion },
    scripts: {
      warmCache: {
        type: 'local-script-with-bastion-tunneling',
        properties: {
          bastionResource: 'bastion',
          executeCommand: 'node scripts/warm-cache.js',
          connectTo: ['cache']
        }
      }
    }
  };
});
```


Stacktape runs the script locally while routing network traffic to connected resources through the bastion host.

### Bastion script

A bastion script runs directly on the bastion EC2 instance inside your VPC, with output streaming back to your terminal in real-time. Use this when you need direct network access to VPC resources without running any tools locally.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Redis, Bastion } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const cache = new Redis({
    defaultUserPassword: "$Secret('redis', 'password')",
    engine: { type: 'valkey-serverless' }
  });
  const bastion = new Bastion({});

  return {
    resources: { cache, bastion },
    scripts: {
      checkRedis: {
        type: 'bastion-script',
        properties: {
          bastionResource: 'bastion',
          executeCommands: [
            'yum install -y redis6 2>/dev/null || yum install -y redis',
            'redis-cli --tls -h $STP_CACHE_HOST -p $STP_CACHE_PORT PING'
          ],
          connectTo: ['cache']
        }
      }
    }
  };
});
```


A bastion script runs on the bastion host inside your VPC. If your command needs extra tools, make sure they are available on that host (as shown above with `yum install`) or use `local-script-with-bastion-tunneling` to keep using your local toolchain.

## Hooks

Hooks attach scripts to deployment lifecycle events so they run automatically. Define hooks in the top-level `hooks` section by referencing one or more script names from your `scripts` section.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  LambdaFunction,
  HostingBucket,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts'
    })
  });

  const website = new HostingBucket({
    uploadDirectoryPath: './frontend/dist'
  });

  return {
    resources: { api, website },
    scripts: {
      buildFrontend: {
        type: 'local-script',
        properties: {
          executeCommand: 'npm run build',
          cwd: './frontend'
        }
      },
      smokeTest: {
        type: 'local-script',
        properties: {
          executeCommand: 'node scripts/smoke-test.js'
        }
      }
    },
    hooks: {
      beforeDeploy: [{ scriptName: 'buildFrontend' }],
      afterDeploy: [{ scriptName: 'smokeTest', skipOnLocal: true }]
    }
  };
});
```


The `buildFrontend` script runs before every deploy and builds the frontend into `./frontend/dist`, where the [hosting bucket](/resources/frontend/static-hosting) picks it up. The `smokeTest` script runs after deploys from CI (`skipOnLocal`). Each hook takes a list of scripts and runs them in order.

To run database migrations, use a [release command](/deployment-and-lifecycle/release-commands) rather than a hook: it runs inside the VPC before your services and functions get the new code, and a failure stops the deploy. An `afterDeploy` hook runs after the new code is live, so its failure fails the command but does not undo the deploy. If the machine that deploys a function has no Docker, which a function's release command needs, run the migration from an `afterDeploy` hook with `connectTo: ['database']`, through a bastion if the database is private.

### Basic override example

This example sets reserved concurrent executions on a Lambda function's underlying `AWS::Lambda::Function` resource:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts'
    }),
    overrides: {
      ApiFunction: {
        ReservedConcurrentExecutions: 100
      }
    }
  });

  return { resources: { api } };
});
```


Example (YAML):

```yaml
resources:
  api:
    type: function
    properties:
      packaging:
        type: js-bundle
        properties:
          entryfilePath: ./src/handler.ts
    overrides:
      ApiFunction:
        ReservedConcurrentExecutions: 100
```


The key `ApiFunction` is the logical ID of the function's `AWS::Lambda::Function` child resource. This example uses an override to set the CloudFormation `ReservedConcurrentExecutions` property directly on that child resource.

### Override with nested properties

For a nested CloudFormation property, use a dot-notation path or a nested object. This example enables time-to-live on a DynamoDB table; the path starts at the table's properties, without `Properties.`:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, DynamoDbTable } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const mainTable = new DynamoDbTable({
    primaryKey: { partitionKey: { name: 'id', type: 'string' } },
    overrides: {
      MainTableGlobalTable: {
        'TimeToLiveSpecification.Enabled': true,
        'TimeToLiveSpecification.AttributeName': 'expiresAt'
      }
    }
  });

  return { resources: { mainTable } };
});
```


Example (YAML):

```yaml
resources:
  mainTable:
    type: dynamo-db-table
    properties:
      primaryKey:
        partitionKey:
          name: id
          type: string
    overrides:
      MainTableGlobalTable:
        TimeToLiveSpecification.Enabled: true
        TimeToLiveSpecification.AttributeName: expiresAt
```


A nested object works the same way. This example configures an RDS parameter group:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, RelationalDatabase, RdsEnginePostgres, $Secret } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const db = new RelationalDatabase({
    credentials: { masterUserPassword: $Secret('db-password') },
    engine: new RdsEnginePostgres({
      version: '16',
      primaryInstance: {
        instanceSize: 'db.t4g.micro'
      }
    }),
    overrides: {
      DbDbParameterGroup: {
        Parameters: {
          max_connections: '200',
          shared_buffers: '256MB'
        }
      }
    }
  });

  return { resources: { db } };
});
```


`DbDbParameterGroup` is the logical ID of the database's `AWS::RDS::DBParameterGroup` child resource. The `Parameters` key is the CloudFormation property that accepts database engine parameters. The valid keys are RDS engine parameters for the selected PostgreSQL version and instance family: check the [AWS RDS parameter group documentation](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_WorkingWithDBInstanceParamGroups.html) for available parameters.

### S3 bucket override example


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Bucket } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const uploads = new Bucket({
    overrides: {
      UploadsBucket: {
        PublicAccessBlockConfiguration: {
          BlockPublicAcls: true,
          BlockPublicPolicy: true,
          IgnorePublicAcls: true,
          RestrictPublicBuckets: true
        }
      }
    }
  });

  return { resources: { uploads } };
});
```

### Basic transform example


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts'
    }),
    transforms: {
      ApiFunction: (props: any) => ({
        ...props,
        Layers: [
          ...(props.Layers || []),
          'arn:aws:lambda:eu-west-1:123456789012:layer:my-shared-utils:3'
        ]
      })
    }
  });

  return { resources: { api } };
});
```


This appends a Lambda layer ARN to whatever layers Stacktape already configured. With a plain override, you'd need to specify the complete final list of layers: with a transform, you append to the existing value safely.

### Transform for container workloads


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new WebService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/server.ts'
    }),
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 },
    transforms: {
      ApiTaskDefinition: (props: any) => ({
        ...props,
        ContainerDefinitions: props.ContainerDefinitions.map((container: any) => ({
          ...container,
          Ulimits: [{ Name: 'nofile', SoftLimit: 65536, HardLimit: 65536 }]
        }))
      })
    }
  });

  return { resources: { api } };
});
```


The `ApiTaskDefinition` transform modifies the ECS task definition to add file descriptor limits to all containers. This is useful for high-connection workloads (WebSocket servers, connection pools) where the default Linux `nofile` limit is too low.

### Adding a raw CloudFormation resource


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  LambdaFunction,
  $CfResourceParam,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const processor = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/processor.ts'
    }),
    environment: { TOPIC_ARN: $CfResourceParam('AlertTopic', 'TopicArn') }
  });

  return {
    resources: { processor },
    cloudformationResources: {
      AlertTopic: {
        Type: 'AWS::SNS::Topic',
        Properties: {
          TopicName: 'alert-notifications',
          DisplayName: 'Alert Notifications'
        }
      }
    }
  };
});
```


Each key in `cloudformationResources` (such as `AlertTopic` above) becomes the CloudFormation logical name. The value uses standard CloudFormation syntax with `Type` and `Properties` keys, as shown in the [AWS CloudFormation Resource Reference](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSCloudFormation/latest/UserGuide/aws-template-resource-type-ref.html). You can add multiple resources in the same block.

### CDK construct example

First, write a construct class that extends `Construct` from the `constructs` package and uses AWS CDK modules from `aws-cdk-lib` as needed:

```typescript
// cdk/monitoring.ts
import { Construct } from 'constructs';
import * as cloudwatch from 'aws-cdk-lib/aws-cloudwatch';

export class MonitoringDashboard extends Construct {
  constructor(scope: Construct, id: string, props: { functionName: string }) {
    super(scope, id);

    new cloudwatch.Dashboard(this, 'Dashboard', {
      dashboardName: `${props.functionName}-metrics`,
      widgets: [
        [
          new cloudwatch.GraphWidget({
            title: 'Invocations',
            left: [
              new cloudwatch.Metric({
                namespace: 'AWS/Lambda',
                metricName: 'Invocations',
                dimensionsMap: { FunctionName: props.functionName }
              })
            ]
          })
        ]
      ]
    });
  }
}
```

Then reference it in your Stacktape config:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  LambdaFunction,
  AwsCdkConstruct,
  $ResourceParam,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/api.ts'
    })
  });

  const monitoring = new AwsCdkConstruct({
    entryfilePath: './cdk/monitoring.ts',
    exportName: 'MonitoringDashboard',
    constructProperties: {
      functionName: $ResourceParam('api', 'name')
    }
  });

  return { resources: { api, monitoring } };
});
```


The `AwsCdkConstruct` class accepts three properties: `entryfilePath` (required, path to the file containing the construct class), `exportName` (optional, defaults to `"default"`, names the exported class), and `constructProperties` (optional, object passed as the third argument to the construct's constructor). Use `$ResourceParam` and `$CfResourceParam` inside `constructProperties` to pass dynamic values from other resources. For the full property reference and advanced patterns, see [AWS CDK constructs](/resources/advanced/aws-cdk-constructs).

## Syntax

`$ResourceParam` takes two arguments: the resource name (matching the key in your config's `resources` map) and the parameter name.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  LambdaFunction,
  HttpApiGateway,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts'
    }),
    environment: {
      GATEWAY_URL: "$ResourceParam('gateway', 'url')"
    }
  });

  const gateway = new HttpApiGateway({});

  return {
    resources: { api, gateway }
  };
});
```


The `$ResourceParam` directive resolves at deploy time. The resulting value is a string: suitable for environment variables, script inputs, stack outputs, or anywhere a string value is accepted in your Stacktape configuration.


> **Info:** Some parameters are only available when a resource is configured a certain way. Each resource's parameter table below notes the conditions under which a given parameter is exposed.


The sections below list the available parameters for each resource type. Not all resource types expose referenceable parameters: those that do are grouped by category.

### Environment variables

The most common use of `$ResourceParam` is injecting resource details into workloads via environment variables. Use it when you want to choose your own environment variable name, reference a specific documented parameter directly, or pass a value into a context that `connectTo` does not support.

In this example, the Lambda function uses `connectTo` for IAM permissions to access the bucket, and `$ResourceParam` to read the bucket's ARN into a custom environment variable.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, Bucket, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const uploads = new Bucket({});

  const processor = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/process.ts'
    }),
    environment: {
      UPLOAD_BUCKET_ARN: "$ResourceParam('uploads', 'arn')"
    },
    connectTo: [uploads]
  });

  return {
    resources: { uploads, processor }
  };
});
```

### Stack outputs

Use `$ResourceParam` in [stack outputs](/configuration/configuration-files) to surface deployment values in the terminal and stack info file. Outputs are useful for sharing endpoints with other teams, CI pipelines, or other stacks.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, HttpApiGateway } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const gateway = new HttpApiGateway({});

  return {
    resources: { gateway },
    stackConfig: {
      outputs: [
        {
          name: 'apiUrl',
          value: "$ResourceParam('gateway', 'url')"
        }
      ]
    }
  };
});
```


Stack outputs can be exported for cross-stack use by setting `export: true`. Exported outputs can be referenced from other stacks using the `$CfStackOutput()` directive.

## Defining resources

Resources live in the `resources` object returned from `defineConfig`. Each key becomes the resource's name: used in `connectTo` environment variable prefixes (e.g., `STP_MY_API_ARN`) and in resource references like `$ResourceParam('myApi', 'url')`.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  LambdaFunction,
  Bucket,
  SqsQueue,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const uploads = new Bucket({});

  const orderQueue = new SqsQueue({});

  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts'
    }),
    connectTo: [uploads, orderQueue]
  });

  return {
    resources: { uploads, orderQueue, api }
  };
});
```


The resource name you choose matters:

- It determines the prefix for injected environment variables (e.g., a resource named `uploads` produces `STP_UPLOADS_NAME`, `STP_UPLOADS_ARN`).
- It's used in `$ResourceParam('uploads', 'arn')` references across your config.
- Changing a resource name affects every reference to it: `$ResourceParam` lookups, `connectTo` listings, and injected environment variable prefixes.

Pick short, descriptive, camelCase names. Resource names must be alphanumeric: letters and numbers only (matching `/^[a-z0-9]+$/i`). Hyphens, underscores, and special characters are not allowed. Stacktape validates this at deploy time and rejects invalid names.

## Connecting resources

Most applications need resources to talk to each other: a Lambda function querying a database, a container writing to a bucket. Stacktape handles this through the `connectTo` property, which automatically:

- Grants the correct IAM permissions (read/write to S3, CRUD on DynamoDB, invoke on Lambda).
- Opens security group rules for VPC-protected resources like relational databases and Redis.
- Injects environment variables with connection details in the `STP_[RESOURCE_NAME]_[PARAM]` format.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, Bucket, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const invoiceBucket = new Bucket({});

  const processOrder = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/process-order.ts'
    }),
    connectTo: [invoiceBucket]
  });

  return {
    resources: { invoiceBucket, processOrder }
  };
});
```


In this example, the `processOrder` function receives `STP_INVOICE_BUCKET_NAME` and `STP_INVOICE_BUCKET_ARN` as environment variables automatically. No manual IAM policies or security group rules needed.

In the TypeScript `defineConfig` style, pass the resource variables directly in the `connectTo` array (`[invoiceBucket]`, not `['invoiceBucket']`). Stacktape resolves these to the underlying resource names automatically. In YAML config, `connectTo` accepts an array of resource name strings. Common workload resource types inject variables such as:

| Resource type | Injected variables |
|---------------|-------------------|
| **Bucket** | `NAME`, `ARN` |
| **DynamoDbTable** | `NAME`, `ARN`, `STREAM_ARN` |
| **RelationalDatabase** | `CONNECTION_STRING`, `JDBC_CONNECTION_STRING`, `HOST`, `PORT` (Aurora also adds `READER_CONNECTION_STRING`, `READER_JDBC_CONNECTION_STRING`, `READER_HOST`) |
| **Redis** | `HOST`, `READER_HOST`, `PORT` |
| **MongoDbAtlasCluster** | `CONNECTION_STRING` |
| **UpstashRedis** | `HOST`, `PORT`, `PASSWORD`, `REST_TOKEN`, `REST_URL`, `REDIS_URL` |
| **SqsQueue** | `ARN`, `NAME`, `URL` |
| **SnsTopic** | `ARN`, `NAME` |
| **EventBus** | `ARN` |
| **Function** | `ARN` |
| **UserAuthPool** | `ID`, `CLIENT_ID`, `ARN` |
| **BatchJob** | `JOB_DEFINITION_ARN`, `STATE_MACHINE_ARN` |
| **PrivateService** | `ADDRESS` |
| **WebService** | `URL` |
| **EmailSender** | `IDENTITY`, `IDENTITY_ARN`, `REGION`, `CONFIGURATION_SET_NAME`, `FEEDBACK_TOPIC_ARN` |
| **KafkaCluster** | `ARN`, `NAME`, `BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS` |

All variable names follow the pattern `STP_[RESOURCE_NAME]_[PARAM]`. For the full per-resource reference, see [Connecting resources](/configuration/connecting-resources).

Use the native [Email sender](/resources/messaging/email-sender) resource for SES. It verifies or reuses the identity, injects the sending values, and scopes permissions to that exact identity and configuration set.

## Resource references with directives

Beyond `connectTo`, you can reference any resource's parameters anywhere in your config using the `$ResourceParam` directive. This is useful for wiring values that `connectTo` doesn't cover: like passing a queue URL to a Step Functions state machine definition or embedding a bucket name in a custom environment variable.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  LambdaFunction,
  SqsQueue,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const orderQueue = new SqsQueue({});

  const worker = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/worker.ts'
    }),
    environment: { QUEUE_URL: "$ResourceParam('orderQueue', 'url')" }
  });

  return {
    resources: { orderQueue, worker }
  };
});
```


The `environment` property accepts an array of `{ name, value }` objects. Use `$ResourceParam` inside the `value` to reference any parameter from another resource.

See [Directives](/configuration/directives) for all built-in directives and [Referenceable parameters](/configuration/referenceable-parameters) for the full list of parameters each resource exposes.

## Permissions for CI

Deploys through the Stacktape Console and its runners use the connected account's role and need nothing more. A CI role limited to its own stacks also needs, for the shared stacks:

- `cloudformation:DescribeStacks`, `CreateStack`, `UpdateStack` and `UpdateTerminationProtection` on `arn:aws:cloudformation:<region>:<account>:stack/stacktape-network*/*`;
- `ssm:PutParameter` on `arn:aws:ssm:<region>:<account>:parameter/stacktape/shared-network/*`, where each deploy records that it uses the shared stacks;
- the EC2 actions the network creates: `ec2:CreateVpc`, `ModifyVpcAttribute`, `CreateSubnet`, `ModifySubnetAttribute`, `CreateRouteTable`, `AssociateRouteTable`, `CreateRoute`, `CreateInternetGateway`, `AttachInternetGateway`, `CreateVpcEndpoint`, `CreateTags` and `ec2:Describe*`;
- for the janitor and the no-access group, which the network gets when it is created or on the next deploy of a stage that uses it: `iam:CreateRole`, `PutRolePolicy`, `GetRole`, `PassRole`, `lambda:CreateFunction`, `lambda:GetFunction`, `lambda:UpdateFunctionCode`, `lambda:UpdateFunctionConfiguration`, `lambda:InvokeFunction`, `logs:CreateLogGroup`, `logs:PutRetentionPolicy`, `scheduler:CreateSchedule`, `scheduler:GetSchedule` and `ec2:CreateSecurityGroup`;
- for the NAT: `ec2:AllocateAddress`, `CreateNetworkInterface`, `CreateSecurityGroup`, `AuthorizeSecurityGroupIngress`, `AuthorizeSecurityGroupEgress`, `CreateLaunchTemplate`, `RunInstances`, `autoscaling:CreateAutoScalingGroup`, `autoscaling:Describe*`, `iam:CreateRole`, `PutRolePolicy`, `AttachRolePolicy`, `PassRole`, `lambda:CreateFunction`, `lambda:GetFunction`, `lambda:InvokeFunction`, `ssm:PutParameter` and `ssm:GetParameters`.

The CI role needs no delete actions: the janitor removes the shared stacks with its own role, which can delete only `stacktape-network` and `stacktape-network-nat`, and whose delete actions on their resources apply only while CloudFormation uses it for those deletes.

If you would rather not grant them, set `stackConfig.vpc.shared: false`; the stage then creates its own VPC as before.

## Production and non-production stages

Stacktape treats production stages differently in a few places: production error groups enter the incident queue, deleting a production stack needs a stronger Console permission, and [security findings](/cli/validate#security-findings-by-stage-purpose) are rated for production. A database open to the internet, for example, is `HIGH` in production and `LOW` in a dev stage.

A stage is production when `stackConfig.stageType` is `production`. Without that setting, the type you chose when you created the stage in the Stacktape Console decides. Otherwise Stacktape decides from the stage name: `prod`, `production`, `prd` and `live`, alone, as a segment (`prod-eu`, `client-a-prod`) or followed by a number (`prod2`), count as production, and names with a rehearsal marker (`pre-prod`, `prod-test`, `staging-live`) do not. `deploy` reads the Console's type; a plain `validate` does not contact the Console and uses the config and the name. Set `stageType` when your production stage has another name, such as `main`:

```typescript
export default defineConfig(({ stage }) => ({
  stackConfig: { stageType: stage === 'main' ? 'production' : 'non-production' },
  resources: {
    // ...
  }
}));
```

## Stage-aware configuration

The `defineConfig` callback receives a `stage` parameter. Use standard TypeScript logic (conditionals, lookup objects, helper functions) to vary your config per stage.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  LambdaFunction,
  RelationalDatabase,
  RdsEnginePostgres,
  $Secret,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(({ stage }) => {
  const isProduction = stage === 'production';

  const database = new RelationalDatabase({
    engine: new RdsEnginePostgres({
      version: '16',
      primaryInstance: {
        instanceSize: isProduction ? 'db.t4g.medium' : 'db.t4g.micro',
        multiAz: isProduction
      }
    }),
    credentials: {
      masterUserPassword: $Secret('db-password-' + stage)
    }
  });

  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts'
    }),
    memory: isProduction ? 1024 : 256,
    timeout: isProduction ? 30 : 10,
    environment: {
      LOG_LEVEL: isProduction ? 'warn' : 'debug'
    },
    connectTo: [database]
  });

  return { resources: { database, api } };
});
```


In this example, the production stage gets a larger database instance with multi-AZ redundancy, more Lambda memory, and
tighter log levels: while development stages use minimal resources to keep costs low. The `$Secret` directive
can reference a different secret name per stage, such as `db-password-dev` and `db-password-production`, when you
create separate secrets for those stages. See [Secrets](/configuration/secrets) for how to create and manage secrets.

### Multi-tier environments

Most production teams use three tiers: `dev` for daily work, `staging` for pre-release validation, and `production` for
live traffic. Use a lookup object to map each stage to its resource configuration.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  WebService,
  RelationalDatabase,
  RdsEnginePostgres,
  $Secret,
  JsBundleImagePackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(({ stage }) => {
  const tiers: Record<
    string,
    { instanceSize: string; minInstances: number; maxInstances: number; multiAz: boolean }
  > = {
    dev: { instanceSize: 'db.t4g.micro', minInstances: 1, maxInstances: 1, multiAz: false },
    staging: { instanceSize: 'db.t4g.small', minInstances: 1, maxInstances: 3, multiAz: false },
    production: { instanceSize: 'db.t4g.medium', minInstances: 2, maxInstances: 10, multiAz: true }
  };
  const tier = tiers[stage] || tiers.dev;

  const database = new RelationalDatabase({
    engine: new RdsEnginePostgres({
      version: '16',
      primaryInstance: {
        instanceSize: tier.instanceSize,
        multiAz: tier.multiAz
      }
    }),
    credentials: {
      masterUserPassword: $Secret('db-password-' + stage)
    }
  });

  const app = new WebService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/app.ts' }),
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 },
    scaling: { minInstances: tier.minInstances, maxInstances: tier.maxInstances },
    connectTo: [database]
  });

  return { resources: { database, app } };
});
```


The fallback `tiers[stage] || tiers.dev` means any unrecognized stage name (like `feat-xyz`) inherits the `dev`
tier. This keeps feature branch stages cheap by default.

## Conditional resources

Since `defineConfig` is plain TypeScript, you can conditionally include or exclude resources per stage. This is useful
for skipping expensive resources in ephemeral stages, or adding production-only safeguards like a
[web application firewall](/resources/security/web-application-firewall).


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  LambdaFunction,
  RelationalDatabase,
  RdsEnginePostgres,
  WebAppFirewall,
  $Secret,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(({ stage }) => {
  const resources: Record<string, any> = {};

  resources.api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/api.ts' }),
    memory: 512
  });

  if (!stage.startsWith('pr-')) {
    resources.database = new RelationalDatabase({
      engine: new RdsEnginePostgres({
        version: '16',
        primaryInstance: { instanceSize: 'db.t4g.micro' }
      }),
      credentials: {
        masterUserPassword: $Secret('db-password-' + stage)
      }
    });
  }

  if (stage === 'production') {
    resources.firewall = new WebAppFirewall({ scope: 'regional' });
  }

  return { resources };
});
```


In this example, PR preview stages skip the database entirely (the API could use an external or shared database
instead), and only production gets a WAF. This pattern keeps preview stages fast to deploy and cheap to run.

## Stage-specific secrets

Store separate Stacktape secrets for each stage. Use
[`stacktape secret set`](/cli/secret-set) to create them, and the
[`$Secret` directive](/configuration/directives) to reference them in config.

Create separate secrets for each stage (for example, `db-password-dev` and `db-password-production`) using [`stacktape secret set`](/cli/secret-set):

```bash
stacktape secret set --region eu-west-1
```

Run the command once per secret. See the [CLI reference](/cli/secret-set) for available options.

Then reference the stage-specific secret in your config using the `stage` parameter:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, RelationalDatabase, RdsEnginePostgres, $Secret } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(({ stage }) => {
  const database = new RelationalDatabase({
    engine: new RdsEnginePostgres({
      version: '16',
      primaryInstance: { instanceSize: 'db.t4g.micro' }
    }),
    credentials: {
      masterUserPassword: $Secret('db-password-' + stage)
    }
  });

  return { resources: { database } };
});
```


With `stage === 'dev'`, the config passes `db-password-dev` to `$Secret`; with `stage === 'production'`, it passes `db-password-production`. Never share production secrets with development stages.
See [Secrets](/configuration/secrets) for the full guide.

## Stage-specific domains

Assign different [custom domains](/resources/networking/custom-domains) per stage to give each environment a clean URL.
Production uses the apex domain; other stages use subdomains.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, HttpApiGateway } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(({ stage }) => {
  const gateway = new HttpApiGateway({
    customDomains:
      stage === 'production'
        ? [{ domainName: 'api.example.com' }]
        : [{ domainName: stage + '.api.example.com' }]
  });

  return { resources: { gateway } };
});
```


This produces:

| Stage | Domain |
|---|---|
| `production` | `api.example.com` |
| `staging` | `staging.api.example.com` |
| `dev` | `dev.api.example.com` |

Custom domains are managed separately. See [custom domains](/resources/networking/custom-domains) and [`stacktape domain add`](/cli/domain-add) for setup.

## Project Structure

- `my-platform`
  - `shared-infra`
    - `stacktape.ts`: The database; exports its host and port
  - `api`
    - `stacktape.ts`: Imports the exports


The shared project exports the host and port as stack outputs:


Example (shared-infra/stacktape.ts):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, RelationalDatabase, RdsEnginePostgres, $Secret, $ResourceParam } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig({ projectName: 'shared-infra' }, ({ stage }) => {
  const database = new RelationalDatabase({
    engine: new RdsEnginePostgres({
      version: '16',
      primaryInstance: { instanceSize: 'db.t4g.micro' }
    }),
    credentials: {
      masterUserPassword: $Secret(`shared-db-password-${stage}`)
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { database },
    stackConfig: {
      outputs: [
        { name: 'DbHost', value: $ResourceParam('database', 'host'), export: true },
        { name: 'DbPort', value: $ResourceParam('database', 'port'), export: true }
      ]
    }
  };
});
```


Deploy it to the two stages the others use:

```bash
stacktape deploy --stage dev --region eu-west-1
```

```bash
stacktape deploy --stage production --region eu-west-1
```

Every stage of the `api` project then imports from one of those two stacks. `pr-42`, `staging` and `dev` all use `shared-infra-dev`:


Example (api/stacktape.ts):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebService, JsBundleImagePackaging, $CfStackOutput, $Secret } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(({ stage }) => {
  const sharedStage = stage === 'production' ? 'production' : 'dev';
  const sharedStack = `shared-infra-${sharedStage}`;

  const api = new WebService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/server.ts' }),
    resources: { cpu: 0.25, memory: 512 },
    environment: {
      DB_HOST: $CfStackOutput(sharedStack, 'DbHost'),
      DB_PORT: $CfStackOutput(sharedStack, 'DbPort')
    },
    secrets: [{ name: 'DB_PASSWORD', valueFrom: $Secret(`shared-db-password-${sharedStage}`) }]
  });

  return { resources: { api } };
});
```


- Export only values that are not secret. The password stays in Secrets Manager and reaches the container through `secrets`.
- `$CfStackOutput` makes CloudFormation import the export. While any stack imports it, the shared stack cannot remove that output or be deleted; delete or update the importing stacks first. `$StackOutput` reads the value once at deploy time and creates no such link.
- `connectTo` does not work across stacks, so it grants nothing here. This example works because the database's default `accessibilityMode` is `internet`, protected by its password. For a database reachable only inside a VPC, both projects must run in a network that can reach it; see [Connecting resources](/configuration/connecting-resources#can-i-connect-to-resources-in-a-different-stack).

### Shared constants

Extract repeated values into `const` declarations. The TypeScript compiler ensures type safety, and your editor provides autocomplete. Both Lambda functions below use the same `timeout` and `APP_PORT` values: extracting these into `const timeout = 30` and `const appPort = '3000'` at the top of the callback means each value is updated in one place.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  LambdaFunction,
  HttpApiGateway,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const listUsers = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/list-users.ts' }),
    timeout: 30,
    environment: { APP_PORT: '3000' }
  });

  const createUser = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/create-user.ts' }),
    timeout: 30,
    environment: { APP_PORT: '3000' }
  });

  const gateway = new HttpApiGateway({});

  return { resources: { listUsers, createUser, gateway } };
});
```


When you spot duplicated values like these, extract them into `const` declarations at the top of the callback. This applies to any repeated value: memory sizes, environment variable blocks, domain prefixes, or timeout durations.

### Stage-based conditional logic

The `stage` parameter from `defineConfig` lets you branch configuration per [stage](/configuration/stages-and-environments) without external tooling or environment-variable hacks. Destructure `stage` from the callback parameter and use it in conditional expressions: for example, `memory: stage === 'production' ? 2048 : 512` gives production more RAM while other stages use a smaller allocation.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/api.ts' }),
    memory: 2048
  });

  return { resources: { api } };
});
```


Use the same conditional pattern for any stage-dependent setting: backup retention, instance counts, alarm thresholds, or whether to provision optional resources at all.

### Helper functions

When multiple resources share the same shape, extract a factory function. This is especially useful for [Lambda functions](/resources/compute/lambda-function) with repeated packaging and event configuration. Define the factory inside `defineConfig` and call it for each endpoint.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  LambdaFunction,
  HttpApiGateway,
  HttpApiIntegration,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const createApiFunction = (
    entryfile: string,
    method: 'GET' | 'POST' | 'PUT' | 'DELETE',
    path: string
  ) =>
    new LambdaFunction({
      packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: entryfile }),
      memory: 512,
      timeout: 30,
      events: [new HttpApiIntegration({ httpApiGatewayName: 'gateway', method, path })]
    });

  const listUsers = createApiFunction('./src/list-users.ts', 'GET', '/users');
  const createUser = createApiFunction('./src/create-user.ts', 'POST', '/users');
  const gateway = new HttpApiGateway({});

  return { resources: { listUsers, createUser, gateway } };
});
```


The `memory` property sets the MB of RAM allocated to each Lambda function (512 MB here), and `timeout` sets the maximum execution time in seconds (30 s). Both Lambda functions share these values and the gateway integration shape: you define them once.

### Shared modules across projects

For organizations with multiple Stacktape projects that share conventions (tagging, naming, scaling defaults), create a shared TypeScript module: either a local file or a published package:

```typescript
// shared/defaults.ts
export const defaultTags = { team: 'platform', managedBy: 'stacktape' };
export const defaultTimeout = 30;
export const defaultMemory = 512;
```

Import these constants into each project's `stacktape.ts`. This keeps org-wide conventions in sync without copy-paste, and TypeScript catches type errors at build time if the shape of your shared config changes.

## The variables property

The top-level `variables` property defines named values you can reference with the `$Var()` [directive](/configuration/directives). It is part of the root configuration alongside `resources`, `hooks`, `scripts`, and `directives`, and works in both YAML and TypeScript configs.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/api.ts' })
  });

  return {
    variables: {
      appPort: 3000,
      apiDomain: 'api.example.com'
    },
    resources: { api }
  };
});
```


Variables can hold any value: strings, numbers, booleans, objects, or arrays. Reference a variable with `$Var().variableName` anywhere in the config: for example, inside a resource's `environment` block:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/api.ts' }),
    environment: {
      APP_PORT: '$Var().appPort',
      API_DOMAIN: '$Var().apiDomain'
    }
  });

  return {
    variables: {
      appPort: '3000',
      apiDomain: 'api.example.com'
    },
    resources: { api }
  };
});
```

## Custom directives

When you need values computed dynamically at deploy time (fetching from external APIs, reading infrastructure state, or running conditional logic) define a [custom directive](/configuration/directives). Custom directives are registered in the `directives` array, each pointing to a JS, TypeScript, or Python handler file.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/api.ts' })
  });

  return {
    directives: [{ name: 'FetchConfig', filePath: './directives/fetch-config.ts' }],
    resources: { api }
  };
});
```


The handler file exports a default function that receives any parameters passed in the directive call and returns the computed value. Once registered, use `$FetchConfig()` anywhere in the config. Custom directives compute config values at deploy time before the CloudFormation template is generated.

For most TypeScript configs, you can replace custom directives with regular imports and logic in your `stacktape.ts`: custom directives are most relevant for YAML workflows or when you need to separate computation into dedicated handler files.

For commands you want to reuse, such as a seed script or a frontend build, see [Hooks and scripts](/configuration/hooks-and-scripts).

Extract a value when it appears in several places that must change together. A config that reads top to bottom is easier to review than one that sends you through several helper files.

## Protect a production stage

Termination protection blocks every delete of the stack, also from the AWS console. Turn it on for stages with production data:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts'
    })
  });

  return {
    resources: { api },
    deploymentConfig: {
      terminationProtection: true
    }
  };
});
```


To delete a protected stage later, deploy it with `terminationProtection: false`, then run `stacktape delete`. Database `deletionProtection` works the same way: deploy with it off first. Stacktape never turns either protection off during a delete. For organization-wide rules, use [guardrails](/guardrails/overview).

## Turn it on for a function


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const checkNewVersion = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/check-new-version.ts'
    }),
    timeout: 60
  });

  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts'
    }),
    deployment: {
      strategy: 'Canary10Percent5Minutes',
      beforeAllowTrafficFunction: 'checkNewVersion'
    }
  });

  return { resources: { checkNewVersion, api } };
});
```


On each deploy that changes `api`, CodeDeploy first runs `checkNewVersion`. If it reports success, 10% of invocations go to the new version, and after 5 minutes the rest. If it reports a failure, no traffic moves and the deploy fails.

A function with `deployment` always deploys through CloudFormation, so code changes to it are not [fast deploys](/deployment-and-lifecycle/deploying-stacks#fast-deployments). The deploy takes at least as long as the strategy.

## Turn it on for a web service

A web service needs `loadBalancing.type: 'application-load-balancer'`. The default HTTP API Gateway and a network load balancer cannot split traffic.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new WebService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/server.ts'
    }),
    resources: { cpu: 0.25, memory: 512 },
    loadBalancing: {
      type: 'application-load-balancer'
    },
    deployment: {
      strategy: 'Canary10Percent5Minutes'
    }
  });

  return { resources: { api } };
});
```


An application load balancer costs about $16 a month plus usage in us-east-1, while an HTTP API Gateway is billed only per request. During the shift, the old and new containers run side by side, so you pay for both for the length of the strategy. Container services with `deployment` use blue/green deployment, which also rules out fast deploys for them.

`beforeAllowTrafficFunction` and `afterTrafficShiftFunction` work for services too. Before production traffic moves, CodeDeploy sends test traffic to the new containers on the load balancer's test listener, port 8080 by default (change it with `testListenerPort`). Your test function can call the load balancer on that port to check the new version.

### Write a test function

The function receives a deployment ID and a hook execution ID, runs your checks and reports the result to CodeDeploy. Stacktape gives it permission to report. Set a `timeout` long enough for your checks.

```ts
import { CodeDeployClient, PutLifecycleEventHookExecutionStatusCommand } from '@aws-sdk/client-codedeploy';

const codeDeploy = new CodeDeployClient({});

export const handler = async (event: { DeploymentId: string; LifecycleEventHookExecutionId: string }) => {
  let status: 'Succeeded' | 'Failed' = 'Succeeded';
  try {
    await runChecks(); // your checks: call the new version, check a dependency
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(error);
    status = 'Failed';
  }
  await codeDeploy.send(
    new PutLifecycleEventHookExecutionStatusCommand({
      deploymentId: event.DeploymentId,
      lifecycleEventHookExecutionId: event.LifecycleEventHookExecutionId,
      status
    })
  );
};
```

AWS describes the event and the response in [AppSpec hooks for Lambda deployments](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/codedeploy/latest/userguide/reference-appspec-file-structure-hooks.html).

## Vary the config by region

One config usually works in every region. When something must differ, such as instance sizes or a feature that only one region has, read `region` in the `defineConfig` callback:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(({ region, stage }) => {
  const isMainRegion = region === 'eu-west-1';

  const api = new WebService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/server.ts'
    }),
    resources: {
      cpu: stage === 'production' && isMainRegion ? 1 : 0.5,
      memory: stage === 'production' && isMainRegion ? 2048 : 1024
    }
  });

  return { resources: { api } };
});
```


The other values the callback receives are listed in [Configuration files](/configuration/configuration-files#the-defineconfig-callback). For a single value, the [`$Region()` directive](/configuration/directives) is often enough.

## Automate the deploys

Run one `stacktape deploy` per region, for example as a matrix job in your pipeline; see [Custom CI/CD](/ci-cd-and-gitops/custom-ci-cd). Each job is independent: a failed deploy in one region does not affect the others, and the regions can briefly run different versions.

To keep deploys out of other regions, add a `region-restriction` [guardrail](/guardrails/deployment#region-restriction):


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/api.ts'
    })
  });

  return {
    guardrails: [
      {
        type: 'region-restriction',
        properties: {
          allowedRegions: ['eu-west-1', 'eu-central-1', 'us-east-1']
        }
      }
    ],
    resources: { api }
  };
});
```


A deploy to another region fails before anything is built or uploaded.

### How many releases you can return to

`deploymentConfig.previousVersionsToKeep` sets how many releases keep their artifacts. Older releases may still appear in the history, but their artifacts are gone and the rollback refuses them. More releases means more S3 and ECR storage.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts'
    })
  });

  return {
    resources: { api },
    deploymentConfig: {
      previousVersionsToKeep: 20
    }
  };
});
```

## Preview pull requests

A preview is a stage for one pull request, such as `pr-42`, that is deleted when the pull request closes. You can run
previews in two ways:

- **From your own CI, without a Stacktape account.** One workflow deploys the stage and deletes it on close. See
  [Pull request previews](/ci-cd-and-gitops/custom-ci-cd#pull-request-previews).
- **With the Console.** [Git deployments](/ci-cd-and-gitops/gitops-with-console) deploy each pull request and can
  delete its stage when it closes.

A database that `stacktape init` added has deletion protection on every stage, so deleting a preview stops before it
removes anything, and the preview keeps running and costing money. Protect only production. In `stacktape.ts`:

```ts
export default defineConfig(({ stage }) => {
  const database = new RelationalDatabase({
    // ...the rest of the database
    deletionProtection: stage === 'production'
  });
  // ...
});
```

A YAML config can't set a value per stage. Switch it to TypeScript, or delete each preview by hand as described in
[Delete the stage](#delete-the-stage).

After your first preview, close the pull request and check that the stage is gone: the Console's stage list, or
`stacktape stack list --region <region>`.

## Delete everything instead

If you don't need the app any more, run [`stacktape delete`](/cli/delete) for each stage while you still have the CLI.
It empties and removes the deployment bucket and the image repository too. It keeps:

- secrets, hosted zones and ACM certificates;
- the shared network, which removes itself a day later;
- CDN edge functions in `us-east-1`, which AWS lets you delete a few hours later;
- the uptime probes and the registered resource types;
- the IAM role `stacktape-api-gateway-cloudwatch-logs` and the `stacktape-shared-email-identity-*` stacks, if a stack
  used them.

Delete those by hand when nothing uses them.

## Alarms

Stacktape alarms monitor CloudWatch metrics and notify your team when thresholds are breached. Define alarms on individual resources in your stack config and deploy to apply them. They are CloudWatch alarms in your AWS account. The [Stacktape Console](/stacktape-console/console-overview) also shows their state and history.

Alarm triggers cover the most critical failure modes: Lambda error rate and duration, database CPU/memory/storage/connections/latency, HTTP API Gateway error rate and latency, Application Load Balancer error rate and unhealthy targets, and SQS received-message count and not-empty alarms. Each alarm supports configurable evaluation windows — period length, how many periods to evaluate, and how many must breach before firing.

Without `notificationChannels`, alarms change state in CloudWatch but nobody gets paged. Always route alarms to at least one notification channel. Stacktape supports five channel types: Slack, Microsoft Teams, Discord, email, and custom webhooks.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/api.ts'
    }),
    memory: 1024,
    timeout: 30,
    alarms: [
      {
        trigger: {
          type: 'lambda-error-rate',
          properties: { thresholdPercent: 5 }
        },
        evaluation: {
          period: 60,
          evaluationPeriods: 3,
          breachedPeriods: 2
        },
        notificationChannels: [
          {
            type: 'email',
            properties: {
              sender: 'alerts@example.com',
              recipient: 'oncall@example.com'
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  });

  return { resources: { api } };
});
```


This alarm fires when more than 5% of invocations fail in at least 2 out of 3 consecutive 60-second windows — filtering out isolated spikes while catching sustained problems. The `evaluation` block controls this: `period` sets each window to 60 seconds, `evaluationPeriods: 3` looks at the last three windows, and `breachedPeriods: 2` requires at least two to breach before firing. The email notification target ensures your on-call team is alerted immediately.

For production, most teams use Slack (`type: 'slack'` with `conversationId` and `accessToken`) or a custom webhook. See [Alarms](/observability/alarms) for all trigger types, notification channel configuration, and migration of legacy Console rules.

## Custom domains

Stacktape [web services](/resources/compute/web-service) support custom domains through the `customDomains` property. When the domain's DNS is in Route 53 in your AWS account, Stacktape creates the DNS records and the TLS certificate for you. The example below shows the most common case, a web service API. Other public resources are covered in the [Custom domains](/resources/networking/custom-domains) guide.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new WebService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/server.ts'
    }),
    resources: { cpu: 0.25, memory: 512 },
    environment: { NODE_ENV: 'production' },
    customDomains: [{ domainName: 'api.example.com' }]
  });

  return { resources: { api } };
});
```


There are two ways to set up the domain:

- **DNS in Route 53.** Run [`stacktape domain add`](/cli/domain-add) for a domain you already own. It checks the domain, can move its DNS to a Route 53 hosted zone in your account and requests the TLS certificates. After that, `customDomains` is all a resource needs.
- **DNS at your current provider**, such as Cloudflare. Request a certificate in AWS Certificate Manager, set `customCertificateArn` and `disableDnsRecordCreation: true`, deploy, and add a CNAME record at your provider. See [External DNS](/resources/networking/custom-domains#external-dns).

Custom domains add no Stacktape cost. In AWS, a Route 53 hosted zone and its DNS queries cost a little each month, and the certificates are free.

### Lambda function example


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/api.ts'
    }),
    deployment: {
      strategy: 'Canary10Percent5Minutes'
    }
  });

  return { resources: { api } };
});
```


`Canary10Percent5Minutes` sends 10% of traffic to the new version for 5 minutes before shifting the rest. This is a good starting point for most production APIs — it validates with real traffic on a small slice before committing. If your service handles high traffic and you want more gradual rollout, consider `Linear10PercentEvery3Minutes` for a 30-minute complete rollout.

### Web service example


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new WebService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/server.ts'
    }),
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 },
    loadBalancing: { type: 'application-load-balancer' },
    deployment: {
      strategy: 'Canary10Percent5Minutes'
    }
  });

  return { resources: { api } };
});
```


Container gradual deployments require `loadBalancing` set to `application-load-balancer` — the default `http-api-gateway` does not support traffic shifting. The ALB costs about $16 a month in us-east-1 plus usage, but enables gradual rollouts, WebSocket support, and top-level firewalls. See [Gradual deployments](/deployment-and-lifecycle/gradual-deployments) for strategy comparisons.

### Project name

The root `serviceName` property is now `projectName`. Keep the same value, because the project name is part of the stack name: a different name deploys a new stack next to the old one.

Before:

```yaml
serviceName: shop
resources:
  # ...
```

After, in YAML, put `projectName` (and optionally `region`) at the top of the file. In TypeScript, pass them as the first argument of `defineConfig`, as string literals; returning them from the config function is rejected:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Bucket } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig({ projectName: 'shop', region: 'eu-west-1' }, () => {
  const uploads = new Bucket({});

  return {
    resources: { uploads }
  };
});
```


Example (YAML):

```yaml
projectName: shop
region: eu-west-1
resources:
  uploads:
    type: bucket
```


Stacktape reads these values without running the configuration, so they must be plain strings, not computed values. To deploy the same configuration as another project, pass `--project <name>` to the command. A project that declares `projectName` in `stacktape.ts` needs the v4 `stacktape` package: with an older package, the CLI stops with `CONFIG_PROJECT_SETTINGS_UNSUPPORTED_RUNTIME`.

### TypeScript configurations

Besides the renamed [packaging classes](#packaging) and `RedisCluster`, three things change in `stacktape.ts`:

- **Constructors take only the properties.** v3 also accepted a name as the first argument, `new Bucket('uploads', { ... })`. In v4, write `new Bucket({ ... })`; the resource's name is its key in the returned `resources` object. Keep the keys you had: a different key is a different resource, which v4 would create while deleting the old one.
- **`.resourceName` is gone.** Pass the resource object itself where a property takes a resource, for example `connectTo: [database]`.
- **Resource parameters cannot be turned into strings.** In v3, a parameter inside a template string, such as `${database.connectionString}`, produced a directive string. In v4, converting a parameter reference to a string throws an error while the configuration is evaluated. Pass the reference as the whole value, or use `$CfFormat` to build a string:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging, RelationalDatabase, RdsEnginePostgres, $Secret, $CfFormat, $ResourceParam } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const database = new RelationalDatabase({
    engine: new RdsEnginePostgres({ version: '16', primaryInstance: { instanceSize: 'db.t4g.micro' } }),
    credentials: { masterUserPassword: $Secret('database.password') }
  });
  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/api.ts' }),
    connectTo: [database],
    environment: {
      // In v3 this could be a template string with database.connectionString inside; v4 throws
      DATABASE_URL: database.connectionString,
      DATABASE_HOST_LABEL: $CfFormat('db-{}', $ResourceParam('database', 'host'))
    }
  });

  return { resources: { database, api } };
});
```


`defineConfig(() => ({ ... }))` still works. Tools that call the exported config function directly get a compiled object (`{ config, transforms, finalTransform }`) instead of the plain configuration.

## Starting dev mode

Run [`stacktape dev`](/cli/dev) to start dev mode. Lambda functions appear as selectable resources in the interactive picker, or you can specify them directly with CLI flags.

Run all dev-compatible resources in your config:

```bash
stacktape dev --stage dev --resources all
```

Run only specific Lambda functions by name (comma-separated):

```bash
stacktape dev --stage dev --resources myApi,myWorker
```

Run everything except specific resources:

```bash
stacktape dev --stage dev --skip-resources myWorker
```

Without `--resources` or `--skip-resources`, Stacktape displays an interactive multi-select picker listing all dev-compatible resources. All resources are pre-selected by default: deselect any you don't need and confirm to start.


> **Info:** If you specify a resource name that doesn't exist in your config, Stacktape suggests similar names using fuzzy matching. The `--resources` and `--skip-resources` flags accept comma-separated values.


Here is a minimal config with a [Lambda function](/resources/compute/lambda-function). This function is dev-compatible and will appear in the resource picker when you run `stacktape dev`:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts'
    }),
    memory: 512,
    timeout: 30
  });

  return {
    resources: { api }
  };
});
```


The `memory` property sets the function's memory allocation in MB: AWS Lambda allocates proportional CPU. The `timeout` property sets the maximum execution time in seconds. Both are tunable; see [Lambda functions](/resources/compute/lambda-function) for supported ranges and defaults.

### Structured logging

Lambda functions run on AWS during dev mode, so the primary debugging approach is structured logging rather than local debugger attachment. The tradeoff is high-fidelity testing against the real Lambda runtime. Structured logging is the most effective debugging approach: add JSON log statements at key points in your function. The dev TUI shows them during the session, and you can query them later with [`stacktape logs`](/cli/logs).

```typescript
export default async (event: any) => {
  console.info(JSON.stringify({
    action: 'request_received',
    path: event.path,
    method: event.httpMethod
  }));

  try {
    const result = await processRequest(event);
    console.info(JSON.stringify({
      action: 'request_processed',
      statusCode: result.statusCode
    }));
    return result;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(JSON.stringify({
      action: 'request_failed',
      error: error.message,
      stack: error.stack
    }));
    throw error;
  }
};
```

Use descriptive `action` fields so you can later filter with `--filter "request_failed"` or query with `--query "fields @timestamp, @message | filter @message like /request_failed/"`.

### PostgreSQL

In dev mode, Stacktape runs a local PostgreSQL container using the configured engine version as the Docker image tag. The container uses the configured credentials and the engine `dbName` when set.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, RelationalDatabase, RdsEnginePostgres } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const mainDb = new RelationalDatabase({
    credentials: {
      masterUserPassword: "$Secret('db', 'password')"
    },
    engine: new RdsEnginePostgres({
      version: '16',
      primaryInstance: {
        instanceSize: 'db.t4g.micro'
      }
    })
  });

  return {
    resources: { mainDb }
  };
});
```


In dev mode, this starts a PostgreSQL container using image tag `16`. The `instanceSize` only applies to the deployed AWS resource: it is ignored locally. Referenceable parameters available for the database include `host`, `port`, `dbName`, `connectionString`, and `jdbcConnectionString`, accessible via `$ResourceParam()`. When the database is listed in a workload's `connectTo`, the documented environment variables are injected following the pattern described in [Connecting resources](/configuration/connecting-resources).

### MySQL and MariaDB

In dev mode, Stacktape runs a local MySQL container for the `mysql` engine and a MariaDB container for the `mariadb` engine, using the configured engine version as the Docker image tag.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, RelationalDatabase, RdsEngineMysql } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const appDb = new RelationalDatabase({
    credentials: {
      masterUserPassword: "$Secret('db', 'password')"
    },
    engine: new RdsEngineMysql({
      version: '8.4',
      primaryInstance: {
        instanceSize: 'db.t4g.micro'
      }
    })
  });

  return {
    resources: { appDb }
  };
});
```


Referenceable parameters available for MySQL and MariaDB include `host`, `port`, `dbName`, `connectionString`, and `jdbcConnectionString`.

## Redis

In dev mode, Stacktape runs a local Redis container using the configured `engineVersion` as the Docker image tag. Redis runs as a single node locally: cluster-mode features such as hash slots and multi-node failover are not available.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Redis } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const cache = new Redis({
    defaultUserPassword: "$Secret('redis', 'password')",
    engine: { type: 'valkey-serverless' }
  });

  return {
    resources: { cache }
  };
});
```


Use the Redis connection information exposed by dev mode and by the resource's [referenceable parameters](/configuration/referenceable-parameters).

## DynamoDB

Stacktape uses the `amazon/dynamodb-local` image with the `-sharedDb` flag, meaning all tables share a single underlying database file. Tables are automatically created from your config if a `primaryKey` is defined: you do not need to create them manually.

Container readiness is checked by making an HTTP `ListTables` request, polling every 300 ms with a 30-second timeout.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, DynamoDbTable } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const tasks = new DynamoDbTable({
    primaryKey: {
      partitionKey: { name: 'pk', type: 'string' },
      sortKey: { name: 'sk', type: 'string' }
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { tasks }
  };
});
```


Referenceable parameters available for the local table include `endpoint` (e.g. `http://localhost:<port>`), `name` (the table name), and `arn` (a synthetic local ARN in the format `arn:aws:dynamodb:local:000000000000:table/<name>`). Your application code should use the `endpoint` parameter to configure the AWS SDK DynamoDB client for local access.


> **Warning:** The `amazon/dynamodb-local` image does not include DynamoDB Streams, Global Tables, TTL, or on-demand backups. Use `dev.remote: true` if your application depends on these features.

## Using remote databases

By default, supported databases run locally. A remote database is deployed in AWS as part of the dev stack instead: the dev stage gets its own real database (an RDS instance, a DynamoDB table, a Redis cluster), created on the first start and reused afterwards. It costs what that AWS resource costs until you delete the dev stage, and a new RDS instance takes several minutes to create. It starts empty; it is not the database of another stage such as `production`.

Use the `--remote-resources` flag for one session, or `dev.remote: true` on the resource to make it permanent.

To switch a single resource to remote at runtime:

```bash
stacktape dev --remote-resources mainDb
```

To switch multiple resources, separate names with commas:

```bash
stacktape dev --remote-resources mainDb,cache
```

Alternatively, set `dev.remote: true` on the resource in your config for a persistent setting:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, RelationalDatabase, RdsEnginePostgres } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const mainDb = new RelationalDatabase({
    credentials: {
      masterUserPassword: "$Secret('db', 'password')"
    },
    engine: new RdsEnginePostgres({
      version: '16',
      primaryInstance: {
        instanceSize: 'db.t4g.micro'
      }
    }),
    dev: {
      remote: true
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { mainDb }
  };
});
```

## Use spot instances for batch jobs

Batch jobs support `useSpotInstances`, which uses discounted spare AWS capacity and can save up to 90%, but jobs can be interrupted. If a Spot interruption happens, your container gets a `SIGTERM` and 120 seconds to shut down gracefully.

Spot instances are a good fit for jobs that can safely restart — data imports, image processing, ML training with checkpoints. Combine with `retryConfig` so interrupted jobs automatically retry, and set `timeout` so a stuck job doesn't bill indefinitely:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, BatchJob, DockerfilePackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const processor = new BatchJob({
    container: {
      packaging: new DockerfilePackaging({
        buildContextPath: '.',
        dockerfilePath: './Dockerfile'
      })
    },
    resources: { cpu: 4, memory: 7680 },
    useSpotInstances: true,
    timeout: 3600,
    retryConfig: {
      attempts: 3,
      retryIntervalSeconds: 30,
      retryIntervalMultiplier: 2
    }
  });

  return { resources: { processor } };
});
```


`resources.cpu` sets the vCPU count and `resources.memory` sets memory in MB. The value `7680` MB is intentionally below 8 GB because AWS reserves memory for system processes — requesting exactly 8,192 MB may provision a larger instance than needed.

In this example, `attempts: 3` means three runs in total: the first run and up to two retries. The default is `1`, no retry. `retryIntervalSeconds: 30` and `retryIntervalMultiplier: 2` wait 30 seconds before the second run and 60 seconds before the third. `timeout: 3600` stops a run after one hour, so a stuck job doesn't keep billing. See [Batch job](/resources/compute/batch-job) for the retry options.

Do not use spot instances for jobs with non-idempotent side effects (sending emails, charging payments) or strict completion deadlines.

## Right-size container resources

For Fargate-backed container workloads, AWS pricing is tied to requested CPU and memory — over-provisioning wastes money every hour. Check CPU and memory utilization in the Stacktape Console [metrics view](/observability/metrics), with [`stacktape metrics`](/cli/metrics) (`CPUUtilization`, `MemoryUtilization`) or in CloudWatch. If utilization is consistently below 30%, you're paying for capacity you don't use.

Start at the smallest CPU/memory combination that keeps your application responsive and scale up only when metrics justify it. See the [web service](/resources/compute/web-service), [private service](/resources/compute/private-service), and [worker service](/resources/compute/worker-service) pages for the supported CPU and memory values on each resource.

For services with variable load, auto-scaling adds instances under pressure rather than over-provisioning a single large instance. See the [web service](/resources/compute/web-service) page for all scaling options — here's a typical setup:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new WebService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/server.ts'
    }),
    resources: {
      cpu: 0.5,
      memory: 1024
    },
    scaling: {
      minInstances: 1,
      maxInstances: 4,
      scalingPolicy: {
        keepAvgCpuUtilizationUnder: 70
      }
    }
  });

  return { resources: { api } };
});
```


In this example, `cpu: 0.5` requests half a vCPU and `memory: 1024` requests 1,024 MB — the smallest values that keep this service responsive. `minInstances: 1` is the always-on floor (you always pay for at least one instance), while `maxInstances: 4` caps how many instances run, and with it this service's compute cost. It doesn't limit the cost of anything else, such as the load balancer, data transfer or the database. `keepAvgCpuUtilizationUnder: 70` tells the auto-scaler to add instances when average CPU exceeds 70% — adjust this threshold based on observed metrics. See the [web service](/resources/compute/web-service) page for all available scaling options and supported CPU/memory values.

## Reduce log retention

CloudWatch log storage accumulates cost over time. [Batch job](/resources/compute/batch-job) logs default to 90 days of retention. For other resources, check the logging section on that resource's page before changing retention. You can view logs with [`stacktape logs`](/cli/logs).

For development and staging stages, 7–14 days of log retention is usually enough. For production, 30–60 days covers most debugging scenarios. For batch jobs, set `retentionDays` in the `logging` block — other resources may expose their own logging settings, so check each resource's page for details:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, BatchJob, DockerfilePackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const processor = new BatchJob({
    container: {
      packaging: new DockerfilePackaging({
        buildContextPath: '.',
        dockerfilePath: './Dockerfile'
      })
    },
    resources: { cpu: 1, memory: 1920 },
    logging: {
      retentionDays: 14
    }
  });

  return { resources: { processor } };
});
```


If you need long-term log storage for compliance, consider [forwarding logs](/observability/log-forwarding) to a cheaper external service rather than keeping them in CloudWatch indefinitely.

### Lambda function alarms

A [Lambda function](/resources/compute/lambda-function) accepts `lambda-error-rate` and `lambda-duration` triggers. The following example fires when more than 5% of invocations fail:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/api.ts'
    }),
    alarms: [
      {
        trigger: {
          type: 'lambda-error-rate',
          properties: {
            thresholdPercent: 5
          }
        }
      }
    ]
  });

  return { resources: { api } };
});
```

### Database alarms

A [relational database](/resources/databases/relational-database) supports six trigger types covering CPU, storage, memory, connections, and I/O latency. The following example combines a CPU alarm with a free-storage alarm and tunes the evaluation window to avoid firing on brief spikes:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, RelationalDatabase, RdsEnginePostgres } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const mainDb = new RelationalDatabase({
    credentials: {
      masterUserPassword: '$Secret(db-password)'
    },
    engine: new RdsEnginePostgres({
      version: '16',
      primaryInstance: { instanceSize: 'db.t4g.micro' }
    }),
    alarms: [
      {
        trigger: {
          type: 'database-cpu-utilization',
          properties: { thresholdPercent: 80 }
        },
        evaluation: {
          period: 300,
          evaluationPeriods: 3,
          breachedPeriods: 2
        }
      },
      {
        trigger: {
          type: 'database-free-storage',
          properties: { thresholdMB: 1000 }
        }
      }
    ]
  });

  return { resources: { mainDb } };
});
```


In this example, the CPU alarm evaluates three 5-minute periods and only fires if at least two of them breach 80%. The free-storage alarm uses the defaults (one 60-second period, fires after a single breached period) — appropriate for storage pressure that you want to catch right away.

### Multiple alarms with notifications

This example shows a Lambda function with two alarms, each sending notifications to a Slack channel when triggered:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/api.ts'
    }),
    alarms: [
      {
        trigger: {
          type: 'lambda-error-rate',
          properties: { thresholdPercent: 5 }
        },
        description: 'API error rate exceeds 5%',
        evaluation: {
          period: 60,
          evaluationPeriods: 3,
          breachedPeriods: 2
        },
        notificationChannels: [
          {
            type: 'slack',
            properties: {
              conversationId: 'C01234ABCDE',
              accessToken: '$Secret(slack-bot-token)'
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        trigger: {
          type: 'lambda-duration',
          properties: {
            thresholdMilliseconds: 5000,
            statistic: 'p95'
          }
        },
        description: 'P95 duration exceeds 5s'
      }
    ]
  });

  return { resources: { api } };
});
```


The `description` property adds context to notification messages. The `statistic: 'p95'` override on the duration alarm fires only when the 95th percentile exceeds the threshold, filtering out occasional slow invocations.

## Webhook

Stacktape sends a `POST` with `Content-Type: application/json` for each alert:

```json
{
  "id": "cmh3x0q2k0001abcd",
  "sourceKind": "INCIDENT",
  "type": "INCIDENT_OPENED",
  "severity": "ERROR",
  "timestamp": "2026-11-12T20:43:10.512Z",
  "project": "checkout-api",
  "stage": "production",
  "region": "eu-west-1",
  "title": "checkout-api production: API error rate above 5%",
  "details": { "incidentId": "cmh3x0p9z0000abcd", "severity": "ERROR" }
}
```

`details` depends on the kind of alert. With a signing secret, the `X-Stacktape-Signature` header holds the hex
HMAC-SHA256 of the raw body, keyed with the secret. Check it before you trust the request:

```ts
import { createHmac, timingSafeEqual } from 'node:crypto';

// rawBody: the request body exactly as received, before parsing it as JSON.
const isFromStacktape = (rawBody: string, signatureHeader: string | undefined, secret: string) => {
  const expected = createHmac('sha256', secret).update(rawBody).digest('hex');
  const received = signatureHeader ?? '';
  return received.length === expected.length && timingSafeEqual(Buffer.from(received), Buffer.from(expected));
};
```

## Automatic briefing

The briefing runs on your organization's own AI API key. An Owner or Admin connects one from **Connect AI key** on any incident's **AI assessment**, or from **Investigate with AI**. From then on every incident gets a briefing when it opens, and a new one when the incident changes: a new signal joins it, it escalates, or it reopens. A briefing usually arrives within two minutes of the change. Alerts and pages never wait for it. When you connect a key, the newest open incidents without a briefing get one right away.

Without a key, incidents have no briefing. The incident page says so where the briefing would be; Slack cards and handoffs leave it out. A member's own coding-agent sign-in powers only their Investigate and Fix runs, never the briefing.

| Your key | Model |
|---|---|
| Anthropic | Claude Sonnet 4.6 (`claude-sonnet-4-6`) |
| OpenAI | GPT-5.4 mini (`gpt-5.4-mini`) |
| xAI | Grok 4.20 (`grok-4.20-non-reasoning`) |

With several keys connected, the briefing uses the first in this order: Anthropic, OpenAI, xAI. The page says which key wrote each briefing. If the provider refuses the key (it is revoked, the account is out of credit, or it hits a rate limit), the incident shows one line naming the provider and what to do, and an Owner or Admin can reconnect the key from there.

The briefing contains:

- a short summary;
- **Observed impact**;
- **Why it may need attention now**, with a note when the model thinks the severity may be understated or overstated;
- **Hypotheses, most likely first**, each with a confidence;
- **Not known**: what the evidence could not establish;
- a **Likely remedy**, labeled as a hypothesis, only when the evidence supports one;
- a **Suggested next step**.

Statements cite the evidence they rest on, such as a signal, an error group, an uptime check, the release that was live, a deploy or alert event around that time, or an earlier related incident of the same project. The incident page also lists those earlier related incidents.

The briefing gives hypotheses from stored evidence, not a verified diagnosis. The incident's status, severity and signals come from live monitoring, and the briefing never changes them. After the incident resolves, the page labels the briefing as the one made while the incident was open.

The briefing appears:

- on the incident page in the Console, under **AI assessment**, with the time its evidence was collected and the key that wrote it;
- on the incident's card in Slack when the incident goes to a [Stacktape Slack app](/observability/alert-channels#slack) channel: the summary and why it may need attention now, with a link to the full assessment. The card is updated when a new briefing is ready;
- in the [agent handoff](/observability/incidents#agent-handoff-bundle) that **Copy details for agent** and `stacktape incident show` return.

Your key's provider writes the briefing from a scrubbed, bounded snapshot of what Stacktape already stores about the incident (see [What leaves your AWS account](#what-leaves-your-aws-account)). It does not read raw log lines, your source code or live state in your AWS account. One briefing is one model call of about 3,000 input and 1,000 output tokens, billed by the provider to your key's account. Stacktape prepares at most 30 briefings per organization per hour, so during a burst of incidents some briefings wait. A briefing that cannot be prepared within a day is dropped. Alerts and monitoring are unaffected either way.

## Investigate with AI

**Investigate with AI** on the incident page runs the [coding agent you choose](#coding-agents) on your project's [runner](/ci-cd-and-gitops/build-runners): the EC2 instance in the incident's AWS account and region that also runs the project's Console deployments. If the runner is stopped or does not exist yet, Stacktape starts or creates it first.

When the project's repository is connected, the runner checks out the commit Stacktape recorded for the release live on the stack, never a branch head. This works for repositories on github.com, gitlab.com and bitbucket.org connected to the project, and for public repositories on those hosts. The agent works in that checkout with its built-in tools, as your own coding agent would: it reads, searches, edits and deletes files, runs commands, installs packages, runs the project's tests and reproductions, searches the web and fetches documentation, with the internet access the runner has, and without asking for approval. Claude Code also reads the repository's `CLAUDE.md`. Without a connected repository or a recorded full commit, the agent works without source and the report says so.

For Stacktape and AWS, the agent has Stacktape's tools:

- the incident and the project's other incidents, with their signals, evidence, releases, timelines and briefings;
- `aws_call`: any operation from Stacktape's reviewed read-only catalog, the same list [`stacktape aws call`](/cli/aws-call) accepts, anywhere in the incident's AWS account. An operation outside the catalog is refused. Secret values are not in the catalog: Secrets Manager secret values, SSM parameter values and the values of function and container environment variables are never returned;
- the resources of the incident's stack, CloudWatch Logs Insights queries over their logs, their metrics, alarms and runtime configuration, by the resource names in your Stacktape config;
- a search of Stacktape's documentation.

Stacktape executes every AWS read for the agent, with your connected AWS account's role, in a session named after the run, so your CloudTrail shows which run read what. Before the agent sees an answer, Stacktape masks known sensitive shapes in it: secrets and tokens, values under sensitive key names, email and IP addresses, card numbers and user home paths. The agent itself has no AWS credentials: nothing on the runner that it can reach holds an AWS credential, a Stacktape API key, a deployment credential or a Git write token. The credential that funds the run goes only to the agent's own process, and to its sign-in file for as long as the agent runs. Whether the commands the agent runs can read it depends on the agent: see [Coding agents](#coding-agents).

When the run ends, the incident page shows its report:

- a summary;
- **What the agent reports seeing**: each observation with where the agent says it saw it;
- **Agent conclusions**, each with a confidence. They are model output, to be checked;
- **Not known**: what the agent could not establish;
- **What the agent did**: every command it ran, file it read, wrote or edited, page it fetched and Stacktape tool it called, in order, recorded from the agent's own output and masked. Stacktape keeps up to 300 steps and counts the rest.

An investigation deploys, rolls back and resolves nothing, and changes nothing in your AWS account. What it changes in the checkout on the runner is discarded with the run.

A few limits apply:

- One run per incident can be queued or running at a time.
- A run stops after 60 minutes. A run funded by the organization's API key also stops at 20 USD of model spend where the agent reports its spend while it works: Claude Code stops itself, and Stacktape stops OpenCode. Codex and Grok report none, so only the 60 minutes bound them. A run funded by a member's subscription counts against that plan's limits.
- A run waits for free capacity on the runner and fails without starting if none is free within 30 minutes.
- The incident must belong to a stack in an AWS account connected to your organization.

The incident's card in Slack also has **Investigate with AI** and **Fix with AI** buttons. They open the incident in the Console, where you sign in and choose the agent and who pays. Nothing starts from Slack itself.

## Using your own coding agent instead

**Copy details for agent** on the incident page, [`stacktape incident show`](/cli/incident-show) and the MCP server's [`stacktape_incident`](/using-with-ai/mcp-server-setup#incident-tool) tool return the same [handoff](/observability/incidents#agent-handoff-bundle). It holds the incident's signals, evidence, releases, timeline and related incidents, the automatic briefing when there is one, and the reports of hosted runs on the incident. Give it to a coding agent on your own machine when:

- the repository is on GitLab or Bitbucket, so Fix with AI is not available;
- no credential is connected for the agent you want;
- you prefer to diagnose and change the code with your own agent and tools.

## Forwarding to Datadog

The `datadog` type needs only `apiKey`. Datadog bills its own ingestion and retention on top of Firehose.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts'
    }),
    logging: {
      logForwarding: {
        type: 'datadog',
        properties: {
          apiKey: '$Secret(datadog-api-key)'
        }
      }
    }
  });

  return { resources: { api } };
});
```


Store the Datadog API key as a [secret](/configuration/secrets) using the `$Secret()` directive so the credential is not committed in plain text.

The default endpoint is `https://aws-kinesis-http-intake.logs.datadoghq.com/v1/input`. Use the EU URL when your Datadog account is in the EU region.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const app = new WebService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/app.ts'
    }),
    resources: { cpu: 0.25, memory: 512 },
    logging: {
      logForwarding: {
        type: 'datadog',
        properties: {
          apiKey: '$Secret(datadog-api-key)',
          endpointUrl: 'https://aws-kinesis-http-intake.logs.datadoghq.eu/v1/input'
        }
      }
    }
  });

  return { resources: { app } };
});
```


The optional `endpointUrl` overrides the default Datadog endpoint. Use the EU URL (`datadoghq.eu`) when your Datadog account is in the EU region. If omitted, Stacktape uses the US endpoint (`datadoghq.com`).

## Forwarding to Highlight.io

Highlight.io is an open-source observability platform. Highlight forwarding requires a `projectId`; the `endpointUrl` defaults to `https://pub.highlight.io/v1/logs/firehose` and can be overridden for self-hosted or regional endpoints.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts'
    }),
    logging: {
      logForwarding: {
        type: 'highlight',
        properties: {
          projectId: '4d7k2qg0'
        }
      }
    }
  });

  return { resources: { api } };
});
```


The `projectId` is found in your Highlight.io console under project settings. The default endpoint is `https://pub.highlight.io/v1/logs/firehose`. Override `endpointUrl` if you run a self-hosted Highlight instance or need a regional endpoint.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WorkerService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const processor = new WorkerService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/processor.ts'
    }),
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 },
    logging: {
      logForwarding: {
        type: 'highlight',
        properties: {
          projectId: '4d7k2qg0',
          endpointUrl: 'https://highlight.internal.example.com/v1/logs/firehose'
        }
      }
    }
  });

  return { resources: { processor } };
});
```

## Forwarding to a custom HTTP endpoint

The `http-endpoint` log forwarding type forwards logs to any HTTPS endpoint that accepts Firehose delivery. This is the most flexible option and the one to use when your destination is not Datadog or Highlight.io — for example, a self-hosted ingestion API or a third-party observability platform that supports Firehose HTTPS delivery.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts'
    }),
    logging: {
      logForwarding: {
        type: 'http-endpoint',
        properties: {
          endpointUrl: 'https://logs.example.com/v1/ingest',
          accessKey: '$Secret(log-ingest-api-key)',
          gzipEncodingEnabled: true,
          retryDuration: 120,
          parameters: {
            'x-source': 'stacktape',
            'x-environment': 'production'
          }
        }
      }
    }
  });

  return { resources: { api } };
});
```

## Writing the script

TypeScript works out of the box — Stacktape bundles the script at deploy time. Imports of your own helper files are allowed; npm packages are not, except the ones the Synthetics runtime provides.

A browser test exports a `handler` and gets the browser from the runtime's `synthetics` helper:

```ts
import { synthetics } from '@aws/synthetics-playwright';
import { expect } from '@playwright/test';

export const handler = async () => {
  try {
    const browser = await synthetics.launch();
    const page = await synthetics.newPage(browser);
    await page.goto(process.env.BASE_URL, { timeout: 30000 });
    await page.getByRole('link', { name: 'Sign in' }).click();
    await page.screenshot({ path: '/tmp/signin.png' });
    expect(await page.title()).toContain('Sign in');
  } finally {
    await synthetics.close();
  }
};
```

A run fails when the handler throws (including failed `expect` assertions). Screenshots written to `/tmp` are stored with the run's results in your deployment bucket. `retentionDays` (default 31) controls how long the pass/fail run history is kept; the S3 artifacts themselves and the canary's logs are not auto-deleted by AWS.

An `api` test uses `executeHttpStep` so every step gets its own timing and success metrics:

```ts
const synthetics = require('Synthetics');

exports.handler = async () => {
  await synthetics.executeHttpStep('health', {
    hostname: 'api.example.com',
    method: 'GET',
    path: '/health',
    protocol: 'https:'
  });
};
```

## Basic example

All properties are optional. `sourceDirectoryPath` defaults to the directory of the Stacktape configuration file.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebService, BuildpackImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new WebService({
    packaging: new BuildpackImagePackaging({
      sourceDirectoryPath: './api'
    }),
    resources: {
      cpu: 0.5,
      memory: 1024
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { api }
  };
});
```


Example (YAML):

```yaml
resources:
  api:
    type: web-service
    properties:
      # stp-focus
      packaging:
        type: buildpack
        properties:
          sourceDirectoryPath: ./api
      # stp-end-focus
      resources:
        cpu: 0.5
        memory: 1024
```


A web service must listen on the port in the `PORT` environment variable. Most detected start commands already do. If yours does not, set `startCommand`, for example `uvicorn app.main:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port $PORT`.

### Start, build and install commands


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebService, BuildpackImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new WebService({
    packaging: new BuildpackImagePackaging({
      sourceDirectoryPath: './api',
      installCommand: 'uv sync --locked --no-dev',
      buildCommand: 'python manage.py collectstatic --noinput',
      startCommand: 'gunicorn --bind 0.0.0.0:$PORT config.wsgi'
    }),
    resources: {
      cpu: 0.5,
      memory: 1024
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { api }
  };
});
```

### Tool versions and system packages

`packages` sets tool versions by mise tool name, for example `node`, `python`, `go`, `rust`, `ruby`, `php`, `java` or `pnpm`. A version file in the project, such as `.nvmrc` or `.python-version`, does the same and also works outside Stacktape.

`aptPackages` installs Debian packages. Common libraries, such as `libpq` for PostgreSQL clients, are installed automatically when a dependency that needs them is detected.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WorkerService, BuildpackImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const renderer = new WorkerService({
    packaging: new BuildpackImagePackaging({
      sourceDirectoryPath: './renderer',
      packages: { node: '22', pnpm: '10' },
      aptPackages: ['ffmpeg']
    }),
    resources: {
      cpu: 1,
      memory: 2048
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { renderer }
  };
});
```

### Build environment variables

`buildEnvironment` makes variables available to the install and build commands, for example a token for a private package registry or a public URL that a frontend build embeds. Values are passed as Docker build secrets. They never appear in the image or in the build log, and a changed value rebuilds the image.

Runtime variables come from the resource's `environment` property, not from `buildEnvironment`.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebService, BuildpackImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const web = new WebService({
    packaging: new BuildpackImagePackaging({
      sourceDirectoryPath: './web',
      buildEnvironment: [{ name: 'VITE_API_URL', value: 'https://api.example.com' }]
    }),
    resources: {
      cpu: 0.25,
      memory: 512
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { web }
  };
});
```

### Railpack configuration

`railpackConfig` accepts the same object as a [railpack.json](https://railpack.com/config/file) file. Use it for build steps, caches or deploy settings that the properties above do not cover. In a command list, `"..."` stands for the detected commands, so the example below runs `npx prisma generate` after the detected build.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebService, BuildpackImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new WebService({
    packaging: new BuildpackImagePackaging({
      sourceDirectoryPath: './api',
      railpackConfig: {
        steps: { build: { commands: ['...', 'npx prisma generate'] } }
      }
    }),
    resources: {
      cpu: 0.5,
      memory: 1024
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { api }
  };
});
```

## Basic example

The only required packaging property is `buildContextPath`: the directory sent to `docker build` as context, relative to your Stacktape config file. The `dockerfilePath` property is optional; when set, it specifies the Dockerfile path relative to `buildContextPath`. Examples below include a `resources` property) this belongs to the resource configuration (e.g., `WebService`, `BatchJob`), not the packaging type. See each resource's dedicated page for supported CPU and memory values.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebService, DockerfilePackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new WebService({
    packaging: new DockerfilePackaging({
      buildContextPath: './'
    }),
    resources: {
      cpu: 0.25,
      memory: 512
    }
  });

  return { resources: { api } };
});
```


A matching Dockerfile for a Node.js app:

```dockerfile
FROM node:20-alpine
WORKDIR /app
COPY package*.json ./
RUN npm ci --omit=dev
COPY . .
CMD ["node", "src/server.js"]
```

## Dockerfile path

The `dockerfilePath` property is optional. When set, it specifies the path to your Dockerfile relative to `buildContextPath`. This is useful when you maintain multiple Dockerfiles (development, production, CI) in the same repository or keep Dockerfiles in a subdirectory.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebService, DockerfilePackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new WebService({
    packaging: new DockerfilePackaging({
      buildContextPath: './',
      dockerfilePath: './docker/Dockerfile.prod'
    }),
    resources: {
      cpu: 0.5,
      memory: 1024
    }
  });

  return { resources: { api } };
});
```


In this example, `buildContextPath` is the project root (`./`), and the Dockerfile lives at `./docker/Dockerfile.prod` relative to that root. The leading `./` is conventional path syntax; Stacktape interprets `dockerfilePath` relative to `buildContextPath`.

## Build arguments

Pass values into the Dockerfile at build time using `buildArgs`. Each entry has an `argName` and a `value`. Stacktape passes `buildArgs` to `docker build`. In Docker, matching `ARG` instructions can use those values during the build; if you copy an ARG into `ENV`, the value becomes part of the runtime environment.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebService, DockerfilePackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new WebService({
    packaging: new DockerfilePackaging({
      buildContextPath: './',
      buildArgs: [
        { argName: 'NODE_ENV', value: 'production' },
        { argName: 'APP_VERSION', value: '2.1.0' }
      ]
    }),
    resources: {
      cpu: 0.5,
      memory: 1024
    }
  });

  return { resources: { api } };
});
```


In the Dockerfile, declare a matching `ARG` in the stage that uses it, after `FROM`:

```dockerfile
FROM node:20-alpine
ARG NODE_ENV=development
ARG APP_VERSION=latest
ENV NODE_ENV=${NODE_ENV}
LABEL version=${APP_VERSION}
WORKDIR /app
COPY . .
RUN npm ci
CMD ["node", "src/server.js"]
```

An `ARG` declared before `FROM` is visible only to the `FROM` line itself, so `ENV` and `LABEL` would get empty values; each stage of a multi-stage build needs its own `ARG`. The default after `=` applies when no `buildArgs` value is passed, so the Dockerfile also builds on its own.


> **Warning:** Do not pass secrets through `buildArgs`: they are visible in the image layer history. Use runtime configuration for secrets instead. See [secrets](/configuration/secrets) for details.

## Overriding CMD and ENTRYPOINT

You can override the Dockerfile's `CMD` and `ENTRYPOINT` instructions in the Stacktape config without modifying the Dockerfile itself.

- **`command`** overrides `CMD`: the default arguments passed to the entry point.
- **`entryPoint`** overrides `ENTRYPOINT`: the executable that runs when the container starts.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebService, DockerfilePackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new WebService({
    packaging: new DockerfilePackaging({
      buildContextPath: './',
      entryPoint: ['/bin/sh', '-c'],
      command: ['node src/server.js']
    }),
    resources: {
      cpu: 0.5,
      memory: 1024
    }
  });

  return { resources: { api } };
});
```


This is useful when you use a generic base image and want to swap the start command per stage or environment without rebuilding. The container workload packaging variant includes both `entryPoint` and `command`. The batch job packaging variant supports `command` but does not include `entryPoint`.

## Using with batch jobs

The batch job variant (`DockerfileBjImagePackaging`) supports `command` but does not include `entryPoint`. See [batch job](/resources/compute/batch-job) for the full resource configuration.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, BatchJob, Bucket, DockerfilePackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const outputBucket = new Bucket({});

  const processor = new BatchJob({
    container: {
      packaging: new DockerfilePackaging({
        buildContextPath: './job'
      })
    },
    resources: {
      cpu: 2,
      memory: 8000
    },
    connectTo: [outputBucket]
  });

  return { resources: { outputBucket, processor } };
});
```

## Using with multi-container workloads

A [multi-container workload](/resources/compute/multi-container-workload) defines multiple containers, each with its own packaging. This example uses `DockerfilePackaging` for both containers, each pointing to a separate build context. You can also mix packaging modes: for example, one container built from a Dockerfile and another with a [prebuilt image](/packaging/containers/prebuilt-image). See [multi-container workload](/resources/compute/multi-container-workload) for the full resource configuration.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, MultiContainerWorkload, DockerfilePackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const workload = new MultiContainerWorkload({
    containers: [
      {
        name: 'api',
        packaging: new DockerfilePackaging({
          buildContextPath: './api'
        })
      },
      {
        name: 'worker',
        packaging: new DockerfilePackaging({
          buildContextPath: './worker',
          dockerfilePath: './Dockerfile.worker'
        })
      }
    ],
    resources: {
      cpu: 1,
      memory: 2048
    }
  });

  return { resources: { workload } };
});
```

## Basic example

`entryfilePath` is the only required property. It is relative to the Stacktape configuration file.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new WebService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/server.ts'
    }),
    resources: {
      cpu: 0.25,
      memory: 512
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { api }
  };
});
```


Example (YAML):

```yaml
resources:
  api:
    type: web-service
    properties:
      # stp-focus
      packaging:
        type: js-bundle
        properties:
          entryfilePath: ./src/server.ts
      # stp-end-focus
      resources:
        cpu: 0.25
        memory: 512
```


The `cpu` and `memory` values belong to the web service, not to packaging. See [web service](/resources/compute/web-service) for valid values.

## Bundling options

All options are properties of `JsBundleImagePackaging` next to `entryfilePath`.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new WebService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/server.ts',
      nodeVersion: 22,
      outputModuleFormat: 'esm',
      emitTsDecoratorMetadata: true,
      dependenciesToExcludeFromBundle: ['dd-trace']
    }),
    resources: {
      cpu: 0.25,
      memory: 512
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { api }
  };
});
```


`dependenciesToExcludeFromBundle: ['dd-trace']` is an example: an APM agent instruments other modules as they load from `node_modules`. List the packages your application cannot bundle, or omit the property. Native packages, packages that read their own files and Prisma's client work without it.

### Bun and Deno

`runtime` selects the JavaScript runtime of the image: `node` (default), `bun` or `deno`. With `bun`, the bundle targets Bun and runs on the official `oven/bun:1` image with `bun index.js`. With `deno`, the bundle runs on the pinned `denoland/deno` 2.9 Debian image with `deno run --allow-all index.js`. Dependencies that cannot be bundled are installed with the runtime's own package manager (`bun add`, `deno add npm:`). `nodeVersion` has no effect on these runtimes. Lambda functions have no `runtime` option here; they run on the Node.js runtime.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new WebService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/server.ts',
      runtime: 'bun'
    }),
    resources: {
      cpu: 0.25,
      memory: 512
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { api }
  };
});
```

### Using glibc instead of Alpine musl

The Node.js image is based on Alpine Linux, which uses musl libc. (Bun and Deno images are Debian-based and already use glibc.) Some native dependencies need glibc, for example `sharp`, `canvas`, `bcrypt` and `puppeteer`. Set `requiresGlibcBinaries: true` to use a glibc-based image. The image is larger, so enable it only when you see errors such as `Error loading shared library` or `GLIBC_X.XX not found`.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const imageService = new WebService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/processor.ts',
      requiresGlibcBinaries: true
    }),
    resources: {
      cpu: 0.5,
      memory: 1024
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { imageService }
  };
});
```

### Custom Docker build commands

`customDockerBuildCommands` adds shell commands to the image build. Each string runs as a `RUN` instruction. Use it to install system packages or add build-time files.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new WebService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/server.ts',
      customDockerBuildCommands: ['apk add --no-cache ffmpeg', 'apk add --no-cache imagemagick']
    }),
    resources: {
      cpu: 0.5,
      memory: 1024
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { api }
  };
});
```


> **Info:** `apk add` works on the default Alpine image. With `requiresGlibcBinaries: true` the image is not Alpine, so use that image's package manager instead.

## File inclusion and exclusion

`includeFiles` and `excludeFiles` take glob patterns relative to the Stacktape configuration file. `excludeDependencies` removes named packages from the image.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WorkerService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const worker = new WorkerService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/worker.ts',
      includeFiles: ['./config/**', './templates/**'],
      excludeFiles: ['./tests/**', './**/*.test.ts'],
      excludeDependencies: ['jest', '@types/jest']
    }),
    resources: {
      cpu: 0.25,
      memory: 512
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { worker }
  };
});
```


- **`includeFiles`** adds files that the code reads at runtime but does not import, such as templates or static assets.
- **`excludeFiles`** removes files the service does not need, such as tests and development configuration.
- **`excludeDependencies`** removes packages that are not needed at runtime.

## Basic example

This runs the official Apache `httpd` image as a web service. The image listens on port 80, so the example sets `port: 80`; without it, Stacktape sends traffic to port 3000.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebService, PrebuiltImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const webService = new WebService({
    packaging: new PrebuiltImagePackaging({
      image: 'httpd:2.4'
    }),
    port: 80,
    resources: {
      cpu: 0.25,
      memory: 512
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { webService }
  };
});
```


After the deploy, open the service URL the CLI prints; you should see Apache's "It works!" page.

Stacktape also sets the `PORT` environment variable to the service's port. Images that read `PORT` (most Node.js, Python and Go servers) follow it; images with their own fixed port, like `httpd` or `nginx`, need `port` set to that port.

The `image` property accepts any valid container image reference: a Docker Hub short name (`httpd:2.4`), a full registry URL (`123456789012.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/my-repo:v1.2.3`), or a GitHub Container Registry path (`ghcr.io/my-org/my-app:main`).

## Private registry images

To pull from a private registry that requires authentication, store your credentials in AWS Secrets Manager and reference the secret ARN via `repositoryCredentialsSecretArn`. The secret must be a JSON object with `username` and `password` keys.

Create the secret using the Stacktape CLI [`secret set`](/cli/secret-set) command. The command accepts `--secret-name` to set the secret name and `--value` to provide the JSON credentials inline. See the [CLI reference](/cli/secret-set) for the full list of flags (including `--file` and `--region`).

```bash
stacktape secret set my-registry-creds --value '{"username":"my-user","password":"my-token"}'
```

Then reference the secret ARN in your config:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebService, PrebuiltImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new WebService({
    packaging: new PrebuiltImagePackaging({
      image: 'registry.example.com/my-org/my-app:v2.1.0',
      repositoryCredentialsSecretArn:
        'arn:aws:secretsmanager:us-east-1:123456789012:secret:my-registry-creds-AbCdEf'
    }),
    resources: {
      cpu: 0.5,
      memory: 1024
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { api }
  };
});
```


The `repositoryCredentialsSecretArn` property accepts the ARN of an AWS Secrets Manager secret containing `username` and `password` keys. Underneath, AWS ECS uses these credentials when pulling the image from the private registry.


> **Info:** Amazon ECR does not need `repositoryCredentialsSecretArn`: underneath, AWS ECS authenticates to ECR via the task execution role. Use `repositoryCredentialsSecretArn` for non-ECR registries that require explicit username/password credentials.

### Command override

The `command` property overrides the image's `CMD` instruction. Use this to pass different arguments or run an alternative script:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WorkerService, PrebuiltImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const worker = new WorkerService({
    packaging: new PrebuiltImagePackaging({
      image: 'my-org/data-pipeline:v1.4.0',
      command: ['node', 'dist/worker.js', '--mode', 'batch']
    }),
    resources: {
      cpu: 1,
      memory: 2048
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { worker }
  };
});
```


The `command` property accepts a string array. Each element becomes a separate argument: this matches Docker's exec-form `CMD ["arg1", "arg2"]` behavior.

### Entrypoint override

The `entryPoint` property overrides the image's `ENTRYPOINT` instruction. This is useful when you need to wrap the default process with an init script or switch the executable entirely:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, PrivateService, PrebuiltImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const internal = new PrivateService({
    packaging: new PrebuiltImagePackaging({
      image: 'my-org/api-server:v3.0.0',
      entryPoint: ['/app/entrypoint.sh'],
      command: ['--config', '/etc/app/config.yaml']
    }),
    resources: {
      cpu: 0.5,
      memory: 1024
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { internal }
  };
});
```


When both `entryPoint` and `command` are specified, `entryPoint` sets the executable and `command` provides its arguments: matching Docker's `ENTRYPOINT` + `CMD` composition model.


> **Info:** The container-workload packaging variant (`PrebuiltImageCwPackagingProps`) supports `image`, `command`, `entryPoint`, and `repositoryCredentialsSecretArn`. The batch-job packaging variant (`PrebuiltImageBjPackagingProps`) supports only `image` and `command`.

## Multi-container workloads

When using prebuilt images in a [multi-container workload](/resources/compute/multi-container-workload), each container specifies its own packaging independently. This lets you combine prebuilt third-party images (like a sidecar proxy) with application containers built from source:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  MultiContainerWorkload,
  PrebuiltImagePackaging,
  JsBundleImagePackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const app = new MultiContainerWorkload({
    containers: [
      {
        name: 'api',
        packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
          entryfilePath: './src/server.ts'
        })
      },
      {
        name: 'envoy-proxy',
        packaging: new PrebuiltImagePackaging({
          image: 'envoyproxy/envoy:v1.28-latest',
          command: ['envoy', '-c', '/etc/envoy/envoy.yaml']
        })
      }
    ],
    resources: {
      cpu: 1,
      memory: 2048
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { app }
  };
});
```


This pattern is common for adding observability sidecars (Datadog agent, OpenTelemetry collector), reverse proxies (Envoy, Nginx), or log forwarders alongside your application container.

## Basic example

`entryfilePath` is the only required property. It is relative to the Stacktape configuration file.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, BuildpackLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const processor = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new BuildpackLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/process.py'
    }),
    runtime: 'python3.13',
    memory: 512,
    timeout: 60
  });

  return {
    resources: { processor }
  };
});
```


Example (YAML):

```yaml
resources:
  processor:
    type: function
    properties:
      # stp-focus
      packaging:
        type: buildpack
        properties:
          entryfilePath: ./src/process.py
      runtime: python3.13
      # stp-end-focus
      memory: 512
      timeout: 60
```


`memory` and `timeout` are [Lambda function](/resources/compute/lambda-function) settings, not packaging options.

## Runtime and language versions

The function's `runtime` property decides the language version. The build uses the Lambda build image of that runtime, so the artifact matches the environment it runs in. When `runtime` is not set, Stacktape selects a default runtime from the entry file's extension: `python3.13`, `java25`, `ruby4.0`, `dotnet10`, or `provided.al2023` for Go and Rust. Python defaults to 3.13 rather than 3.14 because many pinned packages with native code, such as `psycopg-binary` 3.2.3 or `pydantic-core` 2.27, have no wheels for 3.14. Set `runtime` explicitly to pin a version.

A Python build also compiles the `.py` files it packages with the function's Python version. A syntax error in the entry file or in a project module it imports, directly or through other modules, stops the build with the file and line, instead of failing every request after the deployment. A file the entry file does not import, such as a script, a test fixture or a template, only produces a warning; leave it out with `excludeFiles` to silence it. Imports made at run time, for example with `importlib`, are not followed.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, BuildpackLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const reports = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new BuildpackLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/main/java/com/example/Handler.java'
    }),
    runtime: 'java21',
    memory: 1024
  });

  return {
    resources: { reports }
  };
});
```


See [Lambda function runtime](/resources/compute/lambda-function#runtime-resources) for the list of runtime identifiers.

## Python

Stacktape resolves and installs Python dependencies with `uv`. It finds the dependency file automatically; set `python.packageManagerFile` when it is somewhere else.

| Option | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `packageManagerFile` | Path to `requirements.txt`, `Pipfile`, `pyproject.toml` or `uv.lock`. When both `pyproject.toml` and `uv.lock` exist, the lock file is used. |
| `uvOptionalDependencies` | Extras from `pyproject.toml` to include. Passed to `uv pip compile` as `--extra`. |
| `uvWithGroups` | Dependency groups to include. Passed as `--group`. |
| `uvWithoutGroups` | Dependency groups to exclude. Passed as `--no-group`. |
| `uvOnlyGroups` | Include only these groups, without the project dependencies and default groups. Passed as `--only-group`. |


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, BuildpackLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new BuildpackLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './app/main.py',
      python: {
        packageManagerFile: './pyproject.toml',
        uvWithGroups: ['lambda']
      }
    }),
    runtime: 'python3.12'
  });

  return {
    resources: { api }
  };
});
```

## Java

Stacktape builds a Java function with Maven when the project's nearest build file is a `pom.xml`, and with Gradle otherwise. Set `java.useMaven` to choose explicitly. `java.packageManagerFile` points to the `build.gradle` or `pom.xml` build file.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, BuildpackLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const processor = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new BuildpackLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/main/java/Handler.java',
      java: {
        useMaven: true,
        packageManagerFile: './pom.xml'
      }
    }),
    runtime: 'java21',
    memory: 1024,
    timeout: 60
  });

  return {
    resources: { processor }
  };
});
```


Java functions start more slowly than interpreted languages because of JVM startup. 1024 MB of memory is a reasonable starting point.

## .NET

`dotnet.projectFile` points to the function's `.csproj` project file.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, BuildpackLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const dotnetApi = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new BuildpackLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/Handler.cs',
      dotnet: {
        projectFile: './src/MyFunction.csproj'
      }
    }),
    runtime: 'dotnet8',
    memory: 512,
    timeout: 30
  });

  return {
    resources: { dotnetApi }
  };
});
```

## Rust

Rust functions are built with [cargo-lambda](https://www.cargo-lambda.info). Set `entryfilePath` to `src/main.rs` and `runtime` to `provided.al2023`. The compiled binary becomes the function's `bootstrap` file, and `handlerFunction` is ignored.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, BuildpackLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const resizer = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new BuildpackLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/main.rs'
    }),
    runtime: 'provided.al2023',
    architecture: 'arm64',
    memory: 256
  });

  return {
    resources: { resizer }
  };
});
```


Example (YAML):

```yaml
resources:
  resizer:
    type: function
    properties:
      # stp-focus
      packaging:
        type: buildpack
        properties:
          entryfilePath: ./src/main.rs
      runtime: provided.al2023
      # stp-end-focus
      architecture: arm64
      memory: 256
```

## Basic example

Specify `packagePath` and `handler` in the packaging, and `runtime` on the Lambda resource:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, CustomArtifactLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new CustomArtifactLambdaPackaging({
      packagePath: './dist/lambda.zip',
      handler: 'index.js:default'
    }),
    runtime: 'nodejs22.x'
  });

  return {
    resources: { api }
  };
});
```

### handler

The `handler` property tells Lambda which function to invoke when the Lambda is triggered. The syntax is `filepath:functionName`.

For example, `index.js:default` invokes the `default` export from `index.js` at the artifact root, and `handlers/process.js:main` invokes `main` from `handlers/process.js`. Stacktape turns the path into Lambda's handler format by dropping the file extension: `handlers/process.main`.

Java and .NET artifacts use Lambda's own handler format, which Stacktape passes through as written: `com.example.Handler::handleRequest` for Java, `Assembly::Namespace.Class::Method` for .NET.

Set `handler` here and `runtime` on the Lambda resource: Stacktape does not infer them from the artifact.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, CustomArtifactLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const processor = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new CustomArtifactLambdaPackaging({
      packagePath: './build/output',
      handler: 'process.js:main'
    }),
    runtime: 'nodejs22.x',
    memory: 512,
    timeout: 60
  });

  return {
    resources: { processor }
  };
});
```


In this example, `packagePath` points to a directory. Stacktape zips it automatically, and Lambda invokes the `main` export from `process.js` at the root of the resulting package. The `memory` and `timeout` properties are [Lambda function](/resources/compute/lambda-function) settings, not part of the packaging configuration.

## Directory packaging example

Custom artifact packaging accepts directories as input. When your build produces a directory, point `packagePath` at the directory root:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, CustomArtifactLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const fn = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new CustomArtifactLambdaPackaging({
      packagePath: './lambda-build',
      handler: 'app.py:handler'
    }),
    runtime: 'python3.12',
    memory: 256,
    timeout: 30
  });

  return {
    resources: { fn }
  };
});
```


Stacktape zips the directory before deployment. Make sure the file referenced by `handler` is located at the expected path inside the package: in this case, `app.py` must be at the root of the `./lambda-build` directory. As with the previous example, `memory` and `timeout` are [Lambda function](/resources/compute/lambda-function) settings configured alongside packaging.

## Basic example

`entryfilePath` is the only required property. It is relative to the Stacktape configuration file.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts'
    })
  });

  return {
    resources: { api }
  };
});
```


The handler file exports a function that AWS Lambda invokes:

```typescript
export const handler = async (event: any) => {
  return {
    statusCode: 200,
    body: JSON.stringify({ message: 'Hello from Lambda' })
  };
};
```

## Handler function

The `handlerFunction` property names the exported function to execute when the Lambda is invoked. Set it when your entry file exports a specifically named handler function.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const processOrder = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/orders.ts',
      handlerFunction: 'processOrder'
    }),
    memory: 512,
    timeout: 30
  });

  return {
    resources: { processOrder }
  };
});
```


The `memory` and `timeout` properties in these examples are [`LambdaFunction`](/resources/compute/lambda-function) settings, not packaging options: they are included here for realistic context.

The handler file would export the named function:

```typescript
export const processOrder = async (event: any) => {
  // process the order
  return { statusCode: 200, body: 'Order processed' };
};
```

### Including additional files

The `includeFiles` property explicitly includes files matched by glob patterns in the deployment package. Paths are relative to your Stacktape config file. Use this for any file your handler reads at runtime via the filesystem (e.g., `fs.readFile`) rather than through an `import`.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const emailSender = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/email-sender.ts',
      includeFiles: ['./templates/**/*.html', './config/email-config.json']
    }),
    memory: 256,
    timeout: 15
  });

  return {
    resources: { emailSender }
  };
});
```

### Source maps

Each deployment stores the function's source maps in the stack's deployment bucket instead of the deployment package, so the package carries code only. The maps contain no source code. When the [Stacktape Console](/observability/error-tracking) first sees an error from the function, it uses the maps of the code that threw to show the stack trace with the original file, line and function. Stack traces in CloudWatch logs point into the bundled code.

The deployment bucket keeps a function's maps as long as it keeps a deployment package that uses them, so the maps of every release you can [roll back](/cli/rollback) to stay available.

- **`keepSourceMapsInPackage: true`** ships the maps in the package and turns on Node.js `--enable-source-maps`, so CloudWatch stack traces are mapped too. The package gets larger, and Node.js reads the maps on every cold start.
- **`outputSourceMapsTo`** also saves complete maps, original source included, to a local directory, for uploading to Sentry, Datadog or another tool.
- **`disableSourceMaps: true`** generates no maps; error locations then point into the bundled code everywhere.

Key options:

- **`nodeVersion`**: major Node.js version used to build the bundle. Supported: 16, 17, 18, 19, 20, 21, 22, 23, 24. Default: `24`. This does not replace the Lambda resource's `runtime`; set both to matching versions when overriding the default.
- **`outputModuleFormat`**: `'cjs'` (CommonJS) or `'esm'` (ES Modules, enables top-level `await`). Node.js 24 and later use ESM output; with earlier Node.js versions the default is CommonJS. Edge functions remain CommonJS. Some npm packages don't support ESM, and ESM may produce less readable stack traces.
- **`tsConfigPath`**: path to your `tsconfig.json`, used to resolve path aliases during bundling.
- **`emitTsDecoratorMetadata`**: enable for frameworks that rely on TypeScript decorator metadata reflection (NestJS, TypeORM).
- **`dependenciesToExcludeFromBundle`**: packages treated as external (not bundled into the single output file). They're installed separately in the deployment package. Set the array to `['*']` to exclude all dependencies from the bundle.
- **`dependenciesToExcludeFromDeploymentPackage`**: removes non-bundled dependencies from the final package. Only applies to dependencies already excluded from the bundle. Set the array to `['*']` to exclude all non-bundled dependencies.
- **`disableSourceMaps`**: skips source map generation. Error locations then point into the bundled code.
- **`keepSourceMapsInPackage`**: keeps source maps in the deployment package and maps CloudWatch stack traces. Default: `false`. See [Source maps](#source-maps).
- **`outputSourceMapsTo`**: also saves complete source maps to a local directory. Useful for external error tracking tools like Sentry or Datadog.
- **`minify`**: minifies the bundle (whitespace and syntax). Local function and variable names are kept, so stack traces and error messages stay readable. Default: `true`.
- **`minifyIdentifiers`**: also shortens local variable and function names. Slightly smaller bundle, but the names disappear from stack traces and error messages and change with every build, so Stacktape Console cannot group the same error across deployments. Default: `false`.
- **`bundleAwsSdk`**: bundles `@aws-sdk/*` from your `node_modules` instead of using the copy the Lambda runtime provides. Enable it when you need a newer SDK than the runtime ships. Default: `false`.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    runtime: 'nodejs22.x',
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/api.ts',
      nodeVersion: 22,
      outputModuleFormat: 'esm',
      dependenciesToExcludeFromBundle: ['dd-trace'],
      tsConfigPath: './tsconfig.json'
    }),
    memory: 1024,
    timeout: 30
  });

  return {
    resources: { api }
  };
});
```


This example sets both `runtime: 'nodejs22.x'` on the Lambda function and `nodeVersion: 22` in its packaging config. The former selects the AWS Lambda runtime; the latter selects the Node.js version the bundle targets. Keep them aligned. Because Node.js 22 otherwise defaults to CommonJS, `outputModuleFormat: 'esm'` explicitly enables ES Module output. Use `dependenciesToExcludeFromBundle` for dependencies you do not want statically bundled: excluded dependencies are installed separately in the deployment package. An APM agent such as `dd-trace` needs this, because it instruments other modules as they load from `node_modules`. Setting `tsConfigPath` lets the bundler resolve TypeScript path aliases during bundling.


> **Info:** Use `dependenciesToExcludeFromBundle` for dependencies with native binaries. Excluded dependencies are installed separately in the deployment package rather than being statically bundled. This is the most common fix when a dependency works locally but fails in Lambda with a binary-related error.

## Function packaging

Function packaging bundles your code and dependencies into a Lambda deployment zip.

| Type | Use when |
|------|----------|
| [**`js-bundle`**](/packaging/function/js-bundle) | JavaScript or TypeScript. Point to the entry file. Stacktape bundles it into one minified file, keeps its source maps in the deployment bucket, leaves the AWS SDK to the Lambda runtime, moves code shared between functions into a shared layer, and installs dependencies with native binaries separately. |
| [**`buildpack`**](/packaging/function/buildpack) | Python, Java, Go, Ruby, .NET or Rust. Point to the entry file. Stacktape installs dependencies and compiles the code in Docker on the Lambda build image of the function's `runtime`. |
| [**`custom-artifact`**](/packaging/function/custom-artifact) | You build the deployment package yourself. Provide a zip, directory or other file. Stacktape zips directories and non-zip files automatically. |

Use `custom-artifact` only when your build can't be expressed through `js-bundle` or `buildpack`, for example a custom compiler step, a monorepo build tool, or an artifact produced by a separate CI job. It needs a `packagePath` and a `handler`, and the function needs a `runtime`.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts'
    })
  });

  return { resources: { api } };
});
```


`js-bundle` options such as `nodeVersion`, `outputModuleFormat` and `keepSourceMapsInPackage` are direct properties next to `entryfilePath`. See [JS Bundle for Lambda](/packaging/function/js-bundle). For other languages, the function's `runtime` selects the language version; see [Buildpack for Lambda](/packaging/function/buildpack).

### Choosing a container packaging type

Use **`js-bundle`** (`JsBundleImagePackaging`) for JavaScript and TypeScript services that start from one entry file, such as Express, Hono, Fastify or NestJS, on Node.js (the default), Bun or Deno (`runtime: 'bun'` or `'deno'`). Use **`buildpack`** (`BuildpackImagePackaging`) for every other language and for frameworks with their own build output. It works without configuration for projects that follow their ecosystem's conventions, and `startCommand`, `buildCommand` and the other overrides cover the rest. Use **`dockerfile`** (`DockerfilePackaging`) when you need a specific base image, GPU drivers or a custom entrypoint. Use **`prebuilt-image`** (`PrebuiltImagePackaging`) when your CI pipeline already produces images, or for third-party software such as Redis, Nginx or Prometheus.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebService, BuildpackImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new WebService({
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 },
    packaging: new BuildpackImagePackaging({
      sourceDirectoryPath: './api'
    })
  });

  return { resources: { api } };
});
```


The `resources` property belongs to the web service, not to packaging. It sets CPU and memory for the container. See the [web service page](/resources/compute/web-service) for supported values and scaling options.

## Basic example

Install the CDK libraries in your project. Stacktape loads them from the `node_modules` next to the construct file:

```bash
npm install aws-cdk-lib constructs
```


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, AwsCdkConstruct } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const alerts = new AwsCdkConstruct({
    entryfilePath: './cdk/alert-topic.ts'
  });

  return {
    resources: { alerts }
  };
});
```


```typescript
// cdk/alert-topic.ts
import { Construct } from 'constructs';
import { CfnOutput } from 'aws-cdk-lib';
import * as sns from 'aws-cdk-lib/aws-sns';
import * as subscriptions from 'aws-cdk-lib/aws-sns-subscriptions';

export default class AlertTopic extends Construct {
  constructor(scope: Construct, id: string) {
    super(scope, id);
    const topic = new sns.Topic(this, 'Topic', { displayName: 'Application alerts' });
    topic.addSubscription(new subscriptions.EmailSubscription('ops@example.com'));
    new CfnOutput(this, 'TopicArn', { value: topic.topicArn });
  }
}
```

The file must export a class that extends `Construct`. Stacktape uses the default export; for a named export, set
`exportName` to the class name. A class that extends `Stack` isn't accepted: move its resources into a `Construct`.
Stacktape creates the class twice during a deploy (once to check it), so keep the constructor free of side effects such
as API calls.

The construct's resources get logical IDs that start with the resource name in PascalCase (`AlertsTopic…`), and its
`CfnOutput`s become outputs of the stack.

## Pass values in

`constructProperties` is passed to the constructor as its third argument:

```typescript
new AwsCdkConstruct({
  entryfilePath: './cdk/data-access.ts',
  constructProperties: {
    retentionDays: 14,
    dataBucketArn: $ResourceParam('dataBucket', 'arn')
  }
})
```

```typescript
// cdk/data-access.ts
import { Construct } from 'constructs';
import { Duration } from 'aws-cdk-lib';
import * as iam from 'aws-cdk-lib/aws-iam';
import * as sqs from 'aws-cdk-lib/aws-sqs';

export default class DataAccess extends Construct {
  constructor(scope: Construct, id: string, props: { retentionDays: number; dataBucketArn: string }) {
    super(scope, id);
    new sqs.Queue(this, 'Jobs', { retentionPeriod: Duration.days(props.retentionDays) });
    new iam.ManagedPolicy(this, 'ListData', {
      statements: [new iam.PolicyStatement({ actions: ['s3:ListBucket'], resources: [props.dataBucketArn] })]
    });
  }
}
```

Plain values arrive as they are. A directive such as `$ResourceParam` arrives as its text while the construct runs,
and Stacktape replaces it with the real reference in the finished template. So use it only as a whole property value,
as above. Code that parses or builds on it, such as `Bucket.fromBucketArn()` or `` `${arn}/*` ``, gets the directive
text instead of an ARN.

## Basic example


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  CustomResourceDefinition,
  CustomResourceInstance,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging,
  $Secret
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const webhookProvider = new CustomResourceDefinition({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/webhook-provider.ts'
    }),
    timeout: 60,
    environment: { EXTERNAL_API_KEY: $Secret('external-api-key') }
  });

  const apiWebhook = new CustomResourceInstance({
    definitionName: 'webhookProvider',
    resourceProperties: {
      callbackUrl: 'https://myapp.example.com/webhook'
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { webhookProvider, apiWebhook }
  };
});
```


The handler receives `resourceProperties` as `event.ResourceProperties`. The default `timeout` is 10 seconds, which is
short for calls to outside APIs; the maximum is 900.

## The handler

CloudFormation calls the function with a `RequestType` of `Create`, `Update` or `Delete`, then waits until the function
PUTs a result to the `ResponseURL` in the event. The function must report `FAILED` when the outside API refuses the
request; `fetch` doesn't throw on a 4xx or 5xx response, so check the status yourself:

```typescript
type Event = {
  RequestType: 'Create' | 'Update' | 'Delete';
  ResourceProperties: { callbackUrl: string };
  PhysicalResourceId?: string;
  ResponseURL: string;
  StackId: string;
  RequestId: string;
  LogicalResourceId: string;
};

const api = (path: string, init: RequestInit = {}) =>
  fetch(`https://api.example.com${path}`, {
    ...init,
    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.EXTERNAL_API_KEY}`, 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }
  });

export default async (event: Event) => {
  let physicalResourceId = event.PhysicalResourceId;
  let data: Record<string, string> = {};
  let status: 'SUCCESS' | 'FAILED' = 'SUCCESS';
  let reason = '';

  try {
    if (event.RequestType === 'Create') {
      const response = await api('/webhooks', {
        method: 'POST',
        body: JSON.stringify({ url: event.ResourceProperties.callbackUrl })
      });
      if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`Create failed: ${response.status} ${await response.text()}`);
      const webhook = await response.json();
      if (!webhook.id) throw new Error('Create response has no webhook id');
      physicalResourceId = webhook.id;
      data = { webhookId: webhook.id };
    }

    if (event.RequestType === 'Update') {
      const response = await api(`/webhooks/${physicalResourceId}`, {
        method: 'PUT',
        body: JSON.stringify({ url: event.ResourceProperties.callbackUrl })
      });
      if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`Update failed: ${response.status} ${await response.text()}`);
      data = { webhookId: physicalResourceId! };
    }

    if (event.RequestType === 'Delete' && physicalResourceId?.startsWith('wh_')) {
      const response = await api(`/webhooks/${physicalResourceId}`, { method: 'DELETE' });
      // Already gone counts as deleted.
      if (!response.ok && response.status !== 404) {
        throw new Error(`Delete failed: ${response.status} ${await response.text()}`);
      }
    }
  } catch (error) {
    status = 'FAILED';
    reason = error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error);
  }

  const result = await fetch(event.ResponseURL, {
    method: 'PUT',
    body: JSON.stringify({
      Status: status,
      Reason: reason,
      // A failed Create has no id yet; reuse the request ID so CloudFormation gets a value.
      PhysicalResourceId: physicalResourceId ?? event.RequestId,
      StackId: event.StackId,
      RequestId: event.RequestId,
      LogicalResourceId: event.LogicalResourceId,
      Data: data
    })
  });
  if (!result.ok) throw new Error(`Could not send the response to CloudFormation: ${result.status}`);
};
```

`wh_` stands for the outside service's ID format. The check skips the API call when a failed Create never got a real
ID, for example during the rollback that follows it.

Rules that keep a stack out of trouble:

- **Always respond.** Without a response, CloudFormation waits about an hour and then fails the deploy.
- **Keep the physical ID stable on Update.** A different ID means "replaced": CloudFormation then sends a `Delete` for
  the old ID.
- **Make Delete tolerant.** It also runs during rollbacks, sometimes for something that was never created. A missing
  resource is a success. A Delete that fails leaves the resource in `DELETE_FAILED`.
- **Keep the response under 4 KB.** That limit covers the whole body, including `Data`.

| Event field | Contains |
|---|---|
| `RequestType` | `Create`, `Update` or `Delete` |
| `ResourceProperties` | The instance's `resourceProperties` |
| `OldResourceProperties` | The previous properties, on `Update` only |
| `PhysicalResourceId` | The ID your function returned last time; absent on `Create` |
| `ResponseURL` | Pre-signed URL for the result |
| `StackId`, `RequestId`, `LogicalResourceId` | Copy them into the result |

## Instances

Each instance is its own resource with its own lifecycle. Changing an instance's `resourceProperties` sends an `Update`
with the old and new properties; removing the instance from the config sends a `Delete`.

```typescript
const apiDns = new CustomResourceInstance({
  definitionName: 'dnsProvider',
  resourceProperties: { name: 'api.example.com', target: '203.0.113.10' }
});

const appDns = new CustomResourceInstance({
  definitionName: 'dnsProvider',
  resourceProperties: { name: 'app.example.com', target: '203.0.113.20' }
});
```

## Basic example

This script runs the database migrations after every deploy:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  DeploymentScript,
  RelationalDatabase,
  RdsEnginePostgres,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging,
  $Secret
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const mainDatabase = new RelationalDatabase({
    engine: new RdsEnginePostgres({ version: '16', primaryInstance: { instanceSize: 'db.t4g.micro' } }),
    credentials: { masterUserPassword: $Secret('main-database-password') }
  });

  const migrate = new DeploymentScript({
    trigger: 'after:deploy',
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './scripts/migrate.ts' }),
    connectTo: ['mainDatabase'],
    joinDefaultVpc: true,
    timeout: 300
  });

  return {
    resources: { mainDatabase, migrate }
  };
});
```


Create the password secret once per region before the first deploy:

```bash
stacktape secret set main-database-password --region eu-west-1
```

`connectTo` injects `STP_MAIN_DATABASE_CONNECTION_STRING`; `joinDefaultVpc` puts the script in the VPC so it can reach
the database. Set `timeout` explicitly: the default is 10 seconds, and the maximum is 900.

The script must bring its migration code with it. The bundle contains only what `migrate.ts` imports, so a CLI such as
`prisma migrate` or a folder of `.sql` files isn't there unless you add it. This script keeps the migrations as imported
TypeScript and runs each one in a transaction with the `postgres` package, recording what ran:

```typescript
// scripts/migrations.ts
export const migrations = [
  { id: '001_create_users', sql: 'CREATE TABLE users (id uuid PRIMARY KEY, email text NOT NULL UNIQUE)' },
  { id: '002_add_user_name', sql: 'ALTER TABLE users ADD COLUMN name text' }
];
```

```typescript
// scripts/migrate.ts
import postgres from 'postgres';
import { migrations } from './migrations';

export default async () => {
  const sql = postgres(process.env.STP_MAIN_DATABASE_CONNECTION_STRING!, { ssl: 'require' });
  try {
    await sql`CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS schema_migrations (id text PRIMARY KEY, applied_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now())`;
    for (const migration of migrations) {
      await sql.begin(async (tx) => {
        const [done] = await tx`SELECT 1 FROM schema_migrations WHERE id = ${migration.id}`;
        if (done) return;
        await tx.unsafe(migration.sql);
        await tx`INSERT INTO schema_migrations (id) VALUES (${migration.id})`;
      });
    }
  } finally {
    await sql.end();
  }
};
```

If a migration fails, its transaction rolls back and the earlier ones stay recorded, so the next run continues where
this one stopped. A migration tool that runs in-process, such as Drizzle's or Knex's migrator, works the same way; add
its migration files to the bundle with `includeFiles` on the packaging.

## Environment and parameters

- `environment` sets environment variables. Put secrets here with [`$Secret()`](/configuration/directives).
- `parameters` is passed to the handler as its event. Don't put secrets in it: the values are stored in the stack
  template.

```typescript
new DeploymentScript({
  trigger: 'after:deploy',
  packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './scripts/seed.ts' }),
  environment: { ADMIN_EMAIL: $Secret('admin-email') },
  parameters: { seedCount: 100 },
  timeout: 60
})
```

```typescript
export default async (event: { seedCount: number }) => {
  console.info(`Seeding ${event.seedCount} rows for ${process.env.ADMIN_EMAIL}`);
};
```

Script logs are in CloudWatch like any function's. A deployment script runs on Lambda pricing; a 10-second migration at
512 MB costs a fraction of a cent.

## Basic example

`cloudformationResources` sits next to `resources`. Each key is the resource's logical ID in the template, and each
value is ordinary CloudFormation with a `Type` and usually `Properties`:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging, $CfResourceParam } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/api.ts' }),
    environment: {
      FEATURE_FLAGS_PARAM: $CfResourceParam('FeatureFlags', 'Name')
    },
    iamRoleStatements: [
      {
        Effect: 'Allow',
        Action: ['ssm:GetParameter'],
        Resource: ['arn:aws:ssm:*:*:parameter/my-app/feature-flags']
      }
    ]
  });

  return {
    resources: { api },
    cloudformationResources: {
      FeatureFlags: {
        Type: 'AWS::SSM::Parameter',
        Properties: {
          Name: '/my-app/feature-flags',
          Type: 'String',
          Value: '{"newCheckout":false}'
        }
      }
    }
  };
});
```


The function reads the parameter's name from `FEATURE_FLAGS_PARAM`. Raw resources don't work with `connectTo`, so the
function gets access through `iamRoleStatements`, which every compute resource accepts.

## Reference resources both ways

- From a Stacktape resource to a raw one: `$CfResourceParam('<logical ID>', '<attribute>')`, where the attribute is one
  that `Fn::GetAtt` returns, or `Ref`.
- From a raw resource to a Stacktape one: `$ResourceParam('<resource name>', '<parameter>')` with a
  [referenceable parameter](/configuration/referenceable-parameters) such as `arn` or `url`.
- Between raw resources: CloudFormation's own `{ Ref: 'LogicalId' }` and `{ 'Fn::GetAtt': ['LogicalId', 'Arn'] }`.

This subscribes an SQS queue from the stack to a raw SNS topic:

```typescript
cloudformationResources: {
  DeployAlerts: {
    Type: 'AWS::SNS::Topic'
  },
  DeployAlertsToQueue: {
    Type: 'AWS::SNS::Subscription',
    Properties: {
      TopicArn: { Ref: 'DeployAlerts' },
      Protocol: 'sqs',
      Endpoint: $ResourceParam('alertQueue', 'arn')
    }
  }
}
```

The queue also needs a policy that lets SNS send to it; the [SNS topic](/resources/messaging/sns-topic) page shows one.

In TypeScript, intrinsic functions are objects: `{ Ref: 'Id' }`, `{ 'Fn::GetAtt': ['Id', 'Arn'] }`,
`{ 'Fn::Sub': '…' }`, `{ 'Fn::Join': [',', […]] }`.

## Resource attributes

`DependsOn`, `DeletionPolicy`, `UpdateReplacePolicy`, `CreationPolicy`, `UpdatePolicy`, `Metadata` and `Condition`
pass through to the template. `DeletionPolicy: 'Retain'` keeps a resource, such as a log group with audit data, when
you delete the stack:

```typescript
cloudformationResources: {
  AuditLogGroup: {
    Type: 'AWS::Logs::LogGroup',
    DeletionPolicy: 'Retain',
    Properties: { LogGroupName: '/my-app/audit', RetentionInDays: 365 }
  }
}
```

Without it, deleting the stack deletes every raw resource with it.

## Basic example


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, AgentCoreBrowser, AgentCoreRuntime, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const browser = new AgentCoreBrowser({});

  const researchAgent = new AgentCoreRuntime({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './agent/server.ts' }),
    useBrowser: 'browser'
  });

  return {
    resources: { browser, researchAgent }
  };
});
```


`useBrowser` gives the runtime access to the browser and injects its ID as `STP_AGENTCORE_BROWSER_ID`. The agent starts a
browser session with that ID through the AgentCore SDK and drives it, for example with Playwright over the session's
automation endpoint:

```typescript
const browserId = process.env.STP_AGENTCORE_BROWSER_ID!;
```

The browser runs in AgentCore's public network mode, so it can open any site on the internet.

## Record sessions

`recording` saves browser sessions to an S3 bucket so you can replay what the agent did:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, AgentCoreBrowser, Bucket, $ResourceParam } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const recordings = new Bucket({});
  const browser = new AgentCoreBrowser({
    recording: {
      enabled: true,
      bucketName: $ResourceParam('recordings', 'name'),
      prefix: 'sessions/'
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { recordings, browser }
  };
});
```


Stacktape lets the browser write to that bucket and prefix. Recordings can contain whatever the agent saw, including
personal data, so set access and a lifecycle rule on the bucket to match your retention rules. For a bucket outside the
stack, pass its name as a string.

## Basic example


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, AgentCoreCodeInterpreter, AgentCoreRuntime, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const codeInterpreter = new AgentCoreCodeInterpreter({});

  const analyst = new AgentCoreRuntime({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './agent/server.ts' }),
    useCodeInterpreter: 'codeInterpreter'
  });

  return {
    resources: { codeInterpreter, analyst }
  };
});
```


`useCodeInterpreter` gives the runtime access to the interpreter and injects its ID as
`STP_AGENTCORE_CODE_INTERPRETER_ID`. The agent starts an interpreter session with that ID through the AgentCore SDK
and sends it code to run:

```typescript
const codeInterpreterId = process.env.STP_AGENTCORE_CODE_INTERPRETER_ID!;
```

The interpreter runs Python, JavaScript and TypeScript. The
[AgentCore documentation](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock-agentcore/latest/devguide/) lists the preinstalled
libraries and session limits.

## Basic example


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  AgentCoreGateway,
  AgentCoreRuntime,
  LambdaFunction,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging,
  JsBundleImagePackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const orderLookup = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/order-lookup.ts' })
  });

  const tools = new AgentCoreGateway({
    authorizer: {
      type: 'jwt',
      properties: {
        discoveryUrl:
          'https://cognito-idp.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/us-east-1_ABC123/.well-known/openid-configuration',
        allowedAudience: ['my-app-client-id']
      }
    },
    tools: [
      {
        name: 'orders',
        function: 'orderLookup',
        toolSchema: [
          {
            name: 'lookupOrder',
            description: 'Find an order by its ID',
            inputSchema: {
              type: 'object',
              properties: { orderId: { type: 'string', description: 'The order ID' } },
              required: ['orderId']
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  });

  const agent = new AgentCoreRuntime({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './agent/server.ts' }),
    useGateway: 'tools'
  });

  return {
    resources: { orderLookup, tools, agent }
  };
});
```


`function` names a Lambda function in the stack; for a function elsewhere, use `lambdaArn`. Stacktape lets the gateway
invoke it. `useGateway` injects the gateway's MCP endpoint into the runtime as `STP_AGENTCORE_GATEWAY_URL`; connect an MCP
client to that URL.

## Write a tool function

The gateway invokes the function with the tool's arguments as the event. Which operation was called is in the Lambda
client context under `custom.bedrockAgentCoreToolName`, as `<target>___<operation>`; take the part after the last
`___`. The [AgentCore developer guide](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock-agentcore/latest/devguide/) describes the
Lambda target format.

```typescript
import type { Context } from 'aws-lambda';

export default async (event: { orderId: string }, context: Context) => {
  const custom = (context.clientContext as { custom?: Record<string, string> } | undefined)?.custom;
  const operation = (custom?.bedrockAgentCoreToolName ?? '').split('___').pop();
  if (operation === 'lookupOrder') {
    return { orderId: event.orderId, status: 'shipped' };
  }
  throw new Error(`Unknown operation ${operation}`);
};
```

A tool can list several operations in `toolSchema`, all served by the same function. Each operation needs a `name` and
an `inputSchema`; `description` and `outputSchema` are optional. Keep schemas to plain JSON Schema (`type`,
`properties`, `required`, `items`, `description`). Stacktape passes them to AWS, which rejects what it doesn't support.

## Basic example


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, AgentCoreMemory, AgentCoreRuntime, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const agentMemory = new AgentCoreMemory({});

  const agent = new AgentCoreRuntime({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './agent/server.ts' }),
    useMemory: 'agentMemory'
  });

  return {
    resources: { agentMemory, agent }
  };
});
```


`useMemory` gives the runtime access to the memory and injects its ID as `STP_AGENTCORE_MEMORY_ID`. Pass it to the
AgentCore SDK when you save and read events:

```typescript
const memoryId = process.env.STP_AGENTCORE_MEMORY_ID!;
```

Another workload, such as a Lambda function, can use the memory through its `id` or `arn`
[parameter](/configuration/referenceable-parameters) with `$ResourceParam()`, plus `iamRoleStatements` for the
`bedrock-agentcore` actions it calls.

## Basic example


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, AgentCoreRuntime, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const agent = new AgentCoreRuntime({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
      entryfilePath: './agent/server.ts'
    }),
    protocol: 'HTTP'
  });

  return {
    resources: { agent }
  };
});
```


With the `HTTP` protocol, AgentCore expects the container to listen on `0.0.0.0:8080` and answer two routes:

- `POST /invocations` with the request payload. Return JSON, or stream with server-sent events.
- `GET /ping`, the health check. Return `{"status":"Healthy"}`.

```typescript
// agent/server.ts
import { createServer } from 'node:http';

createServer(async (req, res) => {
  if (req.method === 'GET' && req.url === '/ping') {
    res.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }).end(JSON.stringify({ status: 'Healthy' }));
    return;
  }
  if (req.method === 'POST' && req.url === '/invocations') {
    let body = '';
    for await (const chunk of req) body += chunk;
    const { prompt } = JSON.parse(body || '{}');
    // Call your model here.
    res.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }).end(JSON.stringify({ answer: `You asked: ${prompt}` }));
    return;
  }
  res.writeHead(404).end();
}).listen(8080, '0.0.0.0');
```

AgentCore runs `linux/arm64` images. Stacktape builds the image for arm64; a `prebuilt-image` must be arm64 too. The
[AWS HTTP contract](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock-agentcore/latest/devguide/runtime-http-protocol-contract.html)
has the details, including WebSocket support.

After `stacktape deploy`, get the runtime ARN and invoke it. The session ID groups requests of one conversation and must
be at least 33 characters:

```bash
stacktape param get agent arn --stage dev --region eu-west-1
```

```bash
aws bedrock-agentcore invoke-agent-runtime --region eu-west-1 \
  --agent-runtime-arn <runtime-arn> --qualifier default \
  --runtime-session-id "session-$(uuidgen)" \
  --payload '{"prompt":"Hello"}' --cli-binary-format raw-in-base64-out response.json
```

`response.json` then holds the agent's answer.

## Tools: memory, gateway, browser and code interpreter

Each tool is its own resource. Attach it by name, and Stacktape grants the runtime access and injects its identifier:

| Property | Resource | Injected variable |
|----------|----------|-------------------|
| `useMemory` | [AgentCore Memory](/resources/ai/agentcore-memory) | `STP_AGENTCORE_MEMORY_ID` |
| `useGateway` | [AgentCore Gateway](/resources/ai/agentcore-gateway) | `STP_AGENTCORE_GATEWAY_URL` |
| `useBrowser` | [AgentCore Browser](/resources/ai/agentcore-browser) | `STP_AGENTCORE_BROWSER_ID` |
| `useCodeInterpreter` | [AgentCore Code Interpreter](/resources/ai/agentcore-code-interpreter) | `STP_AGENTCORE_CODE_INTERPRETER_ID` |


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  AgentCoreRuntime,
  AgentCoreMemory,
  AgentCoreBrowser,
  JsBundleImagePackaging
} from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const agentMemory = new AgentCoreMemory({});
  const browser = new AgentCoreBrowser({});

  const agent = new AgentCoreRuntime({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './agent/server.ts' }),
    protocol: 'HTTP',
    useMemory: 'agentMemory',
    useBrowser: 'browser'
  });

  return {
    resources: { agentMemory, browser, agent }
  };
});
```


The agent reads `process.env.STP_AGENTCORE_MEMORY_ID` and `process.env.STP_AGENTCORE_BROWSER_ID` and passes them to the
AgentCore SDK. Attach only what the agent uses: a browser or code interpreter adds its own sessions and charges.

## Basic example

A TypeScript job that runs every night at 02:00 UTC:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, BatchJob, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const nightlyReport = new BatchJob({
    container: {
      packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
        entryfilePath: './src/jobs/nightly-report.ts'
      }),
      environment: { REPORT_BUCKET_PREFIX: 'reports/nightly' }
    },
    resources: {
      cpu: 2,
      memory: 4096
    },
    timeout: 3600,
    events: [
      {
        type: 'schedule',
        properties: {
          scheduleRate: 'cron(0 2 * * ? *)'
        }
      }
    ]
  });

  return { resources: { nightlyReport } };
});
```


`js-bundle` builds the image from `./src/jobs/nightly-report.ts`, `resources` asks for 2 vCPU and 4096 MB, and
`timeout` stops a run after one hour. Schedules use AWS cron or `rate(2 hours)` syntax in UTC; see
[Schedule triggers](/resources/triggers/schedule-triggers). Each run's output appears in
`stacktape logs nightlyReport --stage dev`.

## Compute resources

`cpu` is a whole number of vCPUs and `memory` is in MB; AWS Batch picks instances that fit. Request a little less than a
power of two, for example `7680` instead of `8192` MB, because the instance keeps some memory for itself. Add `gpu` only
for GPU work such as training or rendering.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, BatchJob, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const trainingJob = new BatchJob({
    container: {
      packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
        entryfilePath: './src/jobs/train-model.ts'
      })
    },
    resources: {
      cpu: 8,
      memory: 30720,
      gpu: 1
    }
  });

  return { resources: { trainingJob } };
});
```


Jobs run on x86 instances with the Amazon ECS-optimized Amazon Linux 2023 image; GPU jobs use its NVIDIA variant on
`p4d` and `g5` instances. The instances require IMDSv2 and use cgroup v2, so software in your image that reads instance
metadata with IMDSv1, or memory limits only from cgroup v1 (such as Java before 8u372), needs a current version.

### Reading trigger data

The job is a process, not a handler: it gets the trigger event as JSON in `STP_TRIGGER_EVENT_DATA`, and the attempt
number in `STP_CURRENT_ATTEMPT` and `STP_MAXIMUM_ATTEMPTS`.

```typescript
const event = JSON.parse(process.env.STP_TRIGGER_EVENT_DATA ?? '{}');
const attempt = Number(process.env.STP_CURRENT_ATTEMPT ?? '1');
const maximumAttempts = Number(process.env.STP_MAXIMUM_ATTEMPTS ?? '1');
await processJob(event, { attempt, maximumAttempts });
```

## Retries and timeout

`timeout` (in seconds) stops a job that runs too long. `retryConfig` runs failed or timed-out jobs again.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, BatchJob, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const importJob = new BatchJob({
    container: {
      packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
        entryfilePath: './src/jobs/import-catalog.ts'
      })
    },
    resources: { cpu: 2, memory: 4096 },
    timeout: 7200,
    retryConfig: {
      attempts: 3,
      retryIntervalSeconds: 30,
      retryIntervalMultiplier: 2
    }
  });

  return { resources: { importJob } };
});
```


`attempts` counts the first run and defaults to 1. The wait before each retry is `retryIntervalSeconds` × the number of
failed attempts × `retryIntervalMultiplier`: with 3 attempts, interval 30 and multiplier 2, the job waits 60 seconds
before attempt 2 and 120 seconds before attempt 3.


> **Warning:** A retry runs the whole job again. Make writes, imports and notifications safe to repeat before you allow more
than one attempt.

## Spot capacity

`useSpotInstances: true` runs jobs on spare EC2 capacity for up to 90% less. AWS can take the instance back: the
container gets `SIGTERM` and 120 seconds to stop. Use Spot for restartable imports, image processing or training with
checkpoints, together with `retryConfig`, and keep on-demand capacity for deadlines and work that can't be repeated.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, BatchJob, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const thumbnailJob = new BatchJob({
    container: {
      packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
        entryfilePath: './src/jobs/thumbnails.ts'
      })
    },
    resources: { cpu: 1, memory: 2048 },
    useSpotInstances: true,
    retryConfig: {
      attempts: 5,
      retryIntervalSeconds: 60
    }
  });

  return { resources: { thumbnailJob } };
});
```

## Connecting to resources

`connectTo` grants IAM permissions, opens the network path to databases and Redis, and injects
`STP_<RESOURCE>_<PARAM>` variables into the job.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  BatchJob,
  Bucket,
  RelationalDatabase,
  RdsEnginePostgres,
  $Secret,
  JsBundleImagePackaging
} from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const reports = new Bucket({});
  const mainDatabase = new RelationalDatabase({
    engine: new RdsEnginePostgres({ version: '16', primaryInstance: { instanceSize: 'db.t4g.micro' } }),
    credentials: { masterUserPassword: $Secret('database', 'password') }
  });

  const exportJob = new BatchJob({
    connectTo: [mainDatabase, reports],
    container: {
      packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
        entryfilePath: './src/jobs/export.ts'
      })
    },
    resources: { cpu: 1, memory: 2048 }
  });

  return { resources: { reports, mainDatabase, exportJob } };
});
```


This job gets `STP_MAIN_DATABASE_CONNECTION_STRING`, `STP_MAIN_DATABASE_HOST`, `STP_MAIN_DATABASE_PORT` and the other
database variables, plus `STP_REPORTS_NAME` and `STP_REPORTS_ARN`. For other AWS permissions, use `iamRoleStatements`.

## Logging

The job's output goes to CloudWatch Logs, kept for 90 days by default (`logging.retentionDays`). Log the input ID and
attempt number so you can follow one run across retries.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, BatchJob, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const cleanupJob = new BatchJob({
    container: {
      packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
        entryfilePath: './src/jobs/cleanup.ts'
      })
    },
    resources: { cpu: 1, memory: 2048 },
    logging: {
      retentionDays: 30
    }
  });

  return { resources: { cleanupJob } };
});
```

## Basic example

`appDirectory` points to your `convex/` directory, the one with `schema.ts` and your functions. It's the only required
property.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Convex } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myConvex = new Convex({
    appDirectory: './convex',
    backend: {
      resources: {
        cpu: 0.5,
        memory: 1024
      }
    },
    dashboard: {
      resources: {
        cpu: 0.25,
        memory: 512
      }
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { myConvex }
  };
});
```


The deploy creates everything listed above, then runs `npx convex deploy` against `appDirectory` to push your functions.
Stacktape generates the admin key, stores it in AWS Secrets Manager and passes it to that command.

Without custom domains, the backend is served on a `*.stacktape-app.com` domain, on ports 3210 (client API), 3211
(HTTP actions) and 6791 (dashboard). That domain comes from the [Stacktape Console](/getting-started/add-the-console).
Without the Console connected, set [custom domains](#custom-domains) for `cloud` and `site`, and for `dashboard` unless
you turn the dashboard off, or the deploy stops and asks for them.

## Custom domains

Convex serves three origins:

| Origin | Serves | Example |
|---|---|---|
| `cloud` | The API and WebSocket endpoint that `convex-js` clients connect to | `api.myapp.com` |
| `site` | Your `httpAction()` routes, such as webhooks and OAuth callbacks | `webhooks.myapp.com` |
| `dashboard` | The admin dashboard | `convex-admin.myapp.com` |

With `customDomains`, `cloud` and `site` are required, and `dashboard` is required while the dashboard is enabled.
[Set up the domain](/resources/networking/custom-domains#set-up-the-domain) with `stacktape domain add` before the
first deploy, so that issued certificates exist for these names.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Convex } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myConvex = new Convex({
    appDirectory: './convex',
    backend: {
      resources: {
        cpu: 2,
        memory: 4096
      }
    },
    dashboard: {
      resources: {
        cpu: 0.25,
        memory: 512
      },
      allowedIpRanges: ['203.0.113.0/24']
    },
    customDomains: {
      cloud: { domainName: 'api.myapp.com' },
      site: { domainName: 'webhooks.myapp.com' },
      dashboard: { domainName: 'convex-admin.myapp.com' }
    },
    deletionProtection: true,
    storage: {
      versioning: true
    },
    database: {
      automatedBackupRetentionDays: 7
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { myConvex }
  };
});
```


This production setup also turns on deletion protection, keeps old versions of stored files and allows the dashboard
only from one address range.

## Passing the Convex URL to other workloads

`$ResourceParam('myConvex', 'url')` is the `cloud` origin. Pass it to a frontend or another workload as an
environment variable for the `convex-js` client:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Convex, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myConvex = new Convex({
    appDirectory: './convex',
    backend: {
      resources: {
        cpu: 1,
        memory: 2048
      }
    },
    dashboard: {
      resources: {
        cpu: 0.25,
        memory: 512
      }
    }
  });

  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts'
    }),
    environment: {
      CONVEX_URL: "$ResourceParam('myConvex', 'url')"
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { myConvex, api }
  };
});
```

## Functions deployment

After the infrastructure is up, Stacktape runs `npx convex deploy --codegen disable --typecheck try` in the parent
directory of `appDirectory`, with `CONVEX_SELF_HOSTED_URL` and `CONVEX_SELF_HOSTED_ADMIN_KEY` set. `functionsDeployment`
changes the command (for example to use `pnpm`), the directory (`workingDirectory`), or turns the step off
(`enabled: false`) when your CI deploys functions itself.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Convex } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myConvex = new Convex({
    appDirectory: './convex',
    backend: {
      resources: { cpu: 1, memory: 2048 }
    },
    dashboard: {
      resources: { cpu: 0.25, memory: 512 }
    },
    functionsDeployment: {
      command: 'pnpm convex deploy --codegen disable'
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { myConvex }
  };
});
```

## Backend

The backend container runs the Convex server. All queries, mutations, actions and subscriptions go through it. Read
its logs with [`stacktape logs`](/cli/logs).

The open-source backend runs as a single process: two backends on one database would corrupt it. Stacktape runs
exactly one backend task, so you scale it up, not out. Sizes from Convex's self-hosting guidance:

| Use | CPU | Memory |
|---|---|---|
| Development, a few dozen users | 0.5 vCPU | 1024 MB (default) |
| Production, hundreds of subscribers | 1 vCPU | 2048 MB |
| Heavier production, vector search | 2 vCPU | 4096 MB |
| Upper end of one process | 4 vCPU | 8192 MB |


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Convex } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myConvex = new Convex({
    appDirectory: './convex',
    dashboard: {
      resources: { cpu: 0.25, memory: 512 }
    },
    backend: {
      resources: {
        cpu: 2,
        memory: 4096
      }
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { myConvex }
  };
});
```


For EC2 instead of Fargate, set `instanceTypes` (Graviton types such as `c6g.large` cost the least per vCPU).
`enableWarmPool: true` keeps a pre-initialized instance ready for faster replacement and works with one instance type.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Convex } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myConvex = new Convex({
    appDirectory: './convex',
    dashboard: {
      resources: { cpu: 0.25, memory: 512 }
    },
    backend: {
      resources: {
        instanceTypes: ['c6g.large'],
        enableWarmPool: true
      }
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { myConvex }
  };
});
```


`backend.image` overrides the backend image (by default a tested release of `ghcr.io/get-convex/convex-backend`).


> **Warning:** A new backend image can run data migrations. Try an upgrade on a staging stage before production.

## Dashboard

The dashboard is the Convex admin UI: data browser, logs, function runner, environment variables and snapshot
export and import. It runs as its own container (0.25 vCPU and 512 MB by default, about $9 a month).

It has no sign-in of its own; the admin key you paste is the only protection. Restrict it to your office or VPN
addresses with `allowedIpRanges`, especially on production stages:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Convex } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myConvex = new Convex({
    appDirectory: './convex',
    backend: {
      resources: { cpu: 1, memory: 2048 }
    },
    dashboard: {
      resources: { cpu: 0.25, memory: 512 },
      allowedIpRanges: ['203.0.113.0/24', '198.51.100.42/32']
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { myConvex }
  };
});
```


`dashboard.enabled: false` removes the container and saves its cost. You then manage data and environment variables
with the `npx convex` CLI. `dashboard.image` pins the dashboard image.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Convex } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myConvex = new Convex({
    appDirectory: './convex',
    backend: {
      resources: { cpu: 1, memory: 2048 }
    },
    dashboard: {
      enabled: false
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { myConvex }
  };
});
```

## Storage

Convex keeps modules, files, search indexes, exports and snapshot imports in five private, encrypted S3 buckets.
`storage` applies to all five: `versioning` keeps old versions of overwritten or deleted objects, and `lifecycleRules`
can expire old exports.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Convex } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myConvex = new Convex({
    appDirectory: './convex',
    backend: {
      resources: { cpu: 1, memory: 2048 }
    },
    dashboard: {
      resources: { cpu: 0.25, memory: 512 }
    },
    storage: {
      versioning: true
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { myConvex }
  };
});
```

## Dev mode

In [`stacktape dev`](/local-development/dev-mode-overview), Convex runs locally in Docker with SQLite and local file
storage. Pushing functions is faster over your own machine, and no AWS resources run for it. Set `dev.remote: true` to
use the deployed backend instead, for example to work with real data; the stage must be deployed first.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Convex } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myConvex = new Convex({
    appDirectory: './convex',
    backend: {
      resources: { cpu: 1, memory: 2048 }
    },
    dashboard: {
      resources: { cpu: 0.25, memory: 512 }
    },
    dev: {
      remote: true
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { myConvex }
  };
});
```

## Basic example

This edge function adds a security header to every response of a bucket's CDN:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Bucket, EdgeLambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const securityHeaders = new EdgeLambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/edge/security-headers.ts'
    })
  });

  const website = new Bucket({
    cdn: {
      enabled: true,
      edgeFunctions: {
        onResponse: 'securityHeaders'
      }
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { securityHeaders, website }
  };
});
```


The handler gets the CloudFront event and returns the changed response:

```ts
export const handler = async (event: any) => {
  const { response } = event.Records[0].cf;
  response.headers['x-frame-options'] = [{ key: 'X-Frame-Options', value: 'DENY' }];
  return response;
};
```

After the deploy, `curl -I` on the CDN URL of any file in the bucket shows `x-frame-options: DENY`. Stacktape deploys
edge functions to `us-east-1`, as CloudFront requires, whatever your stack's region.

## Runtime limits

`memory` defaults to 128 MB and `timeout` to 3 seconds. Viewer hooks (`onRequest`, `onResponse`) allow at most 128 MB
and 5 seconds; origin hooks allow up to 10,240 MB and 30 seconds. Raise them only for an origin hook that needs it.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, EdgeLambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const edgeRewrite = new EdgeLambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/edge-rewrite.ts',
      handlerFunction: 'handler'
    }),
    memory: 128,
    timeout: 3
  });

  return { resources: { edgeRewrite } };
});
```


`runtime` comes from the file extension. Edge functions run Node.js (`nodejs24.x`, `nodejs22.x`, `nodejs20.x`,
`nodejs18.x`) or Python (`python3.13` to `python3.8`).

## Packaging

Edge functions use Lambda packaging:

| Type | Use it for |
|---|---|
| [`js-bundle`](/packaging/function/js-bundle) | A JavaScript or TypeScript entry file (`handlerFunction` names the export) |
| [`buildpack`](/packaging/function/buildpack) | A Python entry file and its dependencies |
| [`custom-artifact`](/packaging/function/custom-artifact) | A package your own build produces (`packagePath`, `handler` as `file:function`) |


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, EdgeLambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const edgeRewrite = new EdgeLambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/edge-rewrite.ts',
      handlerFunction: 'handler'
    })
  });

  return { resources: { edgeRewrite } };
});
```


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, EdgeLambdaFunction, CustomArtifactLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const edgeRewrite = new EdgeLambdaFunction({
    packaging: new CustomArtifactLambdaPackaging({
      packagePath: './dist/edge-rewrite.zip',
      handler: 'index.js:handler'
    })
  });

  return { resources: { edgeRewrite } };
});
```

## Permissions

`connectTo` on an edge function grants IAM permissions only, for example to read a bucket. It injects no
variables, because edge functions can't have any. Add other AWS permissions with `iamRoleStatements`.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  Bucket,
  EdgeLambdaFunction,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const assets = new Bucket({});

  const edgeRewrite = new EdgeLambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/edge-rewrite.ts',
      handlerFunction: 'handler'
    }),
    connectTo: ['assets'],
    iamRoleStatements: [
      {
        Action: ['ses:SendEmail'],
        Resource: ['*']
      }
    ]
  });

  return { resources: { assets, edgeRewrite } };
});
```

## Basic example

This function answers `GET /users/{id}` through an [HTTP API Gateway](/resources/networking/http-api-gateway).
Stacktape bundles `./src/get-user.ts`, picks the Node.js runtime from the file extension and adds the permissions the
gateway needs to call the function.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  LambdaFunction,
  HttpApiGateway,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const apiGateway = new HttpApiGateway({});

  const getUser = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/get-user.ts'
    }),
    events: [
      {
        type: 'http-api-gateway',
        properties: {
          httpApiGatewayName: 'apiGateway',
          method: 'GET',
          path: '/users/{id}'
        }
      }
    ],
    memory: 1024,
    timeout: 30
  });

  return { resources: { apiGateway, getUser } };
});
```


`memory: 1024` gives the function 1,024 MB and a matching share of CPU. `timeout: 30` stops an invocation after 30
seconds (default 10, maximum 900).

The handler:

```ts
export async function handler(event: { pathParameters?: { id?: string } }) {
  return {
    statusCode: 200,
    body: JSON.stringify({ id: event.pathParameters?.id })
  };
}
```

The deploy prints the gateway URL. Call `https://<api-id>.execute-api.<region>.amazonaws.com/users/42` and you get
`{"id":"42"}` back. Read the function's logs with `stacktape logs getUser --stage dev`.

### JS bundle

`JsBundleLambdaPackaging` options such as `nodeVersion`, `outputModuleFormat`, `disableSourceMaps` and `minify` are direct properties next to `entryfilePath`. See [JS Bundle for Lambda](/packaging/function/js-bundle) for all options.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/api.ts',
      nodeVersion: 22,
      outputModuleFormat: 'esm'
    })
  });

  return { resources: { api } };
});
```


`nodeVersion` selects the Node.js major version for the build; keep it aligned with the function's `runtime`. `outputModuleFormat` switches between `'cjs'` (CommonJS) and `'esm'` (ES Modules, enables top-level `await`). Node.js 24 and later use ESM output automatically; earlier versions default to CommonJS. Some npm packages do not support ESM.

### Buildpack

`BuildpackLambdaPackaging` builds Python, Java, Go, Ruby, .NET and Rust functions. The function's `runtime` selects the language version. Language options are grouped under `python`, `java` and `dotnet`. See [Buildpack for Lambda](/packaging/function/buildpack) for all options.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, BuildpackLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const processor = new LambdaFunction({
    runtime: 'python3.13',
    packaging: new BuildpackLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/process.py',
      python: { packageManagerFile: './pyproject.toml' }
    })
  });

  return { resources: { processor } };
});
```

### Custom artifact

The `CustomArtifactLambdaPackaging` class takes a `packagePath` pointing to a pre-built deployment package. If the path points to a directory or a non-zip file, Stacktape zips it before upload. With `custom-artifact`, there is no source file for runtime auto-detection, so you must set `runtime` on the resource and `handler` in the packaging. The handler file must be at the artifact root; see [custom-artifact limitations](/packaging/function/custom-artifact#handler). If you use `architecture: 'arm64'`, pre-compile any native dependencies for that architecture yourself.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, CustomArtifactLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const processor = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new CustomArtifactLambdaPackaging({
      packagePath: './dist/processor.zip',
      handler: 'index.js:handler'
    }),
    runtime: 'nodejs22.x'
  });

  return { resources: { processor } };
});
```


`handler` follows the format `filepath:functionName` and tells Lambda which exported function to invoke.

## Triggers

A function receives work through its `events` list. Stacktape creates the event source and the permissions for each
entry.

| Trigger | `type` | Guide |
|---|---|---|
| HTTP API Gateway | `http-api-gateway` | [HTTP triggers](/resources/triggers/http-triggers) |
| Application Load Balancer | `application-load-balancer` | [HTTP triggers](/resources/triggers/http-triggers) |
| WebSocket API Gateway | `websocket-api-gateway` | [WebSocket API Gateway](/resources/networking/websocket-api-gateway) |
| AppSync GraphQL resolver | `appsync-api` | [AppSync API](/resources/networking/appsync-api) |
| Schedule | `schedule` | [Schedule triggers](/resources/triggers/schedule-triggers) |
| S3 | `s3` | [S3 events](/resources/triggers/s3-events) |
| SQS | `sqs` | [SQS events](/resources/triggers/sqs-events) |
| SNS | `sns` | [SNS events](/resources/triggers/sns-events) |
| DynamoDB stream | `dynamo-db-stream` | [DynamoDB streams](/resources/triggers/dynamodb-streams) |
| Kinesis stream | `kinesis-stream` | [Kinesis events](/resources/triggers/kinesis-events) |
| EventBridge | `event-bus` | [EventBridge events](/resources/triggers/event-bus-events) |
| CloudWatch Logs | `cloudwatch-log` | [CloudWatch logs](/resources/triggers/cloudwatch-logs) |
| CloudWatch alarm | `cloudwatch-alarm` | [Alarms as triggers](/resources/triggers/alarms-as-triggers) |
| Kafka topic | `kafka-topic` | [Kafka topics](/resources/triggers/kafka-topics) |

Queue and stream triggers (`sqs`, `kinesis-stream`, `dynamo-db-stream`, `kafka-topic`) deliver records in batches.
`batchSize` caps the records per invocation and `maxBatchWindowSeconds` (up to 300) waits to fill a batch. Defaults and
limits differ per source; each trigger guide lists them. When a handler throws, the whole batch is retried, including
the records that succeeded. Make handlers idempotent, or turn on `reportBatchItemFailures` and return the failed record
IDs in `batchItemFailures` so only those are retried.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  LambdaFunction,
  SqsQueue,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const jobs = new SqsQueue({});

  const worker = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/worker.ts' }),
    events: [
      {
        type: 'sqs',
        properties: {
          sqsQueueName: 'jobs',
          batchSize: 10,
          maxBatchWindowSeconds: 30
        }
      }
    ]
  });

  return { resources: { jobs, worker } };
});
```


A `schedule` trigger takes a rate (`rate(5 minutes)`) or a six-field AWS cron expression in UTC
(`cron(0 18 ? * MON-FRI *)`). See [Schedule triggers](/resources/triggers/schedule-triggers).

## Function URLs

A function URL gives one function its own HTTPS endpoint (`https://<id>.lambda-url.<region>.on.aws`) with no API
gateway. It suits a webhook receiver or an app that does its own routing, such as Hono or Express behind `handle()`.
Use an [HTTP API Gateway](/resources/networking/http-api-gateway) when several functions share one API or you want
routing by method and path.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const webhook = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/webhook.ts' }),
    url: {
      enabled: true,
      authMode: 'NONE',
      cors: {
        enabled: true,
        allowedOrigins: ['https://app.example.com'],
        allowedMethods: ['POST']
      }
    }
  });

  return { resources: { webhook } };
});
```


`authMode` is `'NONE'` by default, so anyone can call the URL. `'AWS_IAM'` accepts only signed requests from AWS
identities with invoke permission. `cors.enabled: true` alone allows every origin and method; `allowedOrigins` and
`allowedMethods` narrow it. These CORS settings override CORS headers your code returns.

`responseStreamEnabled: true` sends the response as your handler writes it, instead of buffering it, and lifts the 6 MB
limit of buffered responses. The handler must use Lambda's streaming handler pattern.

## Concurrency

- **`reservedConcurrency`** caps how many instances of this function run at once and reserves that many from your
  account's limit. Use it when a database or a third-party API can't take unlimited parallel calls. It costs nothing.
- **`provisionedConcurrency`** keeps instances initialized so requests skip the cold start. You pay for them while they
  wait. Use it only on latency-sensitive paths.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const checkout = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/checkout.ts' }),
    reservedConcurrency: 25,
    provisionedConcurrency: 3
  });

  return { resources: { checkout } };
});
```

### Connecting to resources

`connectTo` grants IAM permissions, opens the network path to databases and Redis, and injects
`STP_<RESOURCE>_<PARAM>` environment variables. [Connecting resources](/configuration/connecting-resources) lists the
variables per resource type. Put your own settings in `environment` and secrets in
[`$Secret()`](/configuration/secrets).


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  LambdaFunction,
  RelationalDatabase,
  RdsEnginePostgres,
  $Secret,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const database = new RelationalDatabase({
    engine: new RdsEnginePostgres({
      version: '16',
      primaryInstance: { instanceSize: 'db.t4g.micro' }
    }),
    credentials: { masterUserPassword: $Secret('database', 'password') }
  });

  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/api.ts' }),
    memory: 1024,
    timeout: 30,
    connectTo: [database],
    joinDefaultVpc: true,
    environment: { LOG_LEVEL: 'info' }
  });

  return { resources: { database, api } };
});
```


Here the function gets `STP_DATABASE_CONNECTION_STRING`, `STP_DATABASE_HOST`, `STP_DATABASE_PORT` and the other
database variables next to `LOG_LEVEL`.

For AWS services that `connectTo` doesn't cover, such as Rekognition, Bedrock or Textract, add `iamRoleStatements`:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const analyzer = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/analyzer.ts' }),
    iamRoleStatements: [
      {
        Effect: 'Allow',
        Action: ['rekognition:DetectLabels', 'rekognition:DetectText'],
        Resource: ['*']
      }
    ]
  });

  return { resources: { analyzer } };
});
```

## Storage

For files that must outlive an invocation or be shared between functions, mount an
[EFS file system](/resources/storage/efs-filesystem) with `volumeMounts`. Mounts need `joinDefaultVpc: true`, and
`mountPath` must start with `/mnt/`. The `s3files` mount type attaches an S3 Files access point you already manage. For
most data, an [S3 bucket](/resources/storage/s3-bucket) or a database is simpler than a file system.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  LambdaFunction,
  EfsFilesystem,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const sharedFiles = new EfsFilesystem({});

  const renderer = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/renderer.ts' }),
    joinDefaultVpc: true,
    storage: 2048,
    volumeMounts: [
      {
        type: 'efs',
        properties: {
          efsFilesystemName: 'sharedFiles',
          rootDirectory: '/renders',
          mountPath: '/mnt/renders'
        }
      }
    ]
  });

  return { resources: { sharedFiles, renderer } };
});
```


`rootDirectory` limits the mount to a directory of the file system (default `/`).

## CDN

`cdn.enabled: true` puts a CloudFront distribution in front of the function's URL. Cacheable public responses, such as
a product catalog, are then served from CloudFront locations near your users without invoking the function. Leave it
off for personalized responses. See [CDN](/resources/networking/cdn) for cache rules.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const catalog = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/catalog.ts' }),
    url: { enabled: true },
    cdn: {
      enabled: true
    }
  });

  return { resources: { catalog } };
});
```

## Deployments

`deployment.strategy` shifts traffic from the old version to the new one gradually, and rolls back if something fails.
Use it for production functions where a bad release would hurt users.

| Strategy | Behavior |
|---|---|
| `Canary10Percent5Minutes`, `…10Minutes`, `…15Minutes`, `…30Minutes` | 10% of traffic first, the rest after the wait |
| `Linear10PercentEvery1Minute`, `…2Minutes`, `…3Minutes`, `…10Minutes` | 10% more at each interval |
| `AllAtOnce` | Switch at once |


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/api.ts' }),
    deployment: {
      strategy: 'Canary10Percent5Minutes'
    }
  });

  return { resources: { api } };
});
```


`beforeAllowTrafficFunction` and `afterTrafficShiftFunction` name functions that check the release before and after the
shift. If one reports a failure, the deployment rolls back. See
[Gradual deployments](/deployment-and-lifecycle/gradual-deployments).

## Destinations

For asynchronous invocations (S3, SNS, EventBridge, or an `Event` invoke), `destinations` sends each result to an SQS
queue, SNS topic, event bus or another function: `onSuccess` for results, `onFailure` for errors after the retries.
They don't apply to synchronous callers like API gateways and load balancers. Stream triggers have their own
`onFailure` setting.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  LambdaFunction,
  SqsQueue,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const deadLetterQueue = new SqsQueue({});

  const processor = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/processor.ts' }),
    destinations: {
      onFailure: "$ResourceParam('deadLetterQueue', 'arn')"
    }
  });

  return { resources: { deadLetterQueue, processor } };
});
```


Both take an ARN. Use [`$ResourceParam()`](/configuration/directives) for a resource in the same config.

## Logs and alarms

Everything the function writes to stdout and stderr goes to CloudWatch Logs, kept for 90 days by default
(`logging.retentionDays`). Read it with [`stacktape logs`](/cli/logs). `logging.logForwarding` sends logs to an HTTP
endpoint, Datadog or Highlight.io; see [Log forwarding](/observability/log-forwarding).

Function alarms use the `lambda-error-rate` and `lambda-duration` triggers. See [Alarms](/observability/alarms) for
evaluation settings and notification channels.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/api.ts' }),
    logging: {
      retentionDays: 30
    },
    alarms: [
      {
        trigger: {
          type: 'lambda-error-rate',
          properties: { thresholdPercent: 5 }
        }
      },
      {
        trigger: {
          type: 'lambda-duration',
          properties: { thresholdMilliseconds: 5000 }
        }
      }
    ]
  });

  return { resources: { api } };
});
```


This alarm fires when more than 5% of invocations fail, or when an invocation runs longer than 5 seconds.

### HTTP API with DynamoDB

A Hono API that reads and writes a [DynamoDB table](/resources/databases/dynamodb). `connectTo` grants the function
read and write access to the table and injects `STP_USERS_TABLE_NAME`.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  LambdaFunction,
  HttpApiGateway,
  DynamoDbTable,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const apiGateway = new HttpApiGateway({});

  const usersTable = new DynamoDbTable({
    primaryKey: { partitionKey: { name: 'userId', type: 'string' } }
  });

  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/api.ts'
    }),
    events: [
      {
        type: 'http-api-gateway',
        properties: {
          httpApiGatewayName: 'apiGateway',
          method: '*',
          path: '/{proxy+}'
        }
      }
    ],
    connectTo: [usersTable],
    memory: 512,
    timeout: 30
  });

  return { resources: { apiGateway, usersTable, api } };
});
```


The handler:

```ts
import { Hono } from 'hono';
import { handle } from 'hono/aws-lambda';
import { DynamoDBClient, GetItemCommand, PutItemCommand } from '@aws-sdk/client-dynamodb';

const app = new Hono();
const client = new DynamoDBClient({});
const tableName = process.env.STP_USERS_TABLE_NAME!;

app.get('/users/:id', async (c) => {
  const result = await client.send(new GetItemCommand({
    TableName: tableName,
    Key: { userId: { S: c.req.param('id') } }
  }));
  return c.json(result.Item ?? {});
});

app.post('/users', async (c) => {
  const body = await c.req.json();
  await client.send(new PutItemCommand({
    TableName: tableName,
    Item: {
      userId: { S: body.id },
      name: { S: body.name },
      email: { S: body.email }
    }
  }));
  return c.json({ success: true }, 201);
});

export const handler = handle(app);
```

### Scheduled job

A function that runs every night at 03:00 UTC:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  LambdaFunction,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const cleanup = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/cleanup.ts'
    }),
    events: [
      {
        type: 'schedule',
        properties: { scheduleRate: 'cron(0 3 * * ? *)' }
      }
    ],
    timeout: 120
  });

  return { resources: { cleanup } };
});
```


To delete expired DynamoDB items, a [TTL attribute](/resources/databases/dynamodb) is cheaper than a scheduled scan.

## Basic example

An API server that takes HTTP traffic through an HTTP API Gateway, and a metrics exporter only the API can reach.
Both share 0.5 vCPU and 1024 MB, so size the task for their combined peak use.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  MultiContainerWorkload,
  HttpApiGateway,
  JsBundleImagePackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const apiGateway = new HttpApiGateway({});

  const workload = new MultiContainerWorkload({
    containers: [
      {
        name: 'server',
        packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
          entryfilePath: './src/server.ts'
        }),
        events: [
          {
            type: 'http-api-gateway',
            properties: {
              httpApiGatewayName: 'apiGateway',
              containerPort: 3000,
              method: '*',
              path: '/{proxy+}'
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        name: 'metrics',
        packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
          entryfilePath: './src/metrics.ts'
        }),
        events: [
          {
            type: 'workload-internal',
            properties: { containerPort: 9090 }
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    resources: {
      cpu: 0.5,
      memory: 1024
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { apiGateway, workload }
  };
});
```


The deploy prints the gateway URL, which reaches the `server` container. The `workload-internal` event opens the
`metrics` container's port to the other containers of the workload only.

## Containers

Every entry in `containers` has its own packaging, environment, health check and traffic. Two properties set the
lifecycle:

- **`essential`** (default `true`): when an essential container exits, ECS replaces the whole task. Set `false` for a
  container that is meant to exit, such as a migration.
- **`dependsOn`**: a container starts only when another reaches a condition: `START`, `COMPLETE` (exited),
  `SUCCESS` (exited with code 0) or `HEALTHY` (passed its health check).

Run migrations before the API starts:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  MultiContainerWorkload,
  HttpApiGateway,
  JsBundleImagePackaging,
  DockerfilePackaging
} from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const apiGateway = new HttpApiGateway({});

  const workload = new MultiContainerWorkload({
    containers: [
      {
        name: 'migrate',
        essential: false,
        packaging: new DockerfilePackaging({
          buildContextPath: './migrations',
          dockerfilePath: './Dockerfile'
        })
      },
      {
        name: 'server',
        dependsOn: [{ containerName: 'migrate', condition: 'SUCCESS' }],
        packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
          entryfilePath: './src/server.ts'
        }),
        events: [
          {
            type: 'http-api-gateway',
            properties: {
              httpApiGatewayName: 'apiGateway',
              containerPort: 3000,
              method: '*',
              path: '/{proxy+}'
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 }
  });

  return { resources: { apiGateway, workload } };
});
```


If the migration exits with a non-zero code, the API doesn't start, the task fails and ECS starts a new one. On
shutdown, each container gets `stopTimeout` seconds (default 30, at most 120) after `SIGTERM` before it is killed.

### HTTP API Gateway

`method` and `path` are required; `'*'` and `'/{proxy+}'` send everything to the container.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  MultiContainerWorkload,
  HttpApiGateway,
  JsBundleImagePackaging
} from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const apiGateway = new HttpApiGateway({});

  const workload = new MultiContainerWorkload({
    containers: [
      {
        name: 'server',
        packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/server.ts' }),
        events: [
          {
            type: 'http-api-gateway',
            properties: {
              httpApiGatewayName: 'apiGateway',
              containerPort: 3000,
              method: '*',
              path: '/{proxy+}'
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 }
  });

  return { resources: { apiGateway, workload } };
});
```

### Application Load Balancer

Use a load balancer for WebSockets, routing by header, query string or source IP, a
[firewall](/resources/security/web-application-firewall) or [gradual deployments](#gradual-deployments). Rules run
from the lowest `priority` up.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  MultiContainerWorkload,
  ApplicationLoadBalancer,
  JsBundleImagePackaging
} from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const alb = new ApplicationLoadBalancer({});

  const workload = new MultiContainerWorkload({
    containers: [
      {
        name: 'server',
        packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/server.ts' }),
        events: [
          {
            type: 'application-load-balancer',
            properties: {
              loadBalancerName: 'alb',
              containerPort: 3000,
              priority: 10,
              paths: ['/*']
            }
          }
        ],
        loadBalancerHealthCheck: {
          healthcheckPath: '/health',
          healthcheckInterval: 10,
          healthcheckTimeout: 5
        }
      }
    ],
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 }
  });

  return { resources: { alb, workload } };
});
```


`loadBalancerHealthCheck` sets the load balancer's check for this container: by default it requests `/` every 5
seconds and waits 4 seconds for an answer. Rules also take `methods`, `hosts`, `headers`, `queryParams` and
`sourceIps`.

### Network Load Balancer

For TCP or TLS protocols such as MQTT or game servers. `listenerPort` is the load balancer's port (here `8883`),
`containerPort` the port the container listens on.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  MultiContainerWorkload,
  NetworkLoadBalancer,
  JsBundleImagePackaging
} from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const nlb = new NetworkLoadBalancer({
    listeners: [{ protocol: 'TCP', port: 8883 }]
  });

  const workload = new MultiContainerWorkload({
    containers: [
      {
        name: 'broker',
        packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/broker.ts' }),
        events: [
          {
            type: 'network-load-balancer',
            properties: {
              loadBalancerName: 'nlb',
              listenerPort: 8883,
              containerPort: 1883
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 }
  });

  return { resources: { nlb, workload } };
});
```

### Internal and service-connect

`workload-internal` opens a port to the other containers of the workload, as the basic example shows.

`service-connect` lets other container workloads in the stack call this container by name, at
`protocol://alias:containerPort`. `alias` defaults to `<workloadName>-<containerName>`. `protocol` (`http`, `http2` or
`grpc`) adds protocol metrics such as the 5xx count.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, MultiContainerWorkload, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const workload = new MultiContainerWorkload({
    containers: [
      {
        name: 'api',
        packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/api.ts' }),
        events: [
          {
            type: 'service-connect',
            properties: {
              containerPort: 3000,
              alias: 'internal-api',
              protocol: 'http'
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 }
  });

  return { resources: { workload } };
});
```


Other services in the stack now reach it at `http://internal-api:3000`.

## Compute resources

CPU and memory belong to the task and all containers share them; you can't give one container its own limit.

**Fargate** (default): set `cpu` (0.25 to 16 vCPU) and a matching `memory`, as on the
[worker service](/resources/compute/worker-service#fargate). `architecture: 'arm64'` costs about 20% less.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, MultiContainerWorkload, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const workload = new MultiContainerWorkload({
    containers: [
      {
        name: 'server',
        packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/server.ts' })
      }
    ],
    resources: {
      cpu: 1,
      memory: 2048,
      architecture: 'arm64'
    }
  });

  return { resources: { workload } };
});
```


**EC2**: set `instanceTypes`; the first listed is preferred. Stacktape replaces the instances every Sunday to apply OS
patches. EC2 suits GPU work (`g4dn`, `p3`) and sustained heavy use. `enableWarmPool: true` keeps a pre-initialized
instance ready for sudden bursts; it bills at the full instance rate while it waits.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, MultiContainerWorkload, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const workload = new MultiContainerWorkload({
    containers: [
      {
        name: 'server',
        packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/server.ts' })
      }
    ],
    resources: {
      instanceTypes: ['c6g.large'],
      enableWarmPool: true
    }
  });

  return { resources: { workload } };
});
```


x86 Fargate in us-east-1, one task running all month ([Fargate pricing](https://aws.amazon.com/fargate/pricing/)):

| vCPU | Memory | Per month |
|---|---|---|
| 0.25 | 512–2048 MB | $9–$14 |
| 0.5 | 1024–4096 MB | $18–$27 |
| 1 | 2048–8192 MB | $36–$55 |
| 2 | 4096–16384 MB | $71–$110 |
| 4 | 8192–30720 MB | $142–$213 |

## Scaling

The workload scales by whole tasks: a new task starts all its containers. It runs `minInstances` tasks (default 1)
and adds more, up to `maxInstances` (default 1), when average CPU or memory passes its target (80% by default).


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, MultiContainerWorkload, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const workload = new MultiContainerWorkload({
    containers: [
      {
        name: 'server',
        packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/server.ts' })
      }
    ],
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 },
    scaling: {
      minInstances: 2,
      maxInstances: 10,
      scalingPolicy: {
        keepAvgCpuUtilizationUnder: 70,
        keepAvgMemoryUtilizationUnder: 80
      }
    }
  });

  return { resources: { workload } };
});
```

## Connecting to other resources

`connectTo` applies to every container in the workload: each gets the IAM permissions, the network path and the
`STP_<RESOURCE>_<PARAM>` variables. See [Connecting resources](/configuration/connecting-resources).


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  MultiContainerWorkload,
  RelationalDatabase,
  RdsEnginePostgres,
  Bucket,
  $Secret,
  JsBundleImagePackaging
} from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myDatabase = new RelationalDatabase({
    engine: new RdsEnginePostgres({ version: '16', primaryInstance: { instanceSize: 'db.t4g.micro' } }),
    credentials: { masterUserPassword: $Secret('database', 'password') }
  });
  const myBucket = new Bucket({});

  const workload = new MultiContainerWorkload({
    connectTo: [myDatabase, myBucket],
    containers: [
      {
        name: 'server',
        packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/server.ts' })
      }
    ],
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 }
  });

  return { resources: { myDatabase, myBucket, workload } };
});
```


Here every container gets `STP_MY_DATABASE_CONNECTION_STRING` and the other database and bucket variables.

## Health checks

Each container can have two checks:

- **`loadBalancerHealthCheck`**, for containers behind a load balancer: the load balancer stops sending traffic to
  unhealthy tasks. A Network Load Balancer checks the TCP port by default; `healthCheckProtocol: 'HTTP'` checks a path.
- **`internalHealthCheck`**: ECS runs a command inside the container and replaces the task when an essential container
  fails it `retries` times in a row.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, MultiContainerWorkload, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const workload = new MultiContainerWorkload({
    containers: [
      {
        name: 'server',
        packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/server.ts' }),
        internalHealthCheck: {
          healthCheckCommand: ['CMD-SHELL', 'curl -f http://localhost:3000/health || exit 1'],
          intervalSeconds: 30,
          timeoutSeconds: 5,
          retries: 3,
          startPeriodSeconds: 60
        }
      }
    ],
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 }
  });

  return { resources: { workload } };
});
```


Defaults: `intervalSeconds` 30, `timeoutSeconds` 5, `retries` 3. `startPeriodSeconds` ignores failures while the
container starts.

## EFS storage

Containers mount an [EFS file system](/resources/storage/efs-filesystem) for files that outlive the task or that
containers share, such as model weights. EFS bills for storage and for reads and writes on top of the task.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  MultiContainerWorkload,
  EfsFilesystem,
  JsBundleImagePackaging
} from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const uploads = new EfsFilesystem({});

  const workload = new MultiContainerWorkload({
    containers: [
      {
        name: 'server',
        packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/server.ts' }),
        volumeMounts: [
          {
            type: 'efs',
            properties: {
              efsFilesystemName: 'uploads',
              mountPath: '/data/uploads',
              rootDirectory: '/uploads'
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 }
  });

  return { resources: { uploads, workload } };
});
```


`rootDirectory` (default `/`) mounts a directory of the file system.

## Gradual deployments

A gradual deployment shifts traffic to the new version step by step and rolls back on failure. It needs a container
with an `application-load-balancer` event. Strategies: `Canary10Percent5Minutes`, `Canary10Percent15Minutes`,
`Linear10PercentEvery1Minutes`, `Linear10PercentEvery3Minutes` and `AllAtOnce`. Each one except `AllAtOnce` makes the
deploy take longer by its wait time.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  MultiContainerWorkload,
  ApplicationLoadBalancer,
  JsBundleImagePackaging
} from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const alb = new ApplicationLoadBalancer({});

  const workload = new MultiContainerWorkload({
    containers: [
      {
        name: 'server',
        packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/server.ts' }),
        events: [
          {
            type: 'application-load-balancer',
            properties: {
              loadBalancerName: 'alb',
              containerPort: 3000,
              priority: 10,
              paths: ['/*']
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 },
    deployment: {
      strategy: 'Canary10Percent5Minutes'
    }
  });

  return { resources: { alb, workload } };
});
```


`beforeAllowTrafficFunction` and `afterTrafficShiftFunction` run checks before and after the shift. See
[Gradual deployments](/deployment-and-lifecycle/gradual-deployments).

## Basic example


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, PrivateService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const internalApi = new PrivateService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/internal-api.ts'
    }),
    resources: {
      cpu: 0.25,
      memory: 512
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { internalApi }
  };
});
```


The service listens on port 3000 unless you set `port`; Stacktape passes the port to your code as the `PORT` variable.
Resources that call it list it in `connectTo` and get its address; see
[Connecting to other resources](#connecting-to-other-resources).

### Fargate

Set `cpu` (0.25 to 16 vCPU) and a `memory` that fits it; the [configuration reference](#configuration-reference)
lists the combinations. `architecture: 'arm64'` runs on Graviton, about 20% cheaper than the default `x86_64`.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, PrivateService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const internalApi = new PrivateService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/internal-api.ts'
    }),
    resources: {
      cpu: 1,
      memory: 2048,
      architecture: 'arm64'
    }
  });

  return { resources: { internalApi } };
});
```

### EC2

Set `instanceTypes` instead of `cpu` and `memory`, for example `t3.medium` or the Graviton `c6g.large`. Start with
Fargate and switch only for a concrete cost or capability reason.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, PrivateService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const internalApi = new PrivateService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/internal-api.ts'
    }),
    resources: {
      instanceTypes: ['t3.medium']
    }
  });

  return { resources: { internalApi } };
});
```

## Scaling

The service runs `minInstances` containers at all times and adds more, up to `maxInstances`, when average CPU or
memory use passes its target. Requests are spread across all running containers.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, PrivateService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const internalApi = new PrivateService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/internal-api.ts'
    }),
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 },
    scaling: {
      minInstances: 2,
      maxInstances: 10,
      scalingPolicy: {
        keepAvgCpuUtilizationUnder: 70,
        keepAvgMemoryUtilizationUnder: 80
      }
    }
  });

  return { resources: { internalApi } };
});
```


With the same `minInstances` and `maxInstances`, the service doesn't scale.

### Using Service Connect


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, PrivateService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const internalApi = new PrivateService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/internal-api.ts'
    }),
    resources: { cpu: 0.25, memory: 512 },
    loadBalancing: {
      type: 'service-connect'
    }
  });

  return { resources: { internalApi } };
});
```


Callers that list the service in `connectTo` get its address in `STP_<NAME>_ADDRESS`.

### Using an Application Load Balancer


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, PrivateService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const internalApi = new PrivateService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/internal-api.ts'
    }),
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 },
    loadBalancing: {
      type: 'application-load-balancer'
    }
  });

  return { resources: { internalApi } };
});
```


The service gets an internal load balancer with a stable DNS name in the VPC. `connectTo` injects the same
`STP_<NAME>_ADDRESS` variable.

This mode needs the [Stacktape Console](/getting-started/add-the-console) for now: without an account, the deploy stops
because the CLI expects the Console's default domain for every load balancer without its own domain or certificate, and
this one can't have either. Without an account, keep `service-connect`.

### Using a Dockerfile


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, PrivateService, DockerfilePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const internalApi = new PrivateService({
    packaging: new DockerfilePackaging({
      buildContextPath: '.',
      dockerfilePath: './Dockerfile'
    }),
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 }
  });

  return { resources: { internalApi } };
});
```


`dockerfilePath` is relative to `buildContextPath`.

## Connecting to other resources

`connectTo` works both ways: the service uses it to reach other resources, and callers use it to reach the service.
Here the private service reads a bucket, and a public web service calls the private service:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  PrivateService,
  WebService,
  Bucket,
  JsBundleImagePackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const dataBucket = new Bucket({});

  const internalApi = new PrivateService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/internal-api.ts'
    }),
    resources: { cpu: 0.25, memory: 512 },
    connectTo: [dataBucket]
  });

  const publicApi = new WebService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/public-api.ts'
    }),
    resources: { cpu: 0.25, memory: 512 },
    connectTo: [internalApi]
  });

  return { resources: { dataBucket, internalApi, publicApi } };
});
```


`internalApi` gets `STP_DATA_BUCKET_NAME` and `STP_DATA_BUCKET_ARN`. `publicApi` gets `STP_INTERNAL_API_ADDRESS`, a
`host:port` without a scheme: call `http://${process.env.STP_INTERNAL_API_ADDRESS}` over HTTP, or pass it as it is to a
gRPC client. In a config value, build the URL with `$CfFormat('http://{}', $ResourceParam('internalApi', 'address'))`.
For AWS services `connectTo` doesn't cover, add `iamRoleStatements`.

## Health checks

`internalHealthCheck` runs a command inside the container at an interval and replaces the container after `retries`
failures in a row. It catches a process that runs but no longer answers.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, PrivateService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const internalApi = new PrivateService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/internal-api.ts'
    }),
    resources: { cpu: 0.25, memory: 512 },
    internalHealthCheck: {
      healthCheckCommand: ['CMD-SHELL', 'curl -f http://localhost:3000/health || exit 1'],
      intervalSeconds: 30,
      timeoutSeconds: 5,
      retries: 3,
      startPeriodSeconds: 60
    }
  });

  return { resources: { internalApi } };
});
```


`startPeriodSeconds` is a grace period after start; set it to your service's start time. With an Application Load
Balancer, the load balancer also runs its own HTTP checks against the containers, which you can tune with
[overrides](/configuration/overrides-and-escape-hatches).

### Using run-on-init sidecars

A `run-on-init` container runs before the service starts, for example for migrations. It must exit with code 0, or
the task stops.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, PrivateService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const internalApi = new PrivateService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/internal-api.ts'
    }),
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 },
    sideContainers: [
      {
        name: 'migrate',
        containerType: 'run-on-init',
        packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
          entryfilePath: './src/migrate.ts'
        })
      }
    ]
  });

  return { resources: { internalApi } };
});
```

### Using always-running sidecars

An `always-running` container runs next to the service for its whole life, for example an OpenTelemetry collector
the service reaches on `localhost`. If it crashes, ECS replaces the whole task, service included.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  PrivateService,
  PrebuiltImagePackaging,
  JsBundleImagePackaging
} from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const internalApi = new PrivateService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/internal-api.ts'
    }),
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 },
    sideContainers: [
      {
        name: 'otel-collector',
        containerType: 'always-running',
        packaging: new PrebuiltImagePackaging({
          image: 'otel/opentelemetry-collector:latest'
        })
      }
    ]
  });

  return { resources: { internalApi } };
});
```

## Basic example


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new WebService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/server.ts'
    }),
    resources: {
      cpu: 0.5,
      memory: 1024
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { api }
  };
});
```


`JsBundleImagePackaging` bundles `./src/server.ts` into a small image; your server listens on the port in the `PORT`
environment variable (3000). `cpu` and `memory` size the Fargate task. Traffic comes through an HTTP API Gateway, so
the service needs no load balancer.

The deploy prints the service URL (`https://<api-id>.execute-api.<region>.amazonaws.com`). Open it, and read the
container output with `stacktape logs api --stage dev`.

## Traffic

`loadBalancing.type` picks how traffic reaches the containers:

| Mode | Use it for | Cost |
|---|---|---|
| `http-api-gateway` (default) | Most HTTP APIs and web apps | $1 per million requests |
| `application-load-balancer` | WebSockets, gradual deployments, `useFirewall`, high steady traffic | About $16 a month plus capacity units |
| `network-load-balancer` | TCP or TLS protocols such as MQTT | About $16 a month plus capacity units |

The load balancer gets cheaper than the gateway from about 20 million requests a month; see
[the comparison](/resources/networking/http-api-gateway#choosing-between-http-api-gateway-and-a-load-balancer). The
gateway also limits each request to 30 seconds, and it doesn't check that your app responds: add an
[`internalHealthCheck`](#health-checks) so a broken release rolls back instead of going live.

With `application-load-balancer`, the load balancer checks `/` every 5 seconds (4-second timeout); change that in
`loadBalancing.properties`. Without `customDomains`, a load balancer is served on a `*.stacktape-app.com` domain that
the [Stacktape Console](/getting-started/add-the-console) provides; without the Console connected, add a
[custom domain](#domains-and-cors) or the deploy stops and asks for one.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new WebService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/server.ts' }),
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 },
    loadBalancing: {
      type: 'application-load-balancer',
      properties: {
        healthcheckPath: '/health',
        healthcheckInterval: 10,
        healthcheckTimeout: 5
      }
    }
  });

  return { resources: { api } };
});
```


With `network-load-balancer`, list the `ports`: each sets the public port, the protocol (`TLS` by default, or `TCP`)
and, if it differs, the `containerPort`. Like the Application Load Balancer, a `TLS` port needs a custom domain when
the Console isn't connected.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const broker = new WebService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/broker.ts' }),
    resources: { cpu: 1, memory: 2048 },
    loadBalancing: {
      type: 'network-load-balancer',
      properties: {
        healthCheckProtocol: 'TCP',
        ports: [
          {
            port: 8883,
            protocol: 'TLS',
            containerPort: 1883
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  });

  return { resources: { broker } };
});
```


`healthCheckProtocol: 'TCP'` checks that the port accepts connections; use `HTTP` only if the container serves a
health path.

## Domains and CORS

<span id="custom-domain-names" />

[Set up the domain](/resources/networking/custom-domains#set-up-the-domain) with `stacktape domain add` once, then
list it in `customDomains`. The deploy uses the issued certificate and creates the DNS record.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new WebService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/server.ts' }),
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 },
    customDomains: [
      {
        domainName: 'api.example.com'
      }
    ]
  });

  return { resources: { api } };
});
```


`disableDnsRecordCreation: true` skips the DNS record when you manage DNS elsewhere, and `customCertificateArn` uses a
certificate you manage; see [External DNS](/resources/networking/custom-domains#external-dns).

`cors` works only with the default `http-api-gateway` mode. It replaces the CORS headers your app returns, so keep
CORS in your app when it differs by route.

## Compute resources

Fargate (default) runs the container without servers to manage; you set `cpu` and `memory`. Graviton
(`architecture: 'arm64'`) costs about 20% less than x86.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new WebService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/server.ts' }),
    resources: {
      cpu: 1,
      memory: 2048
    }
  });

  return { resources: { api } };
});
```


EC2 runs it on instances Stacktape manages; you set `instanceTypes`, for specific instance families, GPUs or cheaper
sustained heavy use. List several types for capacity flexibility, and match the image architecture: `c6g.large` is
Graviton.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new WebService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/server.ts' }),
    resources: {
      instanceTypes: ['c6g.large']
    }
  });

  return { resources: { api } };
});
```

## Scaling

The service runs `minInstances` containers at all times and adds more, up to `maxInstances`, when average CPU or memory
passes its target. Requests are spread across healthy containers.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new WebService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/server.ts' }),
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 },
    scaling: {
      minInstances: 2,
      maxInstances: 10,
      scalingPolicy: {
        keepAvgCpuUtilizationUnder: 70,
        keepAvgMemoryUtilizationUnder: 80
      }
    }
  });

  return { resources: { api } };
});
```


`minInstances` is your baseline cost. Keep it at 1 on non-production stages; the service never scales to zero.

### Port

Stacktape sends traffic to the port in the `PORT` variable, 3000 by default. If your app listens on a fixed port, such
as `app.listen(8080)` or Spring Boot's 8080, set `port` instead of changing the code. `stacktape init` sets it when it
finds a fixed port. With `network-load-balancer`, `ports[].containerPort` sets it instead.

Changing `port` on a deployed service replaces the resources that route to it, so requests can fail during that
deploy.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new WebService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/server.ts'
    }),
    port: 8080,
    resources: {
      cpu: 0.5,
      memory: 1024
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { api }
  };
});
```

## Connecting to other resources

`connectTo` grants IAM permissions, opens the network path to databases and Redis, and injects
`STP_<RESOURCE>_<PARAM>` environment variables.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  WebService,
  RelationalDatabase,
  RdsEnginePostgres,
  Bucket,
  JsBundleImagePackaging
} from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myDatabase = new RelationalDatabase({
    credentials: { masterUserPassword: "$Secret('db', 'password')" },
    engine: new RdsEnginePostgres({ version: '16', primaryInstance: { instanceSize: 'db.t4g.micro' } })
  });

  const uploadsBucket = new Bucket({});

  const api = new WebService({
    connectTo: [myDatabase, uploadsBucket],
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/server.ts' }),
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 }
  });

  return { resources: { myDatabase, uploadsBucket, api } };
});
```


The service gets `STP_MY_DATABASE_CONNECTION_STRING`, `STP_MY_DATABASE_HOST`, `STP_MY_DATABASE_PORT` and the other
database variables, plus `STP_UPLOADS_BUCKET_NAME` and `STP_UPLOADS_BUCKET_ARN`.
[Connecting resources](/configuration/connecting-resources) lists them per resource type. Put your own settings in
`environment`, secrets in [`$Secret()`](/configuration/secrets), and use `iamRoleStatements` for AWS services
`connectTo` doesn't cover.

## Side containers

`sideContainers` runs helper containers in the same task, sharing its CPU and memory:

- **`run-on-init`** must exit with code 0 before the service starts, for example migrations.
- **`always-running`** runs for the whole life of the task, for example a log or monitoring agent the service reaches
  on `localhost`. If it crashes, the task is replaced.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  WebService,
  JsBundleImagePackaging,
  DockerfilePackaging
} from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new WebService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/server.ts' }),
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 },
    sideContainers: [
      {
        name: 'migrations',
        containerType: 'run-on-init',
        packaging: new DockerfilePackaging({
          buildContextPath: './migrations',
          dockerfilePath: './Dockerfile'
        })
      }
    ]
  });

  return { resources: { api } };
});
```


For several containers that each take their own traffic, use a
[multi-container workload](/resources/compute/multi-container-workload).

## EFS storage

Mount an [EFS file system](/resources/storage/efs-filesystem) for files that must survive container replacement or be
shared between containers, such as uploads or model files. For most files, S3 is cheaper and simpler.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  WebService,
  EfsFilesystem,
  JsBundleImagePackaging
} from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const uploads = new EfsFilesystem({});

  const api = new WebService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/server.ts' }),
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 },
    volumeMounts: [
      {
        type: 'efs',
        properties: {
          efsFilesystemName: 'uploads',
          mountPath: '/data/uploads',
          rootDirectory: '/uploads'
        }
      }
    ]
  });

  return { resources: { uploads, api } };
});
```


`rootDirectory` mounts a directory of the file system and `readOnly` mounts it read-only. `stacktape dev` doesn't mount
EFS, so test persistence and read-only mounts on a deployed stage.

## Gradual deployments

With `loadBalancing.type: 'application-load-balancer'`, `deployment.strategy` shifts traffic to a new release step by
step (canary or linear) and rolls back on failure. Use it for production services where a bad release should reach
only part of the traffic. See [Gradual deployments](/deployment-and-lifecycle/gradual-deployments) for the strategies.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new WebService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/server.ts' }),
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 },
    loadBalancing: { type: 'application-load-balancer' },
    deployment: { strategy: 'Linear10PercentEvery3Minutes' }
  });

  return { resources: { api } };
});
```

## Private subnets and NAT

Containers run in public subnets with a public IP address by default and reach the internet directly.
`usePrivateSubnetsWithNAT: true` removes the public address and sends outbound traffic through NAT, for example when
an external API allowlists your IP addresses.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new WebService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/server.ts' }),
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 },
    usePrivateSubnetsWithNAT: true
  });

  return {
    resources: { api },
    stackConfig: { nat: 'ha-ec2' }
  };
});
```


The flag requires `stackConfig.nat`, which decides the NAT device, its cost and the outbound IP addresses: one
Stacktape-managed instance (`ec2`, about $7 a month), one per availability zone with failover (`ha-ec2`, two zones by
default), or AWS NAT Gateways (`gateway`, about $32 a month per zone plus $0.045 per GB). See
[VPC and networking](/configuration/connecting-resources#vpc-and-networking).

## Remote sessions

`enableRemoteSessions: true` lets you open a shell in a running container with
[`stacktape container shell`](/cli/container-shell), for example to check a live process or network reachability.
It adds a small Systems Manager agent container.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new WebService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/server.ts' }),
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 },
    enableRemoteSessions: true
  });

  return { resources: { api } };
});
```

## Basic example

A worker needs `packaging`, which builds the container image, and `resources`, which sizes it.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WorkerService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const worker = new WorkerService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/worker.ts'
    }),
    resources: {
      cpu: 0.5,
      memory: 1024
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { worker }
  };
});
```


`JsBundleImagePackaging` bundles `./src/worker.ts` into a small image. `cpu: 0.5` and `memory: 1024` size the Fargate
task. The deploy starts one container; read its output with `stacktape logs worker --stage dev`.

### Fargate

`architecture: 'arm64'` runs the task on Graviton, about 20% cheaper than the default `x86_64`, if your image and
native dependencies support ARM.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WorkerService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const worker = new WorkerService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/worker.ts' }),
    resources: {
      cpu: 1,
      memory: 2048,
      architecture: 'arm64'
    }
  });

  return { resources: { worker } };
});
```


`cpu` and `memory` must be one of the Fargate combinations:

| vCPU | Memory |
|---|---|
| 0.25 | 512–2048 MB |
| 0.5 | 1024–4096 MB |
| 1 | 2048–8192 MB |
| 2 | 4096–16384 MB |
| 4 | 8192–30720 MB |
| 8 | 16384–61440 MB |
| 16 | 32768–122880 MB |

Check real CPU and memory use on the Console's [Metrics](/observability/metrics) page or with
[`stacktape metrics`](/cli/metrics), and resize from there.

### EC2

`instanceTypes` lists the instance types to use; the first one is preferred. `enableWarmPool: true` keeps a
pre-initialized instance ready so scaling is faster (one instance type only).


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WorkerService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const worker = new WorkerService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/worker.ts' }),
    resources: {
      instanceTypes: ['c6g.large'],
      enableWarmPool: true
    }
  });

  return { resources: { worker } };
});
```


EC2 pays off for sustained heavy CPU use, GPUs or a specific instance family. For most workers, Fargate is simpler and
the price difference is small.

## Scaling

One container runs by default (`minInstances` and `maxInstances` are 1). To scale, raise `maxInstances` and set a
target for average CPU or memory use; ECS adds containers above the target and removes them below it.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WorkerService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const worker = new WorkerService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/worker.ts' }),
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 },
    scaling: {
      minInstances: 2,
      maxInstances: 8,
      scalingPolicy: {
        keepAvgCpuUtilizationUnder: 70,
        keepAvgMemoryUtilizationUnder: 80
      }
    }
  });

  return { resources: { worker } };
});
```


Both targets are 80% by default. For queue consumers, CPU usually follows the queue depth. Lower the target for more
headroom, or raise it for I/O-bound workers whose CPU stays low under load.

Each extra container runs and bills all the time, and each one is another consumer for your database or API. Raise
`minInstances` only after checking that they can take it.

## Connecting resources

`connectTo` grants IAM permissions, opens the network path to databases and Redis, and injects
`STP_<RESOURCE>_<PARAM>` environment variables.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  WorkerService,
  SqsQueue,
  RelationalDatabase,
  RdsEnginePostgres,
  JsBundleImagePackaging
} from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const jobsQueue = new SqsQueue({});
  const mainDatabase = new RelationalDatabase({
    credentials: { masterUserPassword: "$Secret('my-db-password')" },
    engine: new RdsEnginePostgres({
      version: '16',
      primaryInstance: { instanceSize: 'db.t4g.micro' }
    })
  });

  const worker = new WorkerService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/worker.ts' }),
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 },
    connectTo: [jobsQueue, mainDatabase]
  });

  return { resources: { jobsQueue, mainDatabase, worker } };
});
```


This worker gets `STP_JOBS_QUEUE_URL`, `STP_JOBS_QUEUE_ARN` and `STP_JOBS_QUEUE_NAME` for the queue, and
`STP_MAIN_DATABASE_CONNECTION_STRING`, `STP_MAIN_DATABASE_HOST`, `STP_MAIN_DATABASE_PORT` and the other database
variables. [Connecting resources](/configuration/connecting-resources) lists them per resource type. For AWS services
that `connectTo` doesn't cover, such as Bedrock, add `iamRoleStatements`. For an API key or another secret, see
[Use a secret in a container](/configuration/secrets#use-it-in-a-container).

### Consuming an SQS queue

A container reads the queue itself: it asks for messages, processes them and deletes each one it finished. A message it
doesn't delete comes back after the queue's visibility timeout and is processed again, so make the processing safe to
repeat. This is `src/worker.ts` from the config above; add `@aws-sdk/client-sqs` to your dependencies.

```ts
import { SQSClient, ReceiveMessageCommand, DeleteMessageCommand } from '@aws-sdk/client-sqs';

const sqs = new SQSClient({});
const queueUrl = process.env.STP_JOBS_QUEUE_URL!;
let running = true;
process.on('SIGTERM', () => (running = false));

while (running) {
  const { Messages = [] } = await sqs.send(
    new ReceiveMessageCommand({ QueueUrl: queueUrl, MaxNumberOfMessages: 10, WaitTimeSeconds: 20 })
  );
  for (const message of Messages) {
    await handleJob(JSON.parse(message.Body!)); // your processing
    await sqs.send(new DeleteMessageCommand({ QueueUrl: queueUrl, ReceiptHandle: message.ReceiptHandle! }));
    console.log(`processed message ${message.MessageId}`);
  }
}
```

`WaitTimeSeconds: 20` makes each request wait for messages instead of polling an empty queue. If you'd rather not run a
loop, a [Lambda function with an SQS trigger](/resources/triggers/sqs-events) receives and deletes messages for you.

To test it after a deploy, print the queue URL, send one message and read the worker's logs:

```bash
stacktape param get jobsQueue url --stage dev --region eu-west-1
```

```bash
aws sqs send-message --queue-url "<queue-url>" --message-body '{"job":"test"}' --region eu-west-1
```

```bash
stacktape logs worker --stage dev --region eu-west-1
```

The send command prints a `MessageId`, and within a few seconds the logs show `processed message <MessageId>` with the
same ID. If the line doesn't appear, the worker isn't receiving: check that `jobsQueue` is in its `connectTo`.

## Health checks

ECS replaces a container that exits. A health check also replaces one that keeps running but stopped working, for
example a polling loop that lost its connection. ECS runs the command inside the container; exit code 0 means healthy.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WorkerService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const worker = new WorkerService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/worker.ts' }),
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 },
    internalHealthCheck: {
      healthCheckCommand: ['CMD-SHELL', 'node ./healthcheck.js'],
      intervalSeconds: 30,
      timeoutSeconds: 5,
      retries: 3,
      startPeriodSeconds: 60
    }
  });

  return { resources: { worker } };
});
```


`intervalSeconds` (default 30) is how often the check runs, `retries` (default 3) how many failures in a row replace the
container, and `startPeriodSeconds` how long after start failures don't count. Set it to at least your worker's start
time.

## Graceful shutdown

On a deploy or scale-in, ECS sends `SIGTERM`, waits `stopTimeout` seconds (default 30, 2 to 120) and then kills the
container. Handle `SIGTERM`: stop taking new messages, finish the current one and exit.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WorkerService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const worker = new WorkerService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/worker.ts' }),
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 },
    stopTimeout: 60
  });

  return { resources: { worker } };
});
```


Raise `stopTimeout` when one message can take longer than about 20 seconds. A full deployment waits until the old
workers have stopped. A [fast deployment](/deployment-and-lifecycle/deploying-stacks#fast-deployments), when only code
changed, finishes once the new workers pass their `internalHealthCheck` (or, without one, 40 seconds after they start)
and lets the old ones shut down in the background.

## Side containers

`sideContainers` runs helper containers in the same task:

- **`run-on-init`** must exit with code 0 before the worker starts. Use it for migrations or one-time setup.
- **`always-running`** runs next to the worker for its whole life, for example a log or monitoring agent the worker
  reaches on `localhost`.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  WorkerService,
  JsBundleImagePackaging,
  DockerfilePackaging
} from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const worker = new WorkerService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/worker.ts' }),
    resources: { cpu: 1, memory: 2048 },
    sideContainers: [
      {
        name: 'migrate',
        containerType: 'run-on-init',
        packaging: new DockerfilePackaging({
          buildContextPath: './migrations'
        })
      },
      {
        name: 'log-forwarder',
        containerType: 'always-running',
        packaging: new DockerfilePackaging({
          buildContextPath: './log-forwarder'
        })
      }
    ]
  });

  return { resources: { worker } };
});
```


Side containers share the task's CPU and memory. For several containers with their own traffic and settings, use a
[multi-container workload](/resources/compute/multi-container-workload).

## EFS storage

Mount an [EFS file system](/resources/storage/efs-filesystem) for files that must survive container replacement or be
shared between containers, such as model files.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  WorkerService,
  EfsFilesystem,
  JsBundleImagePackaging
} from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const sharedData = new EfsFilesystem({});

  const worker = new WorkerService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/worker.ts' }),
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 },
    connectTo: [sharedData],
    volumeMounts: [
      {
        type: 'efs',
        properties: {
          efsFilesystemName: 'sharedData',
          mountPath: '/mnt/shared',
          rootDirectory: '/workers'
        }
      }
    ]
  });

  return { resources: { sharedData, worker } };
});
```


`rootDirectory` (default `/`) mounts a directory of the file system. For state, a database, queue or bucket is usually
the better place.

## Private subnets and NAT

By default the container runs in a public subnet with a public IP address and reaches the internet directly.
`usePrivateSubnetsWithNAT: true` moves it to private subnets, with no public address, and sends its outbound traffic
through NAT. Use it when an external API allowlists your IP addresses or your security policy forbids public IPs.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WorkerService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const worker = new WorkerService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/worker.ts' }),
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 },
    usePrivateSubnetsWithNAT: true
  });

  return { resources: { worker } };
});
```


The flag requires `stackConfig.nat`, which decides the NAT device, its cost and its outbound IP addresses: one
Stacktape-managed instance (`ec2`, about $7 a month), one per availability zone with failover (`ha-ec2`), or AWS NAT
Gateways (`gateway`, about $32 a month per zone plus $0.045 per GB). The deploy fails without it. See
[VPC and networking](/configuration/connecting-resources#vpc-and-networking).

## Remote sessions

`enableRemoteSessions: true` lets you open a shell in a running container with
[`stacktape container shell`](/cli/container-shell), for example to inspect a stuck worker. It adds a small Systems
Manager agent container. Turn it on for stages where you debug, not as a routine production tool.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WorkerService, JsBundleImagePackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const worker = new WorkerService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/worker.ts' }),
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 },
    enableRemoteSessions: true
  });

  return { resources: { worker } };
});
```

## Basic example

A DSQL database needs no properties:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, DsqlDatabase } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const database = new DsqlDatabase({});

  return { resources: { database } };
});
```


Example (YAML):

```yaml
resources:
  database:
    type: dsql-database
```

## Connect a workload

`connectTo` is the shortest path for a new application. It injects the endpoint and other non-secret connection details and grants the workload `dsql:DbConnectAdmin` on this cluster only.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  DsqlDatabase,
  LambdaFunction,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const database = new DsqlDatabase({
    deletionProtection: true
  });

  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts'
    }),
    connectTo: [database]
  });

  return { resources: { database, api } };
});
```


Example (YAML):

```yaml
resources:
  database:
    type: dsql-database
    properties:
      deletionProtection: true

  api:
    type: function
    properties:
      packaging:
        type: js-bundle
        properties:
          entryfilePath: ./src/handler.ts
      connectTo:
        - database
```


The workload receives:

| Variable | Value |
|---|---|
| `STP_DATABASE_ENDPOINT` | Cluster hostname |
| `STP_DATABASE_PORT` | `5432` |
| `STP_DATABASE_DATABASE_NAME` | `postgres` |
| `STP_DATABASE_USERNAME` | `admin` |
| `STP_DATABASE_REGION` | Deployment region |
| `STP_DATABASE_ID` | Cluster ID |
| `STP_DATABASE_ARN` | Cluster ARN |

There is deliberately no password or connection string. Generate an IAM authentication token for each new database connection and pass it to the PostgreSQL client as the password. Because `connectTo` injects the `admin` username, a raw signer must use the admin-token operation: `getDbConnectAdminAuthToken` in the JavaScript signer or `generate-db-connect-admin-auth-token` in the AWS CLI. The ordinary `DbConnect` token is for a custom database role and will not authenticate `admin`.

For Node.js, the [official Aurora DSQL connector](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aurora-dsql/latest/userguide/SECTION_program-with-dsql-connector-for-node-postgres.html) automatically chooses the admin token from the injected username and refreshes credentials for new pooled connections:

```ts
import { AuroraDSQLPool } from '@aws/aurora-dsql-node-postgres-connector';

const pool = new AuroraDSQLPool({
  host: process.env.STP_DATABASE_ENDPOINT!,
  user: process.env.STP_DATABASE_USERNAME!,
  database: process.env.STP_DATABASE_DATABASE_NAME!
});
```

Keep TLS enabled when using another PostgreSQL client.

DSQL limits a database session to 60 minutes. Configure the client pool to recycle connections before that limit, and do not generate one token at process startup and reuse it forever.

## Basic example

A table needs only its primary key:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, DynamoDbTable } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const postsTable = new DynamoDbTable({
    primaryKey: {
      partitionKey: {
        name: 'id',
        type: 'string'
      }
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { postsTable }
  };
});
```


The table runs in on-demand mode because `provisionedThroughput` is not set. Check it after the deploy:

```bash
stacktape query dynamodb postsTable --operation schema --stage dev
```

## Primary key

Every DynamoDB table needs a primary key that uniquely identifies each item. The primary key **cannot be changed after creation**, so design it carefully based on your access patterns.

DynamoDB supports two key types:

- **Simple key**: a `partitionKey` alone. Each item must have a unique partition key value. Use when items are accessed individually (e.g., user profiles by `userId`).
- **Composite key**: a `partitionKey` plus a `sortKey`. Multiple items can share the same partition key, distinguished by the sort key. Use when you need to query groups of related items (e.g., all orders for a user, sorted by date).


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, DynamoDbTable } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const ordersTable = new DynamoDbTable({
    primaryKey: {
      partitionKey: {
        name: 'userId',
        type: 'string'
      },
      sortKey: {
        name: 'createdAt',
        type: 'string'
      }
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { ordersTable }
  };
});
```


The `partitionKey` determines which partition stores the item. Choosing a high-cardinality partition key (like `userId` or `orderId`) distributes data evenly across partitions. A sort key enables range queries within a partition, for example querying all orders for a user between two dates.

Each attribute's `type` must be `string`, `number`, or `binary`. Most applications use `string` for identifiers and `number` or `string` for timestamps.

### Provisioned mode

Provisioned mode lets you set fixed read and write capacity units for predictable pricing. One read capacity unit (RCU) handles one 4 KB strongly consistent read per second. One write capacity unit (WCU) handles one 1 KB write per second. Requests that exceed your provisioned capacity get throttled.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, DynamoDbTable } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const sessionsTable = new DynamoDbTable({
    primaryKey: {
      partitionKey: { name: 'sessionId', type: 'string' }
    },
    provisionedThroughput: {
      readUnits: 50,
      writeUnits: 25,
      readScaling: {
        minUnits: 10,
        maxUnits: 200,
        keepUtilizationUnder: 70
      },
      writeScaling: {
        minUnits: 5,
        maxUnits: 100,
        keepUtilizationUnder: 70
      }
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { sessionsTable }
  };
});
```


The `readUnits` and `writeUnits` values set the initial (seed) capacity. Without auto-scaling, these are also the fixed limits — any excess traffic gets throttled.

## Secondary indexes

Without secondary indexes, you can only query items by their primary key. A global secondary index (GSI) lets you query by any other attribute, for example looking up users by `email` when the primary key is `userId`. `secondaryIndexes` creates global secondary indexes; local secondary indexes aren't available.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, DynamoDbTable } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const usersTable = new DynamoDbTable({
    primaryKey: {
      partitionKey: { name: 'userId', type: 'string' }
    },
    secondaryIndexes: [
      {
        name: 'byEmail',
        partitionKey: { name: 'email', type: 'string' }
      },
      {
        name: 'byDepartment',
        partitionKey: { name: 'department', type: 'string' },
        sortKey: { name: 'joinedAt', type: 'string' },
        projections: ['fullName', 'role']
      }
    ]
  });

  return {
    resources: { usersTable }
  };
});
```


Each GSI has its own partition key and optional sort key. The `projections` array lists additional attributes to copy into the index beyond the table's primary key, which is always projected automatically. Only projected attributes are available when querying through the index — if you need an attribute in query results, include it in `projections`.

GSIs consume their own read/write capacity (or request units in on-demand mode) and add to storage costs because data is duplicated. Add indexes based on actual query needs rather than speculatively.

## Streams

DynamoDB Streams capture item-level changes. Stacktape exposes a [DynamoDB stream trigger](/resources/triggers/dynamodb-streams) for [Lambda functions](/resources/compute/lambda-function) and [batch jobs](/resources/compute/batch-job), invoking them when items change. Enable streams for use cases like auditing, analytics, cache invalidation, or downstream synchronization.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, DynamoDbTable } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const productsTable = new DynamoDbTable({
    primaryKey: {
      partitionKey: { name: 'productId', type: 'string' }
    },
    streamType: 'NEW_AND_OLD_IMAGES'
  });

  return {
    resources: { productsTable }
  };
});
```


The `streamType` controls what data each stream record contains:

| Stream type | What's included | Use case |
|---|---|---|
| `KEYS_ONLY` | Only key attributes | Lightweight notifications, minimal data transfer |
| `NEW_IMAGE` | Full item after the change | Downstream sync, projections |
| `OLD_IMAGE` | Full item before the change | Undo/restore workflows |
| `NEW_AND_OLD_IMAGES` | Both before and after | Audit trails, change tracking, diff computation |

With a `streamType` set, the table has a `streamArn` parameter, and a [DynamoDB stream trigger](/resources/triggers/dynamodb-streams)
on a Lambda function or batch job processes the changes.

## Backups

`enablePointInTimeRecovery: true` keeps continuous backups, so you can restore the table to any second of the last 35
days. A restore creates a new table; it doesn't overwrite the existing one. It costs $0.20 per GB-month of table size
in us-east-1. Turn it on for production tables.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, DynamoDbTable } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const ordersTable = new DynamoDbTable({
    primaryKey: {
      partitionKey: { name: 'orderId', type: 'string' }
    },
    enablePointInTimeRecovery: true
  });

  return {
    resources: { ordersTable }
  };
});
```

## Connecting to other resources

Add the table to a workload's [`connectTo`](/configuration/connecting-resources). The workload can then read, write,
query, scan and run batch and transaction operations on the table and its indexes. AWS authorizes a transaction by the
item operations inside it, so no extra permission is needed. It also gets these variables, for a table named
`itemsTable`:

| Variable | Value |
|---|---|
| `STP_ITEMS_TABLE_NAME` | Table name |
| `STP_ITEMS_TABLE_ARN` | Table ARN |
| `STP_ITEMS_TABLE_STREAM_ARN` | Stream ARN, only when the table has a `streamType` |


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  DynamoDbTable,
  LambdaFunction,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const itemsTable = new DynamoDbTable({
    primaryKey: {
      partitionKey: { name: 'itemId', type: 'string' }
    }
  });

  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts'
    }),
    connectTo: [itemsTable]
  });

  return {
    resources: { itemsTable, api }
  };
});
```


Use the name as `TableName` in SDK calls:

```typescript
import { DynamoDBClient } from '@aws-sdk/client-dynamodb';
import { DynamoDBDocumentClient, PutCommand, GetCommand } from '@aws-sdk/lib-dynamodb';

const client = DynamoDBDocumentClient.from(new DynamoDBClient({}));
const TABLE_NAME = process.env.STP_ITEMS_TABLE_NAME!;

export const handler = async () => {
  await client.send(new PutCommand({
    TableName: TABLE_NAME,
    Item: { itemId: '123', title: 'Hello', createdAt: new Date().toISOString() }
  }));

  const result = await client.send(new GetCommand({
    TableName: TABLE_NAME,
    Key: { itemId: '123' }
  }));

  return { statusCode: 200, body: JSON.stringify(result.Item) };
};
```

For other DynamoDB operations, such as managing backups, add `iamRoleStatements` to the workload.

## Expiring items (TTL)

DynamoDB deletes items whose TTL attribute (a Unix timestamp in seconds) is in the past, at no cost. That suits sessions,
caches and anything with a natural expiry. Turn it on with an [override](/configuration/overrides-and-escape-hatches)
of the table's CloudFormation resource, `<TableName>GlobalTable`:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, DynamoDbTable } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const sessions = new DynamoDbTable({
    primaryKey: { partitionKey: { name: 'sessionId', type: 'string' } },
    overrides: {
      SessionsGlobalTable: {
        TimeToLiveSpecification: { Enabled: true, AttributeName: 'expiresAt' }
      }
    }
  });

  return { resources: { sessions } };
});
```


Deletion can lag the expiry time by a day or two, so filter expired items out of reads if that matters.
[`stacktape synth`](/cli/synth) shows the logical names of all child resources.

## Basic example

Atlas clusters need API keys for your Atlas organization in `providerConfig.mongoDbAtlas`. Create a key in Atlas under
**Organization Settings → API Keys** with the Organization Project Creator role, and store the private key as a
[secret](/configuration/secrets):

```bash
stacktape secret set mongo-atlas-private-key
```


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, MongoDbAtlasCluster } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const mainDb = new MongoDbAtlasCluster({
    clusterTier: 'M10',
    version: '7.0'
  });

  return {
    providerConfig: {
      mongoDbAtlas: {
        publicKey: 'abcdef12',
        privateKey: "$Secret('mongo-atlas-private-key')",
        organizationId: '5f1a2b3c4d5e6f7a8b9c0d1e'
      }
    },
    resources: { mainDb }
  };
});
```


The deploy creates an Atlas project for the stack, an M10 replica set with three nodes running MongoDB 7.0, and a VPC
peering connection between the project and your stack's VPC. The project is named after the stack, so you find the
cluster in the Atlas UI under that name. A new cluster takes several minutes to create.

You can also save the Atlas keys once on the **Third-party providers** page of the [Console](/stacktape-console/console-overview)
instead of adding `providerConfig` to every project.

## Connecting from workloads

Add the cluster to a workload's [`connectTo`](/configuration/connecting-resources). Stacktape injects
`STP_<NAME>_CONNECTION_STRING` (`STP_MAIN_DB_CONNECTION_STRING` here) and creates an Atlas database user for the
workload's IAM role with read and write access to every database in the cluster.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, MongoDbAtlasCluster, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const mainDb = new MongoDbAtlasCluster({
    clusterTier: 'M10'
  });
  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts'
    }),
    connectTo: [mainDb]
  });

  return {
    providerConfig: {
      mongoDbAtlas: {
        publicKey: 'abcdef12',
        privateKey: "$Secret('mongo-atlas-private-key')",
        organizationId: '5f1a2b3c4d5e6f7a8b9c0d1e'
      }
    },
    resources: { mainDb, api }
  };
});
```


The connection string has no password in it. Your code signs in with the workload's AWS credentials, so tell the driver
to use AWS authentication:

```typescript
import { MongoClient } from 'mongodb';

const client = new MongoClient(process.env.STP_MAIN_DB_CONNECTION_STRING!, {
  authMechanism: 'MONGODB-AWS',
  authSource: '$external'
});
const users = client.db('myapp').collection('users');
```

Mongoose takes the same two options in `connect()`. Create the client outside the handler so warm Lambda invocations
reuse the connection.

### Admin user

For your own access from a laptop or a GUI client, add an admin user. It gets the `atlasAdmin` role on this cluster.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, MongoDbAtlasCluster } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const mainDb = new MongoDbAtlasCluster({
    clusterTier: 'M10',
    adminUserCredentials: {
      userName: 'admin',
      password: "$Secret('mongo-admin-password')"
    }
  });

  return {
    providerConfig: {
      mongoDbAtlas: {
        publicKey: 'abcdef12',
        privateKey: "$Secret('mongo-atlas-private-key')",
        organizationId: '5f1a2b3c4d5e6f7a8b9c0d1e'
      }
    },
    resources: { mainDb }
  };
});
```


If the deploy doesn't find the `mongo-admin-password` secret, it generates a random password and stores it there. Your
machine also needs network access: in the default `internet` mode it has it; in the other modes add your address to
`whitelistedIps` or tunnel through a [bastion host](/resources/security/bastion-host).

## Network access

Network settings live in `providerConfig.mongoDbAtlas.accessibility` and apply to every Atlas cluster in the stack.

| Mode | Who can connect |
|---|---|
| `internet` (default) | Anyone on the internet with valid credentials |
| `vpc` | Resources in the stack's VPC, plus `whitelistedIps` |
| `scoping-workloads-in-vpc` | Only workloads that list the cluster in `connectTo`, plus `whitelistedIps` |
| `whitelisted-ips-only` | Only the addresses in `whitelistedIps` |

In `vpc` and `scoping-workloads-in-vpc`, traffic goes over the VPC peering connection. A Lambda function that connects
to the cluster then needs `joinDefaultVpc: true`, and the deploy fails with a message saying so if it's missing.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, MongoDbAtlasCluster } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const mainDb = new MongoDbAtlasCluster({
    clusterTier: 'M10'
  });

  return {
    providerConfig: {
      mongoDbAtlas: {
        publicKey: 'abcdef12',
        privateKey: "$Secret('mongo-atlas-private-key')",
        organizationId: '5f1a2b3c4d5e6f7a8b9c0d1e',
        accessibility: {
          accessibilityMode: 'scoping-workloads-in-vpc',
          whitelistedIps: ['203.0.113.4/32']
        }
      }
    },
    resources: { mainDb }
  };
});
```

## Scaling

Set a tier range to let Atlas move the cluster between tiers as CPU and memory load changes:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, MongoDbAtlasCluster } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const mainDb = new MongoDbAtlasCluster({
    clusterTier: 'M10',
    autoScaling: {
      minClusterTier: 'M10',
      maxClusterTier: 'M40'
    }
  });

  return {
    providerConfig: {
      mongoDbAtlas: {
        publicKey: 'abcdef12',
        privateKey: "$Secret('mongo-atlas-private-key')",
        organizationId: '5f1a2b3c4d5e6f7a8b9c0d1e'
      }
    },
    resources: { mainDb }
  };
});
```


Tier scaling is off until you set `minClusterTier` or `maxClusterTier`. The maximum caps your bill.
`disableScaleDown: true` lets the cluster grow but never shrink.

Storage grows by itself when the disk is 90% full, and never shrinks. `disableDiskScaling: true` turns that off.
`diskSizeGB` sets the starting size.

## Replication

A cluster has three electable nodes by default. The `replication` property changes the node counts:

- `numElectableNodes` (3, 5 or 7): nodes that can become primary. More nodes survive more failures.
- `numReadOnlyNodes`: extra nodes for read throughput. They never become primary.
- `numAnalyticsNodes`: nodes for long reports and aggregations, so they don't slow production queries.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, MongoDbAtlasCluster } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const mainDb = new MongoDbAtlasCluster({
    clusterTier: 'M30',
    replication: {
      numElectableNodes: 5,
      numReadOnlyNodes: 2,
      numAnalyticsNodes: 1
    }
  });

  return {
    providerConfig: {
      mongoDbAtlas: {
        publicKey: 'abcdef12',
        privateKey: "$Secret('mongo-atlas-private-key')",
        organizationId: '5f1a2b3c4d5e6f7a8b9c0d1e'
      }
    },
    resources: { mainDb }
  };
});
```

## Backups

Backups are off by default. `enableBackups: true` takes a daily snapshot at 18:00 UTC.
`enablePointInTimeRecovery: true` adds restores to any second in the last 7 days and needs `enableBackups`. Turn both on
for production data. Restores happen in the Atlas UI.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, MongoDbAtlasCluster } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const mainDb = new MongoDbAtlasCluster({
    clusterTier: 'M10',
    enableBackups: true,
    enablePointInTimeRecovery: true
  });

  return {
    providerConfig: {
      mongoDbAtlas: {
        publicKey: 'abcdef12',
        privateKey: "$Secret('mongo-atlas-private-key')",
        organizationId: '5f1a2b3c4d5e6f7a8b9c0d1e'
      }
    },
    resources: { mainDb }
  };
});
```

## Sharding

`numShards` above 1 splits the data across several replica sets, for write volumes or data sizes one replica set can't
handle. It needs M30 or larger and a well-chosen shard key. Most apps don't need it.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, MongoDbAtlasCluster } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const mainDb = new MongoDbAtlasCluster({
    clusterTier: 'M30',
    numShards: 3
  });

  return {
    providerConfig: {
      mongoDbAtlas: {
        publicKey: 'abcdef12',
        privateKey: "$Secret('mongo-atlas-private-key')",
        organizationId: '5f1a2b3c4d5e6f7a8b9c0d1e'
      }
    },
    resources: { mainDb }
  };
});
```

## BI Connector

The BI Connector gives SQL clients and BI tools read access to the cluster. It uses a lot of CPU, so on M10 and M20 add
an analytics node and read from it:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, MongoDbAtlasCluster } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const analyticsDb = new MongoDbAtlasCluster({
    clusterTier: 'M30',
    biConnector: {
      enabled: true,
      readPreference: 'analytics'
    },
    replication: {
      numAnalyticsNodes: 1
    }
  });

  return {
    providerConfig: {
      mongoDbAtlas: {
        publicKey: 'abcdef12',
        privateKey: "$Secret('mongo-atlas-private-key')",
        organizationId: '5f1a2b3c4d5e6f7a8b9c0d1e'
      }
    },
    resources: { analyticsDb }
  };
});
```


`readPreference: 'analytics'` requires at least one analytics node.

## Basic example


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, OpenSearchDomain } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const search = new OpenSearchDomain({
    clusterConfig: {
      instanceType: 't3.medium.search',
      instanceCount: 1
    },
    storage: {
      size: 20
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { search }
  };
});
```


This creates a single-node domain for development or low-traffic search. Always set `clusterConfig.instanceType`;
without it, AWS picks its own default, a larger and more expensive instance. `storage.size` is the EBS volume per data
node. Check the domain after the deploy:

```bash
stacktape query opensearch search --operation indices --stage dev
```

## Engine version

`version` defaults to `'2.17'`. Set it explicitly so a future Stacktape release with a newer default doesn't upgrade
your domain. Accepted: `2.17`, `2.15`, `2.13`, `2.11`, `2.9`, `2.7`, `2.5`, `2.3`, `1.3`, `1.2`, `1.1` and `1.0`.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, OpenSearchDomain } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const search = new OpenSearchDomain({
    clusterConfig: {
      instanceType: 't3.medium.search',
      instanceCount: 1
    },
    storage: {
      size: 20
    },
    version: '2.17'
  });

  return {
    resources: { search }
  };
});
```

### Production example

Three data nodes, three dedicated masters and Multi-AZ with standby, the setup AWS requires for its 99.99% SLA:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, OpenSearchDomain } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const search = new OpenSearchDomain({
    version: '2.17',
    clusterConfig: {
      instanceType: 'r6g.large.search',
      instanceCount: 3,
      dedicatedMasterType: 'm6g.large.search',
      dedicatedMasterCount: 3,
      standbyEnabled: true
    },
    storage: {
      size: 100,
      iops: 5000,
      throughput: 250
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { search }
  };
});
```


`iops: 5000` and `throughput: 250` raise the gp3 volumes above their 3,000 IOPS and 125 MiB/s baseline, at extra cost.

## Storage

`storage` sets the EBS volume of each data node. Instance types with local NVMe storage, such as `i3`, don't use it.

| Property | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `size` | GiB per data node, required. The range depends on the instance type. |
| `iops` | gp3 IOPS per data node (default 3000) |
| `throughput` | gp3 throughput per data node in MiB/s (default 125) |


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, OpenSearchDomain } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const search = new OpenSearchDomain({
    clusterConfig: {
      instanceType: 'r6g.large.search',
      instanceCount: 2
    },
    storage: {
      size: 200,
      iops: 6000,
      throughput: 400
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { search }
  };
});
```

## Networking

`accessibility.accessibilityMode` decides who can reach the domain. Every request must also be signed with AWS
credentials (SigV4) that have access to the domain.

| Mode | Network access |
|---|---|
| `internet` (default) | Public endpoint |
| `vpc` | Resources in the stack's VPC |
| `scoping-workloads-in-vpc` | Only workloads that list the domain in `connectTo` |

Lambda functions need `joinDefaultVpc: true` to reach a domain in a VPC mode.


> **Warning:** You can't switch between `internet` and the VPC modes on a deployed domain; it needs a new domain. Pick the mode before
the first deploy, or plan a snapshot and restore.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  OpenSearchDomain,
  LambdaFunction,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const search = new OpenSearchDomain({
    clusterConfig: {
      instanceType: 'r6g.large.search',
      instanceCount: 2
    },
    storage: {
      size: 50
    },
    accessibility: {
      accessibilityMode: 'scoping-workloads-in-vpc'
    }
  });

  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts'
    }),
    joinDefaultVpc: true,
    connectTo: [search]
  });

  return {
    resources: { search, api }
  };
});
```

## Connecting to other resources

Add the domain to a workload's `connectTo`. The workload's role gets access to the domain, and it receives
`STP_SEARCH_DOMAIN_ENDPOINT` (the host name) and `STP_SEARCH_ARN` for a domain named `search`. Sign each request with
the workload's AWS credentials, for example with the OpenSearch JavaScript client:

```ts
import { Client } from '@opensearch-project/opensearch';
import { AwsSigv4Signer } from '@opensearch-project/opensearch/aws';
import { defaultProvider } from '@aws-sdk/credential-provider-node';

const endpoint = process.env.STP_SEARCH_DOMAIN_ENDPOINT!;

// In dev mode the endpoint is a local http:// URL that needs no signing.
const client = endpoint.startsWith('http://')
  ? new Client({ node: endpoint })
  : new Client({
      ...AwsSigv4Signer({ region: process.env.AWS_REGION!, service: 'es', getCredentials: defaultProvider() }),
      node: `https://${endpoint}`
    });
```


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  OpenSearchDomain,
  LambdaFunction,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const search = new OpenSearchDomain({
    clusterConfig: {
      instanceType: 't3.medium.search',
      instanceCount: 1
    },
    storage: {
      size: 20
    }
  });

  const indexer = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/indexer.ts'
    }),
    connectTo: [search],
    environment: {
      OPENSEARCH_ENDPOINT: "$ResourceParam('search', 'domainEndpoint')"
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { search, indexer }
  };
});
```

## Logging

The domain sends three log types to CloudWatch, each on by default:

| Log type | Contains | Retention |
|---|---|---|
| Error logs | Script errors, invalid queries, indexing and snapshot problems | 30 days |
| Search slow logs | Queries above the thresholds set in your index settings | 5 days |
| Index slow logs | Indexing above the thresholds set in your index settings | 5 days |

Slow logs stay empty until you set thresholds in the index settings, for example
`index.search.slowlog.threshold.query.warn: 2s`. `disabled: true` on a log type skips its log group.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, OpenSearchDomain } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const search = new OpenSearchDomain({
    clusterConfig: {
      instanceType: 't3.medium.search',
      instanceCount: 1
    },
    storage: {
      size: 20
    },
    logging: {
      errorLogs: {
        retentionDays: 90
      },
      searchSlowLogs: {
        retentionDays: 30
      },
      indexSlowLogs: {
        disabled: true
      }
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { search }
  };
});
```

## OpenSearch Dashboards with Cognito

`userPool` names a [user auth pool](/resources/security/user-auth-pool) whose users can sign in to OpenSearch
Dashboards in the browser. Skip it if only your code talks to the domain.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, OpenSearchDomain, UserAuthPool } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const authPool = new UserAuthPool({});

  const search = new OpenSearchDomain({
    clusterConfig: {
      instanceType: 't3.medium.search',
      instanceCount: 1
    },
    storage: {
      size: 20
    },
    userPool: 'authPool'
  });

  return {
    resources: { authPool, search }
  };
});
```

### Valkey Serverless


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Redis, $Secret } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const cache = new Redis({
    defaultUserPassword: $Secret('redis', 'password'),
    engine: { type: 'valkey-serverless' }
  });

  return {
    resources: { cache }
  };
});
```


There are no instances to size. The cache stores up to 5,000 GB and is billed for the data it holds (minimum 100 MB) plus ElastiCache Processing Units (ECPUs) for requests. A `GET` or `SET` of up to 1 KB uses 1 ECPU. Storage costs about $61 per GB-month, so above roughly 150 MB of data a small `valkey` node costs less.

**Compatibility.** A serverless cache always runs in cluster mode with all keys behind one endpoint. Standard (non-cluster) clients connect unchanged. Commands that touch several keys — `MGET`, `MSET`, transactions, and Lua scripts — fail with a `CROSSSLOT` error unless all their keys share a hash slot. Put a common hash tag in the key names, such as `{user:42}:cart` and `{user:42}:profile`. Queue libraries need the same: for BullMQ, use a prefix with braces, for example `prefix: '{bull}'`. Keyspace notifications are not supported.

**Cost limits.** Two optional limits cap the bill:

- `maxDataGb` caps stored data. At the limit, Valkey evicts the least recently used keys **that have a TTL**; if no key has a TTL, writes fail with an out-of-memory error. Set TTLs on cached keys when you use it.
- `maxEcpuPerSecond` caps request throughput (minimum 1,000). Requests above it are throttled.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Redis, $Secret } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const cache = new Redis({
    defaultUserPassword: $Secret('redis', 'password'),
    engine: {
      type: 'valkey-serverless',
      properties: {
        maxDataGb: 2,
        maxEcpuPerSecond: 5000
      }
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { cache }
  };
});
```


Clients connect on port 6379. Port 6380 serves lower-latency, eventually consistent reads to clients that send `READONLY`; its connection details are the `reader*` parameters.

### Node-based Valkey


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Redis, $Secret } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const cache = new Redis({
    defaultUserPassword: $Secret('redis', 'password'),
    engine: {
      type: 'valkey',
      properties: { instanceSize: 'cache.t4g.micro' }
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { cache }
  };
});
```


This creates a single node running the latest Valkey version. It works with every Redis client and command pattern. Slow-query logging is enabled by default and sent to CloudWatch.

### Redis OSS (existing caches)

AWS publishes no Redis OSS versions after 7.1, so new caches must use Valkey. `engine.type: redis` keeps caches created before Valkey support running on the version they have:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Redis, $Secret } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const cache = new Redis({
    defaultUserPassword: $Secret('redis', 'password'),
    engine: {
      type: 'redis',
      properties: { instanceSize: 'cache.t4g.micro', version: '6.2' }
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { cache }
  };
});
```


**Switching to Valkey.** Change `engine.type` from `redis` to `valkey`. AWS migrates the cache in place, which took about 20 minutes for a single `cache.t4g.micro` node: the endpoint and data stay, writes pause for a few seconds during a failover (longer when the cache has no replicas), and the switch cannot be reversed.

### Read replicas and automatic failover

Set `numReplicaNodes` to add read replicas, and `enableAutomaticFailover` to promote a replica when the primary fails.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Redis, $Secret } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const cache = new Redis({
    defaultUserPassword: $Secret('redis', 'password'),
    engine: {
      type: 'valkey',
      properties: {
        instanceSize: 'cache.m7g.large',
        numReplicaNodes: 2,
        enableAutomaticFailover: true
      }
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { cache }
  };
});
```


Each replica runs the same instance size as the primary, so two replicas triple the cost. Failover causes a brief connectivity interruption; clients should reconnect and retry.


> **Warning:** Deploy replicas first, then enable `enableAutomaticFailover` in a separate deployment.

### Sharding

`enableSharding` splits the key space across `numShards` shards, each with its own primary and replicas. Clients must support cluster mode. Sharding requires `numReplicaNodes >= 1` and must be set when the cache is created.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Redis, $Secret } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const cache = new Redis({
    defaultUserPassword: $Secret('redis', 'password'),
    engine: {
      type: 'valkey',
      properties: {
        instanceSize: 'cache.r7g.large',
        enableSharding: true,
        numShards: 3,
        numReplicaNodes: 1
      }
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { cache }
  };
});
```


Most teams don't need sharding: add replicas to scale reads, or use `valkey-serverless`, which scales without sharding decisions.

## Networking

Caches run inside your stack's VPC and have no public IP addresses.

- **`vpc` (default)**: any resource in the same VPC can connect: container workloads, batch jobs, and Lambda functions with `joinDefaultVpc: true`.
- **`scoping-workloads-in-vpc`**: only resources that list this cache in their `connectTo` can connect.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Redis, $Secret } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const cache = new Redis({
    defaultUserPassword: $Secret('redis', 'password'),
    engine: { type: 'valkey-serverless' },
    accessibility: {
      accessibilityMode: 'scoping-workloads-in-vpc'
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { cache }
  };
});
```


To connect from your local machine, deploy a [bastion host](/resources/security/bastion-host) and use [`stacktape bastion tunnel`](/cli/bastion-tunnel) or [`stacktape query redis`](/cli/query-redis).

## Connecting from workloads

Use [`connectTo`](/configuration/connecting-resources) to grant a workload access. Stacktape opens the security group rules and injects connection details as environment variables, for example `STP_CACHE_HOST` and `STP_CACHE_PORT` for a resource named `cache`. To pass a complete connection URL, use the `connectionString` [referenceable parameter](/configuration/referenceable-parameters):


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  Redis,
  LambdaFunction,
  $Secret,
  $ResourceParam,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const cache = new Redis({
    defaultUserPassword: $Secret('redis', 'password'),
    engine: { type: 'valkey-serverless' }
  });

  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts'
    }),
    joinDefaultVpc: true,
    connectTo: [cache],
    environment: { REDIS_URL: $ResourceParam('cache', 'connectionString') }
  });

  return {
    resources: { cache, api }
  };
});
```


> **Info:** Lambda functions must set `joinDefaultVpc: true` to reach the cache. Container workloads and batch jobs are already in the VPC.

### Example handler

```typescript
import { createClient } from 'redis';

const client = createClient({ url: process.env.REDIS_URL });
await client.connect();

export const handler = async () => {
  await client.set('key', 'value');
  const result = await client.get('key');
  return { statusCode: 200, body: JSON.stringify({ result }) };
};
```

The connection string uses `rediss://` (TLS) and the `default` user. All traffic is encrypted in transit, so the client library must support TLS.

## Backups

Set `automatedBackupRetentionDays` to keep daily snapshots for that many days (maximum 35 for serverless caches). `0` (the default) disables backups.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Redis, $Secret } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const cache = new Redis({
    defaultUserPassword: $Secret('redis', 'password'),
    engine: { type: 'valkey-serverless' },
    automatedBackupRetentionDays: 7
  });

  return {
    resources: { cache }
  };
});
```

## Dev mode

During [`stacktape dev`](/local-development/dev-mode-overview), the cache runs locally in Docker: Valkey for `valkey` and `valkey-serverless`, Redis for `redis`. For `valkey-serverless`, the local container runs in cluster mode, so `CROSSSLOT` errors show up locally the same way they do in AWS. Keyspace notifications still work locally; set `dev.remote: true` to test against the deployed cache.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Redis, $Secret } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const cache = new Redis({
    defaultUserPassword: $Secret('redis', 'password'),
    engine: { type: 'valkey-serverless' },
    dev: {
      remote: true
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { cache }
  };
});
```

## Basic example


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, RelationalDatabase, RdsEnginePostgres } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const mainDatabase = new RelationalDatabase({
    credentials: {
      masterUserPassword: "$Secret('database', 'password')"
    },
    engine: new RdsEnginePostgres({
      version: '16',
      primaryInstance: {
        instanceSize: 'db.t4g.micro'
      }
    })
  });

  return {
    resources: { mainDatabase }
  };
});
```


The deploy creates a PostgreSQL 16 database on a `db.t4g.micro` instance with 20 GB of storage. It contains a database
named `defdb`, and you sign in as `db_master_user`. If the `database` secret doesn't exist yet, the deploy generates a
random password and stores it there. Creating a new database takes 10 to 15 minutes.

Check that it answers:

```bash
stacktape query sql mainDatabase "SELECT version()" --stage dev
```

Anyone who has the credentials can reach this database from the internet, because `internet` is the default
[access mode](#accessibility). Restrict it before you store real data.

### Aurora

Aurora is a clustered PostgreSQL- or MySQL-compatible database. It keeps six copies of your data across three
availability zones. Engine types: `aurora-postgresql` and `aurora-mysql` (classes `AuroraEnginePostgresql` and
`AuroraEngineMysql`).


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, RelationalDatabase, AuroraEnginePostgresql } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const mainDatabase = new RelationalDatabase({
    credentials: {
      masterUserPassword: "$Secret('database', 'password')"
    },
    engine: new AuroraEnginePostgresql({
      version: '16',
      instances: [{ instanceSize: 'db.r6g.large' }, { instanceSize: 'db.r6g.large' }]
    })
  });

  return {
    resources: { mainDatabase }
  };
});
```


The first entry in `instances` is the writer, the others are readers. With two or more instances, AWS promotes a reader
if the writer fails, usually within a minute. Readers share read traffic through the reader endpoint; they don't add
write capacity. Each instance bills at its own hourly rate, plus storage at $0.10 per GB-month and $0.20 per million
I/O requests ([Aurora pricing](https://aws.amazon.com/rds/aurora/pricing/)).

### Aurora Serverless v2

Aurora Serverless v2 scales compute in Aurora capacity units (ACUs, about 2 GB of memory each) between `minCapacity`
and `maxCapacity`. Engine types: `aurora-postgresql-serverless-v2` and `aurora-mysql-serverless-v2` (classes
`AuroraServerlessV2EnginePostgresql` and `AuroraServerlessV2EngineMysql`). An ACU-hour costs $0.12 in us-east-1.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, RelationalDatabase, AuroraServerlessV2EnginePostgresql } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const mainDatabase = new RelationalDatabase({
    credentials: {
      masterUserPassword: "$Secret('database', 'password')"
    },
    engine: new AuroraServerlessV2EnginePostgresql({
      version: '16',
      minCapacity: 0.5,
      maxCapacity: 16
    }),
    accessibility: {
      accessibilityMode: 'vpc'
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { mainDatabase }
  };
});
```


- **`minCapacity`**: when you leave it out, Stacktape sets 0. The database then pauses after about five minutes without
  connections and you pay only for storage while it's paused. The first connection after a pause waits about 15 seconds
  while it resumes. Set `0.5` or more to keep it running, which costs at least $44 a month at 0.5 ACU.
- **`maxCapacity`** (default 10) caps scaling and cost. 16 ACUs give about 32 GB of memory.
- **`serverlessReadersCount`** (default 0) adds readers. Each reader scales between the same bounds.

Pausing at 0 ACUs needs a recent engine version (for example PostgreSQL 16.3 or later). A major version such as `'16'`
always gets a current minor version.

AWS retired Aurora Serverless v1 on 31 March 2025, and Stacktape v4 rejects its engine types
(`aurora-postgresql-serverless`, `aurora-mysql-serverless`). To move a deployed v1 database, see
[Upgrading from v3](/getting-started/upgrading-from-v3/#aurora-serverless-v1).

## Accessibility

The access mode decides who can open a network connection to the database. The username and password are checked in
every mode.

| Mode | Who can connect |
|---|---|
| `internet` (default) | Anyone on the internet who has the credentials |
| `vpc` | Resources in the stack's VPC, plus `whitelistedIps` |
| `scoping-workloads-in-vpc` | Only workloads that list the database in `connectTo`, plus `whitelistedIps` |
| `whitelisted-ips-only` | Only the addresses in `whitelistedIps` |

Use `scoping-workloads-in-vpc` for production. It admits the workloads that declare the database in
[`connectTo`](/configuration/connecting-resources), plus any `whitelistedIps`. The example below also admits one office
address; leave `whitelistedIps` out if only your workloads should connect.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, RelationalDatabase, RdsEnginePostgres } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const mainDatabase = new RelationalDatabase({
    credentials: {
      masterUserPassword: "$Secret('database', 'password')"
    },
    engine: new RdsEnginePostgres({
      version: '16',
      primaryInstance: {
        instanceSize: 'db.t4g.micro'
      }
    }),
    accessibility: {
      accessibilityMode: 'scoping-workloads-in-vpc',
      whitelistedIps: ['203.0.113.50/32']
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { mainDatabase }
  };
});
```


`whitelistedIps` adds addresses, for example your office or a CI runner, and has no effect in `internet` mode. In the
VPC modes the database keeps a public IP address that only whitelisted addresses can reach. `forceDisablePublicIp: true`
removes it; set it, without `whitelistedIps`, for a database nothing outside the VPC can reach. On Aurora you can set it
only when you create the database.

To connect from your machine to a database that isn't open to the internet, use a
[bastion host](/resources/security/bastion-host) and [`stacktape bastion tunnel`](/cli/bastion-tunnel), or pass
`--bastion-resource` to `stacktape query sql`.

## High availability and read replicas

- **Standard RDS**: `multiAz: true` on the primary instance keeps a standby copy in a second availability zone. AWS fails
  over to it automatically and patches with less downtime. The standby serves no traffic and doubles the instance cost.
  `readReplicas` adds instances that serve reads, each with its own endpoint and asynchronous replication.
- **Aurora**: add instances to `instances`. Every extra instance is both a failover target and a reader.
- **Aurora Serverless v2**: set `serverlessReadersCount`.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, RelationalDatabase, RdsEnginePostgres } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const mainDatabase = new RelationalDatabase({
    credentials: {
      masterUserPassword: "$Secret('database', 'password')"
    },
    engine: new RdsEnginePostgres({
      version: '16',
      primaryInstance: {
        instanceSize: 'db.r6g.large',
        multiAz: true
      },
      readReplicas: [{ instanceSize: 'db.r6g.large' }]
    })
  });

  return {
    resources: { mainDatabase }
  };
});
```

## Connecting from workloads

List the database in a workload's [`connectTo`](/configuration/connecting-resources). Stacktape opens the network path
(in the VPC modes) and injects one environment variable per [referenceable parameter](#referenceable-parameters). For a
database named `mainDatabase` these include `STP_MAIN_DATABASE_CONNECTION_STRING`, `STP_MAIN_DATABASE_HOST`,
`STP_MAIN_DATABASE_PORT`, `STP_MAIN_DATABASE_DB_NAME` and `STP_MAIN_DATABASE_JDBC_CONNECTION_STRING`. Aurora adds the
`READER_*` variants, and RDS read replicas add `READ_REPLICA_*` lists.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  RelationalDatabase,
  RdsEnginePostgres,
  LambdaFunction,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const mainDatabase = new RelationalDatabase({
    credentials: {
      masterUserPassword: "$Secret('database', 'password')"
    },
    engine: new RdsEnginePostgres({
      version: '16',
      primaryInstance: {
        instanceSize: 'db.t4g.micro'
      }
    })
  });
  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/api.ts'
    }),
    memory: 1024,
    timeout: 30,
    connectTo: [mainDatabase]
  });

  return {
    resources: { mainDatabase, api }
  };
});
```


The connection string includes the username, password, host, port and database name. Prisma, Drizzle, TypeORM and most
drivers accept it as is:

```typescript
import { Client } from 'pg';

const client = new Client({
  connectionString: process.env.STP_MAIN_DATABASE_CONNECTION_STRING
});
await client.connect();
```

A Lambda function opens a connection per concurrent execution. If a busy function exhausts the database's connection
limit, reuse the client across invocations (create it outside the handler) or move to a larger instance.

Run migrations with a [release command](/deployment-and-lifecycle/release-commands): it runs your deployed image
inside the VPC during each deploy, before services and functions get the new code. A `local-script` with
`connectTo: ['mainDatabase']`, called from a [hook](/configuration/hooks-and-scripts), works too, but it runs on the
machine that deploys; a database that isn't open to the internet then needs a `local-script-with-bastion-tunneling`
script and a [bastion host](/resources/security/bastion-host).

## Storage

Standard RDS starts with 20 GB of gp3 storage (`storage.initialSize`) and grows automatically up to 200 GB
(`storage.maxSize`) when free space runs low. You pay for the allocated size, not the maximum. Aurora manages storage
itself and grows up to 128 TB.

Raise `initialSize` for write-heavy workloads, because each automatic expansion can briefly slow I/O. Set `maxSize` to cap
storage cost if a bug starts writing data in a loop.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, RelationalDatabase, RdsEnginePostgres } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const mainDatabase = new RelationalDatabase({
    credentials: {
      masterUserPassword: "$Secret('database', 'password')"
    },
    engine: new RdsEnginePostgres({
      version: '16',
      primaryInstance: {
        instanceSize: 'db.t4g.medium'
      },
      storage: {
        initialSize: 50,
        maxSize: 500
      }
    })
  });

  return {
    resources: { mainDatabase }
  };
});
```

## Logging

Database logs go to CloudWatch and are kept for 90 days (`logging.retentionDays`). The log types depend on the engine:

| Engine | Log types |
|---|---|
| PostgreSQL | `postgresql` |
| MySQL, MariaDB | `audit`, `error`, `general`, `slowquery` |
| Oracle | `alert`, `audit`, `listener`, `trace` |
| SQL Server | `agent`, `error` |

`logging.engineSpecificOptions` tunes what gets logged:

- **PostgreSQL**: `log_min_duration_statement` logs queries slower than this many milliseconds (default 10000).
  `log_statement` picks the statements to log: `none`, `ddl` (default: `CREATE`, `ALTER`, `DROP`), `mod` (also `INSERT`,
  `UPDATE`, `DELETE`) or `all`. `log_connections`, `log_disconnections` and `log_lock_waits` help with connection and
  locking problems.
- **MySQL**: `server_audit_events` (default `['QUERY_DDL']`) picks what the audit log records. `QUERY` and `QUERY_DML`
  log every data operation and produce a lot of output. `long_query_time` (default 10 seconds) sets the slow query
  threshold.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, RelationalDatabase, RdsEnginePostgres } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const mainDatabase = new RelationalDatabase({
    credentials: {
      masterUserPassword: "$Secret('database', 'password')"
    },
    engine: new RdsEnginePostgres({
      version: '16',
      primaryInstance: {
        instanceSize: 'db.t4g.micro'
      }
    }),
    logging: {
      retentionDays: 30,
      engineSpecificOptions: {
        log_min_duration_statement: 1000,
        log_statement: 'mod'
      }
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { mainDatabase }
  };
});
```


Read the logs with [`stacktape logs`](/cli/logs). `logging.disabled: true` turns CloudWatch logging off, and
[log forwarding](/observability/log-forwarding) sends the logs to another service.

## Alarms

Database alarms notify your [alert channels](/observability/alert-channels) when a metric crosses a threshold. See
[Alarms](/observability/alarms) for evaluation settings and channels.

| Trigger type | Threshold unit | Watches |
|---|---|---|
| `database-cpu-utilization` | Percent | CPU usage |
| `database-free-memory` | MB | Available memory |
| `database-free-storage` | MB | Free disk space |
| `database-connection-count` | Count | Open connections |
| `database-read-latency` | Seconds | Read I/O latency |
| `database-write-latency` | Seconds | Write I/O latency |

Set at least a `database-free-storage` alarm on every production database. A database that runs out of storage stops
accepting writes.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, RelationalDatabase, RdsEnginePostgres } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const mainDatabase = new RelationalDatabase({
    credentials: {
      masterUserPassword: "$Secret('database', 'password')"
    },
    engine: new RdsEnginePostgres({
      version: '16',
      primaryInstance: {
        instanceSize: 'db.t4g.micro'
      }
    }),
    alarms: [
      {
        trigger: {
          type: 'database-cpu-utilization',
          properties: { thresholdPercent: 80 }
        }
      },
      {
        trigger: {
          type: 'database-free-storage',
          properties: { thresholdMB: 1000 }
        }
      }
    ]
  });

  return {
    resources: { mainDatabase }
  };
});
```


`disabledGlobalAlarms` excludes named legacy Console alarm rules while you
[export them into the config](/observability/alarms#migrating-legacy-console-alarm-rules).

