`appDirectory` is the folder with `app.config.ts`, relative to the Stacktape config. `.` (the default) fits an app at the
repository root; in a monorepo, point it at the app's folder, such as `./apps/web`.

Run `stacktape deploy --stage dev`. The deploy builds the app and prints its URL, a CloudFront address until you add a
custom domain. Open it and check a server-rendered page and a static asset.

## Connect to resources

`connectTo` gives the server code access to other resources and injects their parameters as environment variables:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, TanStackWeb, RelationalDatabase, RdsEnginePostgres, $Secret } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const mainDatabase = new RelationalDatabase({
    engine: new RdsEnginePostgres({ version: '16', primaryInstance: { instanceSize: 'db.t4g.micro' } }),
    credentials: { masterUserPassword: $Secret('main-database-password') }
  });

  const web = new TanStackWeb({
    appDirectory: '.',
    connectTo: [mainDatabase],
    serverLambda: { joinDefaultVpc: true }
  });

  return {
    resources: { mainDatabase, web }
  };
});
```


The server code reads `STP_MAIN_DATABASE_CONNECTION_STRING`. See
[Connecting resources](/configuration/connecting-resources) for what each resource injects. Set other values with
`environment`, and secrets with [`$Secret()`](/configuration/directives). `environment` applies to the build and to
the server function, so variables the framework inlines into browser code at build time work too. A value from this
stack that isn't deployed yet, such as the URL of a new custom domain, is left out of the build with a warning; the next
deploy includes it. For AWS services that `connectTo` doesn't cover, add `iamRoleStatements`.

To run database migrations on each deploy, set `releaseCommand`, for example `npx prisma migrate deploy`. It runs once
in the VPC before the new code goes live; see [release commands](/deployment-and-lifecycle/release-commands).

## Basic example

```yaml
resources:
  mail:
    type: email-sender
    properties:
      identity: example.com

  sendWelcomeEmail:
    type: function
    properties:
      packaging:
        type: js-bundle
        properties:
          entryfilePath: src/send-welcome-email.ts
      connectTo:
        - mail
```

```ts
import { SESv2Client, SendEmailCommand } from '@aws-sdk/client-sesv2';

const ses = new SESv2Client({ region: process.env.STP_MAIL_REGION });

export const handler = async () => {
  await ses.send(new SendEmailCommand({
    FromEmailAddress: `hello@${process.env.STP_MAIL_IDENTITY}`,
    Destination: { ToAddresses: ['customer@example.net'] },
    ConfigurationSetName: process.env.STP_MAIL_CONFIGURATION_SET_NAME,
    Content: {
      Simple: {
        Subject: { Data: 'Welcome' },
        Body: { Text: { Data: 'Thanks for joining us.' } }
      }
    }
  }));
};
```

`connectTo` injects `IDENTITY`, `IDENTITY_ARN`, `REGION`, `CONFIGURATION_SET_NAME` and `FEEDBACK_TOPIC_ARN` (here
`STP_MAIL_IDENTITY` and so on). It grants `ses:SendEmail` and `ses:SendRawEmail` on that identity and configuration set
only, and no SES administration.

## Basic example

A bus needs no properties. Consumers subscribe with an [event bus trigger](/resources/triggers/event-bus-events), and
producers get access with `connectTo`:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, EventBus, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const orderBus = new EventBus({});

  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/api.ts' }),
    connectTo: [orderBus]
  });

  const orderProcessor = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/order-processor.ts' }),
    events: [
      {
        type: 'event-bus',
        properties: {
          eventBusName: 'orderBus',
          eventPattern: { source: ['order-service'], 'detail-type': ['OrderPlaced'] }
        }
      }
    ]
  });

  return {
    resources: { orderBus, api, orderProcessor }
  };
});
```


Every consumer whose pattern matches gets the event; there are no competing consumers as with SQS. Patterns, payload
transformation and failure handling are on [Event bus events](/resources/triggers/event-bus-events).

## Publish events

`connectTo` gives a workload permission to publish and injects the bus ARN. For a bus named `orderBus`, the variable is
`STP_ORDER_BUS_ARN`.

`PutEvents` can succeed as a request while individual entries fail, for example when AWS throttles them. Check
`FailedEntryCount` and retry only the failed entries:

```typescript
import { EventBridgeClient, PutEventsCommand } from '@aws-sdk/client-eventbridge';

const eventBridge = new EventBridgeClient({});

const result = await eventBridge.send(
  new PutEventsCommand({
    Entries: [
      {
        EventBusName: process.env.STP_ORDER_BUS_ARN,
        Source: 'order-service',
        DetailType: 'OrderPlaced',
        Detail: JSON.stringify({ orderId: '12345', amount: 99.99 })
      }
    ]
  })
);

if (result.FailedEntryCount) {
  const failed = result.Entries?.filter((entry) => entry.ErrorCode);
  throw new Error(`EventBridge rejected ${result.FailedEntryCount} events: ${JSON.stringify(failed)}`);
}
```

One request takes up to 10 entries, and AWS limits the total size of a request (see
[PutEvents](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/userguide/eb-putevents.html)). For large payloads, upload the
data to an [S3 bucket](/resources/storage/s3-bucket) and put the object key in `Detail`.

The consumer receives the whole event. Your data is in `detail`:

```typescript
export default async (event: { source: string; 'detail-type': string; detail: { orderId: string; amount: number } }) => {
  console.info(`Order ${event.detail.orderId} for $${event.detail.amount}`);
};
```

## Create topics

Topics aren't infrastructure here: MSK Serverless doesn't create them automatically, and CloudFormation can't declare
them for Serverless clusters. Create them with a Kafka admin client, from setup code or a
[deployment script](/resources/advanced/deployment-scripts). For Node.js:

```bash
pnpm add kafkajs aws-msk-iam-sasl-signer-js
```

```ts
import { Kafka } from 'kafkajs';
import { generateAuthToken } from 'aws-msk-iam-sasl-signer-js';

const region = process.env.AWS_REGION!;
const kafka = new Kafka({
  clientId: 'topic-setup',
  brokers: process.env.STP_EVENTS_BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS!.split(','),
  ssl: true,
  sasl: {
    mechanism: 'oauthbearer',
    oauthBearerProvider: async () => ({ value: (await generateAuthToken({ region })).token })
  }
});

const admin = kafka.admin();
await admin.connect();
await admin.createTopics({ topics: [{ topic: 'orders', numPartitions: 3 }] });
await admin.disconnect();
```

`createTopics` skips topics that already exist, so the setup can run on every deploy. Choose the partition count on
purpose: MSK Serverless charges per partition-hour, and partitions also set how many consumers can read in parallel.

## Basic example


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, KinesisStream, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const clickStream = new KinesisStream({});

  const collector = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/collect.ts' }),
    connectTo: [clickStream]
  });

  const processor = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/process.ts' }),
    events: [{ type: 'kinesis-stream', properties: { kinesisStreamName: 'clickStream' } }]
  });

  return {
    resources: { clickStream, collector, processor }
  };
});
```


The stream is on-demand and keeps records for 24 hours. `collector` writes to it; `processor` runs on batches of new
records. Batching, retries and failure handling for consumers are on [Kinesis events](/resources/triggers/kinesis-events).

## Write records

`connectTo` gives a workload permission to read and write the stream and injects its `ARN` and `NAME`. For a stream
named `clickStream`, the variables are `STP_CLICK_STREAM_ARN` and `STP_CLICK_STREAM_NAME`:

```typescript
import { KinesisClient, PutRecordCommand } from '@aws-sdk/client-kinesis';

const kinesis = new KinesisClient({});

await kinesis.send(
  new PutRecordCommand({
    StreamName: process.env.STP_CLICK_STREAM_NAME,
    PartitionKey: 'user-42',
    Data: Buffer.from(JSON.stringify({ userId: '42', page: '/pricing' }))
  })
);
```

Records with the same `PartitionKey` go to the same shard and are read in order. Pick a key with many values, such as
a user or device ID, so the load spreads across shards.

## Basic example

A topic needs no properties. Subscribers are Lambda functions with an [SNS event](/resources/triggers/sns-events):


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, SnsTopic, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const orderEvents = new SnsTopic({});

  const sendReceipt = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/send-receipt.ts' }),
    events: [{ type: 'sns', properties: { snsTopicName: 'orderEvents' } }]
  });

  const recordAnalytics = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/record-analytics.ts' }),
    events: [{ type: 'sns', properties: { snsTopicName: 'orderEvents' } }]
  });

  return {
    resources: { orderEvents, sendReceipt, recordAnalytics }
  };
});
```


Each published message runs both functions. Filters, retries and the event shape are on
[SNS events](/resources/triggers/sns-events).

## Publish messages

`connectTo` gives a workload permission to publish and injects the topic's `ARN` and `NAME`:

```typescript
const api = new LambdaFunction({
  packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/api.ts' }),
  connectTo: [orderEvents]
});
```

```typescript
import { SNSClient, PublishCommand } from '@aws-sdk/client-sns';

const sns = new SNSClient({});

await sns.send(
  new PublishCommand({
    TopicArn: process.env.STP_ORDER_EVENTS_ARN,
    Message: JSON.stringify({ type: 'order-placed', orderId: '456' })
  })
);
```

For a topic named `orderEvents`, the variables are `STP_ORDER_EVENTS_ARN` and `STP_ORDER_EVENTS_NAME`. Containers
publish the same way. See [Connecting resources](/configuration/connecting-resources).

A message can be up to 256 KB. For a larger payload, upload the data to an [S3 bucket](/resources/storage/s3-bucket)
and publish the object key.

## Fan out to SQS queues

A Lambda subscriber loses a message after Lambda's retries run out. When each consumer needs a buffer, its own retries
and a dead-letter queue, subscribe an SQS queue per consumer. Stacktape has no property for this subscription, so add it
as a [raw CloudFormation resource](/resources/advanced/raw-cloudformation-resources), and allow the topic to send to
the queue:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, SnsTopic, SqsQueue, $ResourceParam } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const orderEvents = new SnsTopic({});

  const billingQueue = new SqsQueue({
    policyStatements: [
      {
        Effect: 'Allow',
        Principal: { Service: 'sns.amazonaws.com' },
        Action: ['sqs:SendMessage'],
        Condition: { ArnEquals: { 'aws:SourceArn': $ResourceParam('orderEvents', 'arn') } }
      }
    ]
  });

  return {
    resources: { orderEvents, billingQueue },
    cloudformationResources: {
      BillingQueueSubscription: {
        Type: 'AWS::SNS::Subscription',
        Properties: {
          TopicArn: $ResourceParam('orderEvents', 'arn'),
          Protocol: 'sqs',
          Endpoint: $ResourceParam('billingQueue', 'arn'),
          RawMessageDelivery: true
        }
      }
    }
  };
});
```


`RawMessageDelivery: true` puts the published message in the SQS body as is, without the SNS envelope. Consume each
queue with an [SQS event](/resources/triggers/sqs-events) or a worker.

## Basic example


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, SqsQueue } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const taskQueue = new SqsQueue({});

  return {
    resources: { taskQueue }
  };
});
```


This creates a standard queue with the AWS defaults: messages up to 1 MiB, kept for 4 days, hidden for 30 seconds after
a consumer receives them. The queue alone does nothing; add a producer and a consumer.

## Send messages

`connectTo` gives a workload permission to send, receive and delete messages, and injects the queue's `ARN`, `NAME` and
`URL` as environment variables:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, SqsQueue, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const taskQueue = new SqsQueue({});
  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/api.ts' }),
    connectTo: [taskQueue]
  });

  return {
    resources: { taskQueue, api }
  };
});
```


```typescript
import { SQSClient, SendMessageCommand } from '@aws-sdk/client-sqs';

const sqs = new SQSClient({});

await sqs.send(
  new SendMessageCommand({
    QueueUrl: process.env.STP_TASK_QUEUE_URL,
    MessageBody: JSON.stringify({ task: 'resize-image', imageId: '123' })
  })
);
```

For a queue named `taskQueue`, the variables are `STP_TASK_QUEUE_ARN`, `STP_TASK_QUEUE_NAME` and `STP_TASK_QUEUE_URL`.
See [Connecting resources](/configuration/connecting-resources).

## Consume messages

Pick the consumer by how the work runs:

- **A Lambda function**, for jobs that finish within 15 minutes. Add an [SQS event](/resources/triggers/sqs-events) to
  the function. Lambda polls the queue, passes batches and deletes the messages that succeed. Most queues should start
  here.
- **A container**, for long jobs, a steady stream of work or code that needs a long-lived process. Use a
  [worker service](/resources/compute/worker-service) with `connectTo` and poll the queue in your code.

A container consumer receives a message, processes it, then deletes it. If it crashes before the delete, the message
becomes visible again after the visibility timeout and another receive picks it up:

```typescript
import { SQSClient, ReceiveMessageCommand, DeleteMessageCommand } from '@aws-sdk/client-sqs';

const sqs = new SQSClient({});
const QueueUrl = process.env.STP_TASK_QUEUE_URL;

while (true) {
  const { Messages = [] } = await sqs.send(
    new ReceiveMessageCommand({ QueueUrl, MaxNumberOfMessages: 10, WaitTimeSeconds: 20 })
  );
  for (const message of Messages) {
    await processTask(JSON.parse(message.Body!));
    await sqs.send(new DeleteMessageCommand({ QueueUrl, ReceiptHandle: message.ReceiptHandle }));
  }
}
```

Test either consumer by sending one message after `stacktape deploy`:

```bash
aws sqs send-message --queue-url "<queue-url>" --message-body '{"task":"resize-image","imageId":"123"}'
```

`stacktape param get taskQueue url --stage dev --region eu-west-1` prints the queue URL.

## Dead-letter queue

A message that always fails comes back after every visibility timeout until the retention period ends, and each attempt
costs a run of your consumer. A `redrivePolicy` moves it to a second queue after `maxReceiveCount` receives:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, SqsQueue } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const deadLetterQueue = new SqsQueue({
    alarms: [{ trigger: { type: 'sqs-queue-not-empty' } }]
  });
  const taskQueue = new SqsQueue({
    redrivePolicy: {
      targetSqsQueueName: 'deadLetterQueue',
      maxReceiveCount: 3
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { taskQueue, deadLetterQueue }
  };
});
```


The `sqs-queue-not-empty` alarm tells you when a message lands in the dead-letter queue. `targetSqsQueueArn` points at
a queue outside the stack. A FIFO queue needs a FIFO dead-letter queue.

## Deliver EventBridge events to the queue

The queue's `events` property sends matching events from an event bus into the queue, with no producer code. Set one
of `eventBusName`, `eventBusArn` or `useDefaultBus`:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, SqsQueue, EventBus } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const orderBus = new EventBus({});
  const orderQueue = new SqsQueue({
    events: [
      {
        type: 'event-bus',
        properties: {
          eventBusName: 'orderBus',
          eventPattern: {
            source: ['order-service'],
            'detail-type': ['OrderPlaced']
          }
        }
      }
    ]
  });

  return {
    resources: { orderBus, orderQueue }
  };
});
```


A FIFO queue also needs `messageGroupId` on the integration. `input`, `inputPath`, `inputTransformer` and
`onDeliveryFailure` work as on [event bus triggers](/resources/triggers/event-bus-events).

## Basic example

The load balancer holds listeners and domains. Routes live on the functions and containers that use it.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, ApplicationLoadBalancer } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myAlb = new ApplicationLoadBalancer({
    customDomains: [{ domainName: 'app.example.com' }]
  });

  return {
    resources: { myAlb }
  };
});
```


By default the load balancer listens on HTTPS (443) and redirects HTTP (80) to HTTPS. The certificate comes from the
custom domain, so [set up the domain](/resources/networking/custom-domains#set-up-the-domain) before the first deploy.
Without `customDomains`, the load balancer is served on a `*.stacktape-app.com` domain that the
[Stacktape Console](/getting-started/add-the-console) provides; without the Console connected, the deploy stops and
asks for a custom domain.

Functions attach with an `ApplicationLoadBalancerIntegration` event. Containers use an `application-load-balancer`
event with a `containerPort`, or a [web service](/resources/compute/web-service) creates its own load balancer.

### Several services behind one load balancer


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  ApplicationLoadBalancer,
  LambdaFunction,
  ApplicationLoadBalancerIntegration,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myAlb = new ApplicationLoadBalancer({});

  const usersApi = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/users.ts'
    }),
    events: [
      new ApplicationLoadBalancerIntegration({
        loadBalancerName: 'myAlb',
        priority: 10,
        paths: ['/users', '/users/*']
      })
    ]
  });

  const ordersApi = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/orders.ts'
    }),
    events: [
      new ApplicationLoadBalancerIntegration({
        loadBalancerName: 'myAlb',
        priority: 20,
        paths: ['/orders', '/orders/*']
      })
    ]
  });

  return {
    resources: { myAlb, usersApi, ordersApi }
  };
});
```


`/users/123` goes to `usersApi` (priority 10) and `/orders/456` to `ordersApi` (priority 20).

## Listeners

Set `listeners` to change ports, allow only some IP addresses or redirect unmatched requests. Each listener needs a
`protocol` (`HTTP` or `HTTPS`) and a `port`. An HTTPS listener uses the certificates of `customDomains`, or the ACM
certificates in its `customCertificateArns`.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, ApplicationLoadBalancer } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myAlb = new ApplicationLoadBalancer({
    customDomains: [{ domainName: 'api.example.com' }],
    listeners: [
      {
        protocol: 'HTTPS',
        port: 443,
        whitelistIps: ['10.0.0.0/8', '192.168.1.0/24']
      },
      {
        protocol: 'HTTP',
        port: 80
      }
    ]
  });

  return {
    resources: { myAlb }
  };
});
```


`whitelistIps` rejects requests from other addresses at the load balancer. `defaultAction` redirects requests that
match no rule, with `HTTP_301` or `HTTP_302`, and can reuse parts of the URL with `#{protocol}`, `#{host}`, `#{port}`,
`#{path}` and `#{query}`.

## Custom domains


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, ApplicationLoadBalancer } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myAlb = new ApplicationLoadBalancer({
    customDomains: [{ domainName: 'api.example.com' }, { domainName: 'api-v2.example.com' }]
  });

  return {
    resources: { myAlb }
  };
});
```


Each domain needs an issued certificate in the stack's region; [Custom domains](/resources/networking/custom-domains)
covers the setup and DNS hosted elsewhere. The object form takes the certificate and DNS options; the plain list of
names (`['api.example.com']`) also works.

## CDN

`cdn.enabled: true` puts CloudFront in front of the load balancer. It helps with cacheable public content and users far
from your region; for personalized responses it only adds cost. With custom listeners, `listenerPort` tells CloudFront
which one to use. See [CDN](/resources/networking/cdn) for cache rules.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, ApplicationLoadBalancer } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myAlb = new ApplicationLoadBalancer({
    customDomains: [{ domainName: 'api.example.com' }],
    cdn: {
      enabled: true,
      cloudfrontPriceClass: 'PriceClass_100'
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { myAlb }
  };
});
```

## Firewall

`useFirewall` names a [web application firewall](/resources/security/web-application-firewall) resource with
`scope: 'regional'` and attaches it to the load balancer.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, ApplicationLoadBalancer, WebAppFirewall } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const apiFirewall = new WebAppFirewall({
    scope: 'regional'
  });

  const myAlb = new ApplicationLoadBalancer({
    useFirewall: 'apiFirewall'
  });

  return {
    resources: { apiFirewall, myAlb }
  };
});
```


With a CDN in front, attach the firewall to the CDN instead (`cdn.useFirewall`, a firewall with `cdn` scope), so bad
requests stop at CloudFront.

## Internal load balancer

`interface: 'internal'` makes the load balancer reachable only from inside the VPC, for HTTP services your other
services call.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, ApplicationLoadBalancer } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const internalAlb = new ApplicationLoadBalancer({
    interface: 'internal'
  });

  return {
    resources: { internalAlb }
  };
});
```

## Alarms

Load balancer alarms use the `application-load-balancer-error-rate` (share of 4xx and 5xx responses),
`application-load-balancer-unhealthy-targets` and `application-load-balancer-custom` triggers.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, ApplicationLoadBalancer } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myAlb = new ApplicationLoadBalancer({
    alarms: [
      {
        trigger: {
          type: 'application-load-balancer-error-rate',
          properties: { thresholdPercent: 5 }
        }
      },
      {
        trigger: {
          type: 'application-load-balancer-unhealthy-targets',
          properties: { thresholdPercent: 50 }
        }
      }
    ]
  });

  return {
    resources: { myAlb }
  };
});
```


The unhealthy-targets alarm fires when any target group passes the threshold; `onlyIncludeTargets` narrows it to some
services. See [Alarms](/observability/alarms) for `evaluation` settings, comparison operators and channels.

### API-backed CDN


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, HttpApiGateway } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myApi = new HttpApiGateway({
    cdn: {
      enabled: true
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { myApi }
  };
});
```


CloudFront caches API Gateway and load balancer responses only when your API sends `Cache-Control` headers or you set
`cachingOptions.defaultTTL`. The deploy prints the distribution URL (`cdnUrl`).

### Bucket-backed CDN

Static files from a [bucket](/resources/storage/s3-bucket) are the most common case. Files without a `Cache-Control`
header stay cached for six months, and each deploy clears the cache.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Bucket } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const staticAssets = new Bucket({
    cdn: {
      enabled: true,
      cloudfrontPriceClass: 'PriceClass_100'
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { staticAssets }
  };
});
```


`PriceClass_100` limits CloudFront to its North American and European locations, the cheapest option; see
[Price class](#price-class). `$ResourceParam('staticAssets', 'cdnUrl')` gives the URL, using the parent resource's name.

### Static site with custom domain

A bucket behind a CDN with your own domain, for a static site or single-page app:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Bucket } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const frontend = new Bucket({
    cdn: {
      enabled: true,
      cloudfrontPriceClass: 'PriceClass_100',
      customDomains: [{ domainName: 'app.example.com' }]
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { frontend },
    stackConfig: {
      outputs: [{ name: 'siteUrl', value: "$ResourceParam('frontend', 'cdnCanonicalUrl')" }]
    }
  };
});
```


CloudFront needs the certificate in `us-east-1`. `stacktape domain add` creates it there, so
[set up the domain](/resources/networking/custom-domains#set-up-the-domain) before the first deploy.

### Multi-origin API with WAF

One domain for an API and its static assets, with a firewall in front of both:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, HttpApiGateway, Bucket, WebAppFirewall } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const assets = new Bucket({});

  const waf = new WebAppFirewall({
    scope: 'cdn'
  });

  const api = new HttpApiGateway({
    cdn: {
      enabled: true,
      useFirewall: 'waf',
      routeRewrites: [
        {
          path: '/assets/*',
          routeTo: {
            type: 'bucket',
            properties: { bucketName: 'assets' }
          },
          cachingOptions: { defaultTTL: 86400 }
        }
      ]
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { assets, waf, api }
  };
});
```


`/assets/*` comes from the bucket and is cached for 24 hours; every other path goes to the API uncached. `useFirewall`
names a [web application firewall](/resources/security/web-application-firewall) with `scope: 'cdn'`.

## Caching

CDN caching controls how long CloudFront stores responses at edge locations before requesting fresh content from your origin. Proper caching reduces latency, lowers origin load, and saves on data transfer costs.

The default caching behavior depends on the origin type:

- **Bucket origins**: cached for 6 months when the origin has no `Cache-Control` header. Stacktape automatically invalidates all cached content on each deploy.
- **API Gateway / ALB origins**: not cached by default. Responses are only cached if your origin sends `Cache-Control` headers or you set a `defaultTTL`.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, HttpApiGateway } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myApi = new HttpApiGateway({
    cdn: {
      enabled: true,
      cachingOptions: {
        defaultTTL: 300,
        maxTTL: 86400,
        minTTL: 60,
        cacheMethods: ['GET', 'HEAD', 'OPTIONS']
      }
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { myApi }
  };
});
```


The `defaultTTL` (in seconds) is used when the origin response has no `Cache-Control` or `Expires` header — here, 300 seconds (5 minutes). The `maxTTL` caps how long CloudFront caches content even if the origin sends a higher `max-age`. The `minTTL` overrides the origin's `max-age` if the origin sets a lower value. Adding `OPTIONS` to `cacheMethods` is useful when your API serves CORS preflight requests.

Automatic Gzip and Brotli compression is enabled by default, reducing transfer size and cost. Set `disableCompression: true` to turn it off — rarely needed unless your origin handles compression itself.

If you have a pre-existing CloudFront cache policy, pass its ID via `cachePolicyId` instead of configuring TTL and cache key options manually.

### Cache key

The cache key determines what makes each cached response unique. By default, bucket origins use the URL path only, while API/ALB origins use the URL path plus query string. You can include specific headers, cookies, and query parameters in the cache key — different values produce different cached entries. Values included in the cache key are always forwarded to the origin.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, HttpApiGateway } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myApi = new HttpApiGateway({
    cdn: {
      enabled: true,
      cachingOptions: {
        defaultTTL: 600,
        cacheKeyParameters: {
          queryString: { whitelist: ['page', 'limit'] },
          headers: { whitelist: ['Accept-Language'] },
          cookies: { none: true }
        }
      }
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { myApi }
  };
});
```


In this example, only the `page` and `limit` query parameters and the `Accept-Language` header affect the cache key. No cookies are included. Requests with different values for these produce different cached responses.

For cookies, the options are `none` (no cookies), `all` (all cookies), `whitelist` (specific cookies), or `allExcept` (all except listed). For headers: `none` or `whitelist`. For query strings: `none`, `all`, or `whitelist`.


> **Tip:** Keep the cache key as narrow as possible. Including all cookies or headers effectively disables caching for most APIs since every request has unique cookie values.

## Route rewrites

Route rewrites let a single CDN distribution serve requests from multiple backends. Each rewrite matches a URL path pattern and routes matching requests to a different origin. Unmatched requests go to the default origin (the parent resource the CDN is attached to). Rewrites are evaluated in order — the first match wins.

Path patterns support wildcards, for example `/api/*`, `*.jpg`, and `/docs/v2/*`.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, HttpApiGateway, Bucket } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const assetsBucket = new Bucket({});
  const myApi = new HttpApiGateway({
    cdn: {
      enabled: true,
      routeRewrites: [
        {
          path: '/assets/*',
          routeTo: {
            type: 'bucket',
            properties: { bucketName: 'assetsBucket' }
          },
          cachingOptions: {
            defaultTTL: 86400
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { assetsBucket, myApi }
  };
});
```


Requests to `/assets/*` are served from the `assetsBucket` bucket with a 24-hour default cache TTL, while all other requests go to the API gateway. This is a common pattern for SPAs: serve static assets from a bucket and proxy API calls to a backend through the same domain.

In `routeTo.properties`, the names `bucketName`, `httpApiGatewayName`, `loadBalancerName`, and `functionName` refer to Stacktape resource names defined in `resources`, not generated AWS physical names. The name must match the key used when returning the resource from `defineConfig`.

Each route rewrite can independently override `cachingOptions`, `forwardingOptions`, and `edgeFunctions`, so you can cache static assets aggressively while passing API requests through uncached.

Route rewrites support five origin types:

| Origin type | `type` value | Routes to |
|---|---|---|
| S3 bucket | `bucket` | A `bucket` resource in your stack |
| HTTP API Gateway | `http-api-gateway` | An `http-api-gateway` resource |
| Application Load Balancer | `application-load-balancer` | An `application-load-balancer` resource |
| Lambda function | `function` | A `function` resource (must have `url.enabled: true`) |
| External domain | `custom-origin` | Any external domain (e.g., `api.example.com`) |

For the `custom-origin` type, you specify a `domainName`, optional `protocol` (`HTTP` or `HTTPS`, default `HTTPS`), and optional `port`. This is useful for routing part of your traffic to a legacy API or third-party service while keeping everything behind one CDN domain.

Bucket route targets support `disableUrlNormalization` (disables clean URL normalization like `/about` to `/about.html`, defaults to `false`). ALB route targets support `listenerPort` (for custom listener setups) and `originDomainName` (when the ALB has no custom domains and uses `customCertificateArns`).

## Forwarding

Forwarding options control which headers, cookies, and query parameters the CDN passes through to your origin. By default, all headers, cookies, and query parameters are forwarded. Restricting forwarding strips unnecessary data from reaching your backend. Cache hit rate is primarily controlled by `cachingOptions.cacheKeyParameters`; forwarded values only affect cache variants when they are also included in the cache key.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, HttpApiGateway } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myApi = new HttpApiGateway({
    cdn: {
      enabled: true,
      forwardingOptions: {
        cookies: { none: true },
        headers: { whitelist: ['Accept', 'Accept-Language'] },
        queryString: { all: true },
        customRequestHeaders: [{ headerName: 'X-Forwarded-By', value: 'cdn' }]
      }
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { myApi }
  };
});
```


This configuration strips all cookies, forwards only the `Accept` and `Accept-Language` headers, passes all query parameters, and injects a custom `X-Forwarded-By` header on every request sent to the origin. The `customRequestHeaders` property is useful for identifying CDN-routed traffic, injecting API keys, or passing custom identifiers.


> **Warning:** The `Authorization` header requires special handling. To forward it, you must add it to `cachingOptions.cacheKeyParameters.headers` — forwarding options alone do not cover `Authorization`.


For cookie forwarding, the supported modes are `none` (strip all cookies), `whitelist` (forward specific cookies), and `all` (forward all cookies). For query string forwarding: `all`, `none`, and `whitelist`. For header forwarding, several strategies are available: `none` (strip all), `whitelist` (specific headers), `allViewer` (all viewer headers), `allViewerAndWhitelistCloudFront` (viewer headers plus specific CloudFront headers like `CloudFront-Viewer-Country`), and `allExcept` (all viewer headers except listed ones). The CloudFront-specific headers are useful for geo-based routing or content localization. Keep forwarded values narrow — unnecessary cookies or query parameters add overhead, and if they are also part of the cache key, they reduce cache hit rates.

By default, all HTTP methods are forwarded. Use `allowedMethods` to restrict which methods reach the origin, for example `['GET', 'HEAD']` for read-only bucket content or `['GET', 'HEAD', 'OPTIONS', 'PUT', 'PATCH', 'POST', 'DELETE']` to explicitly allow all methods.

If you have a pre-existing CloudFront origin request policy, pass its ID via `originRequestPolicyId` instead of configuring forwarding options manually.

## Custom domains

[Set up the domain](/resources/networking/custom-domains#set-up-the-domain) with `stacktape domain add`, which also
creates the `us-east-1` certificate CloudFront needs. The deploy then attaches the certificate and creates the DNS
record. Without a custom domain, a CDN on a bucket, API Gateway or function uses its `*.cloudfront.net` URL. A CDN on
an Application Load Balancer needs a custom domain, or a `*.stacktape-app.com` domain from the
[Stacktape Console](/getting-started/add-the-console); without the Console connected, the deploy stops and asks for one.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, HttpApiGateway } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myApi = new HttpApiGateway({
    cdn: {
      enabled: true,
      customDomains: [{ domainName: 'api.example.com' }]
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { myApi }
  };
});
```


`disableDnsRecordCreation: true` skips the DNS record when DNS is elsewhere. A `customCertificateArn` must be in
`us-east-1`. `$ResourceParam('myApi', 'cdnCustomDomainUrls')` gives the domain URLs. See
[Custom domains](/resources/networking/custom-domains).

## Edge functions

`edgeFunctions` runs [edge functions](/resources/compute/edge-function), defined as their own resources, at four points
of a request:

| Hook | Runs | Typical use |
|---|---|---|
| `onRequest` | When CloudFront receives the request, before the cache | Auth checks, redirects, URL rewrites, A/B tests |
| `onResponse` | Before the response goes to the client (not after an origin error of 400 or more, or when `onRequest` answered) | Security headers, cookies |
| `onOriginRequest` | Before a cache miss goes to the origin | Change the origin path or headers |
| `onOriginResponse` | After the origin answers, before caching | Change the response that gets cached |

`onRequest` can answer by itself, for example with a 401, so the request never reaches the cache or the origin. Keep
business logic and database access in the origin: edge functions deploy to every location and are harder to debug.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  HttpApiGateway,
  EdgeLambdaFunction,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const authAtEdge = new EdgeLambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/edge/auth.ts'
    })
  });

  const securityHeaders = new EdgeLambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/edge/headers.ts'
    })
  });

  const myApi = new HttpApiGateway({
    cdn: {
      enabled: true,
      edgeFunctions: {
        onRequest: 'authAtEdge',
        onResponse: 'securityHeaders'
      }
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { authAtEdge, securityHeaders, myApi }
  };
});
```


> **Warning:** Stacktape uses `onOriginRequest` itself for bucket and API Gateway origins. Setting your own can break that
behavior.


Each route rewrite can run its own edge functions.

## Price class

`cloudfrontPriceClass` picks the CloudFront locations that serve your users. Fewer locations cost less per request and
are slower for users far from them.

| Price class | Locations |
|---|---|
| `PriceClass_100` | North America and Europe |
| `PriceClass_200` | Also Asia, the Middle East and Africa |
| `PriceClass_All` (default) | Everywhere, including South America and Oceania |

For users in North America and Europe, `PriceClass_100` is the cheapest with no latency cost for them.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, HttpApiGateway } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myApi = new HttpApiGateway({
    cdn: {
      enabled: true,
      cloudfrontPriceClass: 'PriceClass_100'
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { myApi }
  };
});
```

## Firewall

`useFirewall` names a [web application firewall](/resources/security/web-application-firewall) with `scope: 'cdn'` and
attaches it to the distribution, so blocked requests stop at CloudFront. A firewall costs about $7 a month with the
default rules plus $0.60 per million requests.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, HttpApiGateway, WebAppFirewall } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myFirewall = new WebAppFirewall({
    scope: 'cdn'
  });

  const myApi = new HttpApiGateway({
    cdn: {
      enabled: true,
      useFirewall: 'myFirewall'
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { myFirewall, myApi }
  };
});
```

## Cache invalidation

Stacktape clears the whole CDN cache on every deploy, so users see the new version at once. If your file names contain
a content hash, set `disableInvalidationAfterDeploy: true` to keep old files cached between deploys.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, HttpApiGateway } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myApi = new HttpApiGateway({
    cdn: {
      enabled: true,
      disableInvalidationAfterDeploy: true
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { myApi }
  };
});
```

## Basic example


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, HttpApiGateway } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myApi = new HttpApiGateway({
    customDomains: [{ domainName: 'api.example.com' }]
  });

  return {
    resources: { myApi }
  };
});
```


The deploy attaches the issued certificate that covers `api.example.com` and creates the DNS record in the
`example.com` zone. Open `https://api.example.com` after the deploy finishes. If no issued certificate covers the name,
the deploy stops before changing anything and says which certificate is missing.

Write `domainName` without a protocol: `api.example.com`, not `https://api.example.com`.

To give each stage its own hostname, build the name from the stage, for example `api-staging.example.com` for staging
and `api.example.com` for production. Every hostname must be covered by a certificate.

## TLS certificates

Leave out `customCertificateArn` and Stacktape picks the most specific certificate that covers the hostname, issued or
still pending validation: an exact match first, then a wildcard. If it picks a pending certificate, the deploy stops
until that certificate is issued, even when an issued wildcard also covers the name. Wait for validation to finish, or
set `customCertificateArn` to the issued certificate. Set `customCertificateArn` also to use a certificate you manage
yourself, for example one from a certificate authority your company requires.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, HttpApiGateway } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myApi = new HttpApiGateway({
    customDomains: [
      {
        domainName: 'api.example.com',
        customCertificateArn: 'arn:aws:acm:eu-west-1:123456789012:certificate/abc-def-123'
      }
    ]
  });

  return {
    resources: { myApi }
  };
});
```


The certificate must be in ACM in your AWS account and cover `domainName`. CDNs, AppSync and user pool domains need it
in `us-east-1`. API gateways and load balancers need it in the stack's region.

ACM renews DNS-validated certificates on its own while they're in use, as long as the validation records stay in DNS.
Imported certificates you renew yourself. See
[ACM renewal](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/acm/latest/userguide/dns-renewal-validation.html).

## External DNS

Keep DNS at your current provider, such as Cloudflare, when you want to keep managing records there or keep its proxy
and firewall. With `customCertificateArn` and `disableDnsRecordCreation: true`, Stacktape doesn't look for a Route 53
zone for that hostname.

1. Request a public certificate in ACM, in the region the resource needs. Include every hostname you'll serve: a
   certificate for `example.com` alone doesn't cover `app.example.com`.
2. Add the validation CNAME records that ACM shows at your DNS provider, and wait for **Issued**.
3. Set `customCertificateArn` and `disableDnsRecordCreation: true`, then deploy.
4. At your DNS provider, add a CNAME from your hostname to the AWS hostname:
   - **Load balancer**: **DNS name** in EC2 → Load Balancers.
   - **API Gateway**: **API Gateway domain name** in API Gateway → Custom domain names.
   - **CDN**: the CloudFront distribution's domain name.

A plain CNAME doesn't work on the bare `example.com`. Use your provider's ALIAS record or CNAME flattening there.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, ApplicationLoadBalancer } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myAlb = new ApplicationLoadBalancer({
    customDomains: [
      {
        domainName: 'app.example.com',
        customCertificateArn: 'arn:aws:acm:eu-west-1:123456789012:certificate/abc-def-123',
        disableDnsRecordCreation: true
      }
    ]
  });

  return {
    resources: { myAlb }
  };
});
```


For a user pool domain, the parent domain needs an
[A record](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/cognito-user-pools-add-custom-domain.html) before
the deploy; point the CNAME at the alias target Cognito shows. For AppSync, use the target AppSync shows.

If you bought the domain through Cloudflare Registrar, its DNS must stay at Cloudflare, so use this external DNS path.

## Multiple domains

A resource can serve several hostnames. Each entry has its own certificate and DNS settings:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, HttpApiGateway } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myApi = new HttpApiGateway({
    customDomains: [
      { domainName: 'api.example.com' },
      { domainName: 'api.mycompany.io' },
      {
        domainName: 'api-legacy.example.com',
        customCertificateArn: 'arn:aws:acm:eu-west-1:123456789012:certificate/abc-def-456',
        disableDnsRecordCreation: true
      }
    ]
  });

  return {
    resources: { myApi }
  };
});
```


Stacktape uses one hosted zone per root domain. Here `api.example.com` uses the `example.com` zone, and
`api.mycompany.io` needs a `mycompany.io` zone with its own `stacktape domain add` setup. `api-legacy.example.com` uses
external DNS, so Stacktape creates no record for it.

To send different paths of one hostname to different backends, attach the domain to one
[Application Load Balancer](/resources/networking/application-load-balancer) or
[HTTP API Gateway](/resources/networking/http-api-gateway) and route by path there.

## Basic example

The gateway itself holds no routes. Each function or container declares the method and path it serves, with an
[HTTP trigger](/resources/triggers/http-triggers) that names the gateway.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  HttpApiGateway,
  LambdaFunction,
  HttpApiIntegration,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myApi = new HttpApiGateway({});

  const getUsers = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/handlers/users.ts'
    }),
    events: [
      new HttpApiIntegration({
        httpApiGatewayName: 'myApi',
        method: 'GET',
        path: '/users'
      })
    ]
  });

  return {
    resources: { myApi, getUsers }
  };
});
```


The deploy prints the gateway URL (`https://<api-id>.execute-api.<region>.amazonaws.com`). `GET /users` on it now
invokes `getUsers`. In the config, `$ResourceParam('myApi', 'url')` gives the same URL.

### One function for all routes

When a framework such as Hono, Express or FastAPI does the routing, send every request to one function:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  HttpApiGateway,
  LambdaFunction,
  HttpApiIntegration,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myApi = new HttpApiGateway({});

  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/api.ts'
    }),
    events: [
      new HttpApiIntegration({
        httpApiGatewayName: 'myApi',
        method: '*',
        path: '/{proxy+}'
      })
    ]
  });

  return {
    resources: { myApi, api }
  };
});
```

## CORS

CORS lets a browser app on another origin call your API. With CORS on, the gateway answers preflight `OPTIONS`
requests itself and replaces any CORS headers your code sets.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, HttpApiGateway } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myApi = new HttpApiGateway({
    cors: {
      enabled: true,
      allowedOrigins: ['https://myapp.com', 'https://staging.myapp.com'],
      allowCredentials: true,
      maxAge: 86400
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { myApi }
  };
});
```


`enabled: true` alone allows every origin. Allowed methods come from the routes on the gateway, so you rarely set them.
`allowCredentials: true` is needed when the browser sends cookies or an `Authorization` header, and then you must list
the origins instead of using `*`. `maxAge` sets how long browsers cache the preflight answer, in seconds.

Server-side callers, mobile apps and same-origin frontends don't need CORS.

## Custom domains

After you [set up the domain](/resources/networking/custom-domains#set-up-the-domain) with `stacktape domain add`,
attach it to the gateway:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, HttpApiGateway } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myApi = new HttpApiGateway({
    customDomains: [{ domainName: 'api.example.com' }]
  });

  return {
    resources: { myApi }
  };
});
```


The deploy uses the issued certificate that covers `api.example.com` and creates the DNS record. To keep DNS at
another provider, see [External DNS](/resources/networking/custom-domains#external-dns).

## CDN

`cdn.enabled: true` puts a CloudFront distribution in front of the gateway. It helps when the API returns cacheable
public data or users are far from your region. For personalized responses it only adds CloudFront charges.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, HttpApiGateway } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myApi = new HttpApiGateway({
    cdn: {
      enabled: true,
      cloudfrontPriceClass: 'PriceClass_100'
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { myApi }
  };
});
```


`cloudfrontPriceClass` picks the CloudFront locations: `PriceClass_100` (North America and Europe, the cheapest),
`PriceClass_200` or `PriceClass_All`. See [CDN](/resources/networking/cdn) for caching and routing options.

## Access logging

The gateway logs every request (request ID, client IP, method, path, status, response size) and keeps the logs for 30
days. Read them with [`stacktape logs`](/cli/logs).


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, HttpApiGateway } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myApi = new HttpApiGateway({
    logging: {
      format: 'JSON',
      retentionDays: 90
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { myApi }
  };
});
```


`format` is `JSON` by default; `CLF`, `XML` and `CSV` are also accepted. `retentionDays` takes the CloudWatch values (1,
3, 5, 7, 14, 30, 60, 90, 120, 150, 180, 365, 400, 545, 731, 1827 or 3653). `logging.disabled: true` turns logging off,
and [log forwarding](/observability/log-forwarding) sends logs to another service.

## Payload format

The payload format is the shape of the event a function receives. Integrations use `1.0` unless you set `payloadFormat`
on the gateway or on the integration. `2.0` has a flatter event and is what AWS recommends for new code; Hono's Lambda
adapter handles both. All HTTP events of one function must use the same format.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, HttpApiGateway } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myApi = new HttpApiGateway({
    payloadFormat: '2.0'
  });

  return {
    resources: { myApi }
  };
});
```

## Alarms

Gateway alarms use the `http-api-gateway-error-rate` (share of 4xx and 5xx responses) and `http-api-gateway-latency`
triggers.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, HttpApiGateway } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const myApi = new HttpApiGateway({
    alarms: [
      {
        trigger: {
          type: 'http-api-gateway-error-rate',
          properties: { thresholdPercent: 5 }
        }
      },
      {
        trigger: {
          type: 'http-api-gateway-latency',
          properties: {
            thresholdMilliseconds: 3000,
            statistic: 'p95'
          }
        }
      }
    ]
  });

  return {
    resources: { myApi }
  };
});
```


The error-rate alarm fires above 5%, the latency alarm when p95 latency is above 3,000 ms. `statistic` applies to
latency only (`avg` by default). See [Alarms](/observability/alarms) for `evaluation` settings, comparison operators
and notification channels.

## Basic example

A Network Load Balancer needs at least one listener; there is no default.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, NetworkLoadBalancer } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const tcpLb = new NetworkLoadBalancer({
    listeners: [{ protocol: 'TCP', port: 5432 }]
  });

  return {
    resources: { tcpLb }
  };
});
```


This load balancer is reachable from the internet (`interface: 'internet'` is the default) and accepts TCP on port
5432. A container receives the connections with a `network-load-balancer` event that names the load balancer
(`loadBalancerName`), the listener (`listenerPort`) and its own port (`containerPort`). A
[web service](/resources/compute/web-service) with `loadBalancing.type: 'network-load-balancer'` creates its own.

## Listeners

Each listener has a `protocol` and a `port`:

- **TCP** passes connections through as they are. Use it when your app handles TLS itself, including mutual TLS or
  when it needs the client certificate.
- **TLS** decrypts at the load balancer and passes plain traffic to the container, so your app needs no certificate.
  The certificate comes from `customDomains` or from the listener's `customCertificateArns`.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, NetworkLoadBalancer } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const mqttBroker = new NetworkLoadBalancer({
    customDomains: [{ domainName: 'mqtt.example.com' }],
    listeners: [
      { protocol: 'TCP', port: 1883 },
      { protocol: 'TLS', port: 8883 }
    ]
  });

  return {
    resources: { mqttBroker }
  };
});
```


This load balancer takes plain MQTT on 1883 and MQTT over TLS on 8883. Each listener can have its own certificates and
IP allowlist.

Without `customDomains`, give a TLS listener your own ACM certificate:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, NetworkLoadBalancer } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const grpcLb = new NetworkLoadBalancer({
    listeners: [
      {
        protocol: 'TLS',
        port: 443,
        customCertificateArns: ['arn:aws:acm:us-east-1:123456789:certificate/abc-123']
      }
    ]
  });

  return {
    resources: { grpcLb }
  };
});
```


A TLS listener with neither gets a `*.stacktape-app.com` domain from the
[Stacktape Console](/getting-started/add-the-console); without the Console connected, the deploy stops and asks for a
domain or certificate.

## Custom domains

[Set up the domain](/resources/networking/custom-domains#set-up-the-domain) with `stacktape domain add`, then list it.
The deploy uses the issued certificate for the TLS listeners and creates the DNS record.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, NetworkLoadBalancer } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const tcpLb = new NetworkLoadBalancer({
    listeners: [{ protocol: 'TLS', port: 8883 }],
    customDomains: [{ domainName: 'tcp.example.com' }, { domainName: 'mqtt.example.com' }]
  });

  return {
    resources: { tcpLb }
  };
});
```


To keep DNS at another provider, set `disableDnsRecordCreation: true` and `customCertificateArn`; see
[External DNS](/resources/networking/custom-domains#external-dns).

### Internet vs internal

`interface: 'internal'` makes the load balancer reachable only from inside the VPC, for example for an internal gRPC
service.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, NetworkLoadBalancer } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const internalLb = new NetworkLoadBalancer({
    listeners: [{ protocol: 'TCP', port: 50051 }],
    interface: 'internal'
  });

  return {
    resources: { internalLb }
  };
});
```

### IP whitelisting

`whitelistIps` on a listener accepts connections only from the listed addresses and ranges. Each listener has its own
list.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, NetworkLoadBalancer } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const dbProxy = new NetworkLoadBalancer({
    listeners: [
      {
        protocol: 'TCP',
        port: 5432,
        whitelistIps: ['203.0.113.0/24', '198.51.100.42/32']
      }
    ]
  });

  return {
    resources: { dbProxy }
  };
});
```


A Network Load Balancer can't take a [firewall](/resources/security/web-application-firewall), so this allowlist is
the only filter at the load balancer. For rate limits or geo-blocking, use an Application Load Balancer.

## Basic example

Routes live on the functions, not on the gateway. Route handlers get the gateway's variables and permission to send to
its connections without `connectTo`.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  LambdaFunction,
  WebSocketApiGateway,
  WebSocketApiIntegration,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const realtime = new WebSocketApiGateway({});

  const connections = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/connections.ts'
    }),
    events: [
      new WebSocketApiIntegration({
        websocketApiGatewayName: realtime,
        routeKey: '$connect'
      }),
      new WebSocketApiIntegration({
        websocketApiGatewayName: realtime,
        routeKey: '$disconnect'
      })
    ]
  });

  const messages = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/messages.ts'
    }),
    events: [
      new WebSocketApiIntegration({
        websocketApiGatewayName: realtime,
        routeKey: 'sendMessage'
      }),
      new WebSocketApiIntegration({
        websocketApiGatewayName: realtime,
        routeKey: '$default'
      })
    ]
  });

  return { resources: { realtime, connections, messages } };
});
```


Example (YAML):

```yaml
resources:
  realtime:
    type: websocket-api-gateway

  connections:
    type: function
    properties:
      packaging:
        type: js-bundle
        properties:
          entryfilePath: ./src/connections.ts
      events:
        - type: websocket-api-gateway
          properties:
            websocketApiGatewayName: realtime
            routeKey: $connect
        - type: websocket-api-gateway
          properties:
            websocketApiGatewayName: realtime
            routeKey: $disconnect

  messages:
    type: function
    properties:
      packaging:
        type: js-bundle
        properties:
          entryfilePath: ./src/messages.ts
      events:
        - type: websocket-api-gateway
          properties:
            websocketApiGatewayName: realtime
            routeKey: sendMessage
        - type: websocket-api-gateway
          properties:
            websocketApiGatewayName: realtime
            routeKey: $default
```


The deploy prints the `wss://` URL. Connect to it with any WebSocket client, for example `npx wscat -c <url>`, and send
`{"action":"sendMessage","text":"hello"}`. Route changes deploy with the stack; there is no API Gateway stage to manage.

## Sending a message to a connection

For a gateway named `realtime`, every route handler receives:

- `STP_REALTIME_URL`: the public `wss://` client URL
- `STP_REALTIME_MANAGEMENT_ENDPOINT`: the HTTPS endpoint for `ApiGatewayManagementApiClient`
- `STP_REALTIME_API_ID`: the API ID
- `STP_REALTIME_CANONICAL_DOMAIN`: the AWS DNS target

The handler also gets connection-management permission scoped to this gateway and Stacktape's managed stage.

```ts
import {
  ApiGatewayManagementApiClient,
  PostToConnectionCommand
} from '@aws-sdk/client-apigatewaymanagementapi';

const client = new ApiGatewayManagementApiClient({
  endpoint: process.env.STP_REALTIME_MANAGEMENT_ENDPOINT
});

export default async (event) => {
  await client.send(
    new PostToConnectionCommand({
      ConnectionId: event.requestContext.connectionId,
      Data: Buffer.from(JSON.stringify({ type: 'ack' }))
    })
  );

  return { statusCode: 200 };
};
```

Do not post to the connection from `$connect`: the connection does not exist until that handler succeeds. Handle `GoneException` when a connection has already closed.

Other producers can send to clients too. Add `connectTo: [realtime]` to a different Lambda, container workload, batch job, or script to receive the same scoped permission and environment variables. The permission covers `PostToConnection`, `GetConnection` and `DeleteConnection` on every connection of the gateway's stage.

## Authorization

Authorization is optional and belongs on the `$connect` route because API Gateway authorizes a WebSocket connection only while opening it. Later messages use the identity established at connection time and any checks in your application code.

For application-specific authentication, use a Lambda REQUEST authorizer:

```ts
const connect = new LambdaFunction({
  packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
    entryfilePath: './src/connect.ts'
  }),
  events: [
    new WebSocketApiIntegration({
      websocketApiGatewayName: realtime,
      routeKey: '$connect',
      authorizer: {
        type: 'lambda',
        properties: {
          functionName: authorizeConnection
        }
      }
    })
  ]
});
```

The default identity source is `route.request.header.Authorization`. You can explicitly use header or query-string sources such as `route.request.querystring.token`. A WebSocket Lambda authorizer returns an IAM policy response; the HTTP API simple-response and JWT/Cognito authorizer settings do not apply.

Use `{ type: 'aws-iam' }` only for clients that can sign the opening request with AWS Signature Version 4. It is usually unsuitable for ordinary browser clients.

## Return a response to the calling client

WebSocket routes are one-way by default. For a simple request-response interaction on `$default` or a custom message route, set `returnResponse: true` and return a `body` from the Lambda handler:

```ts
new WebSocketApiIntegration({
  websocketApiGatewayName: realtime,
  routeKey: 'getStatus',
  returnResponse: true
});
```

```ts
export default async () => ({
  statusCode: 200,
  body: JSON.stringify({ status: 'ready' })
});
```

Stacktape configures the API Gateway route response, and only the client that invoked the route receives the returned body. Lifecycle routes (`$connect` and `$disconnect`) cannot use `returnResponse`, because no established calling connection is available for that reply. Keep the default for broadcasts, delayed responses, and messages sent to a different connection; use the management endpoint for those cases.

## Basic example

Two [Lambda functions](/resources/compute/lambda-function) in one workflow: it validates an order, branches on the
result, and either processes the order or fails.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  LambdaFunction,
  StateMachine,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging,
  $ResourceParam
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const validateOrder = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/validate-order.ts' })
  });

  const processOrder = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/process-order.ts' }),
    timeout: 60
  });

  const orderWorkflow = new StateMachine({
    definition: {
      Comment: 'Order processing workflow',
      StartAt: 'ValidateOrder',
      States: {
        ValidateOrder: {
          Type: 'Task',
          Resource: $ResourceParam('validateOrder', 'arn'),
          Next: 'CheckValidity',
          Catch: [{ ErrorEquals: ['States.ALL'], Next: 'OrderFailed' }]
        },
        CheckValidity: {
          Type: 'Choice',
          Choices: [{ Variable: '$.valid', BooleanEquals: true, Next: 'ProcessOrder' }],
          Default: 'OrderFailed'
        },
        ProcessOrder: {
          Type: 'Task',
          Resource: $ResourceParam('processOrder', 'arn'),
          Retry: [
            {
              ErrorEquals: ['States.TaskFailed'],
              IntervalSeconds: 3,
              MaxAttempts: 2,
              BackoffRate: 2
            }
          ],
          End: true
        },
        OrderFailed: {
          Type: 'Fail',
          Error: 'OrderProcessingFailed',
          Cause: 'Validation or processing failed'
        }
      }
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { validateOrder, processOrder, orderWorkflow }
  };
});
```


`StartAt` names the first state and `States` lists them. Each state has a `Type` and moves on with `Next` or ends with
`End: true`; `Succeed` and `Fail` end the execution by themselves. A Task state names its function with
`$ResourceParam('validateOrder', 'arn')`, which becomes the function's ARN at deploy time.

After the deploy, start an execution in the AWS console (**Step Functions → your state machine → Start execution**)
with input such as `{"orderId": "42"}`, and watch each step's input and output in the execution graph.

## Error handling

AWS Step Functions provides built-in error handling through `Retry` and `Catch` blocks on Task, Parallel, and Map states. This replaces the need for custom retry logic in your Lambda function code and gives you a declarative way to handle failures at the orchestration layer.

**Retry** accepts an array of rules, each specifying which errors to match (`ErrorEquals`), the initial delay (`IntervalSeconds`), maximum attempts (`MaxAttempts`), and backoff multiplier (`BackoffRate`). Multiple retry rules are evaluated in order — the first matching rule is used.

**Catch** routes to a fallback state when all retries are exhausted or for errors not covered by retry rules. Each catch rule matches error types (`ErrorEquals`) and redirects to a named state (`Next`). This lets you build graceful degradation paths, for example notifying a support team when payment processing fails after retries.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  LambdaFunction,
  StateMachine,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging,
  $ResourceParam
} from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const chargePayment = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/charge.ts' }),
    timeout: 60
  });

  const notifySupport = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/notify-support.ts' })
  });
  const paymentWorkflow = new StateMachine({
    definition: {
      StartAt: 'ChargePayment',
      States: {
        ChargePayment: {
          Type: 'Task',
          Resource: $ResourceParam('chargePayment', 'arn'),
          Retry: [
            {
              ErrorEquals: ['States.TaskFailed'],
              IntervalSeconds: 5,
              MaxAttempts: 3,
              BackoffRate: 2
            }
          ],
          Catch: [
            {
              ErrorEquals: ['States.ALL'],
              Next: 'NotifySupport'
            }
          ],
          Next: 'PaymentComplete'
        },
        NotifySupport: {
          Type: 'Task',
          Resource: $ResourceParam('notifySupport', 'arn'),
          Next: 'PaymentFailed'
        },
        PaymentFailed: {
          Type: 'Fail',
          Error: 'PaymentError',
          Cause: 'Payment failed after 3 retries'
        },
        PaymentComplete: {
          Type: 'Succeed'
        }
      }
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { chargePayment, notifySupport, paymentWorkflow }
  };
});
```


In this example, `ChargePayment` retries up to 3 times on failure with exponential backoff (5s, 10s, 20s). If all retries fail, `Catch` redirects to `NotifySupport`, which alerts the support team before the workflow terminates with a `Fail` state. Without the retry block, any transient error would immediately fail the workflow.

Retry and Catch match error strings in `ErrorEquals`. Common ASL error names include `States.ALL`, `States.TaskFailed`, `States.Timeout`, and `States.HeartbeatTimeout`. You can also match custom error strings thrown by your Lambda functions. See the [ASL specification](https://states-language.net/spec.html) for the complete list and matching rules.

## Starting executions

To start executions from your code, add the state machine to the workload's
[`connectTo`](/configuration/connecting-resources). The workload's role can then start, stop and describe executions,
and it gets `STP_ORDER_WORKFLOW_ARN` and `STP_ORDER_WORKFLOW_NAME`.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  LambdaFunction,
  StateMachine,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging,
  $ResourceParam
} from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const processOrder = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/process-order.ts' })
  });

  const orderWorkflow = new StateMachine({
    definition: {
      StartAt: 'ProcessOrder',
      States: {
        ProcessOrder: {
          Type: 'Task',
          Resource: $ResourceParam('processOrder', 'arn'),
          End: true
        }
      }
    }
  });
  const startOrder = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/start-order.ts' }),
    connectTo: [orderWorkflow]
  });

  return {
    resources: { processOrder, orderWorkflow, startOrder }
  };
});
```


Use the ARN in your handler to start executions:

```typescript
import { Hono } from 'hono';
import { handle } from 'hono/aws-lambda';
import { SFNClient, StartExecutionCommand, DescribeExecutionCommand } from '@aws-sdk/client-sfn';

const sfn = new SFNClient({});
const stateMachineArn = process.env.STP_ORDER_WORKFLOW_ARN!;

const app = new Hono();

app.post('/orders', async (c) => {
  const input = await c.req.text() || '{}';
  const result = await sfn.send(
    new StartExecutionCommand({ stateMachineArn, input })
  );
  return c.json({ executionArn: result.executionArn }, 202);
});

app.get('/orders/status/:executionArn{.+}', async (c) => {
  const executionArn = decodeURIComponent(c.req.param('executionArn'));
  const result = await sfn.send(new DescribeExecutionCommand({ executionArn }));
  return c.json({
    status: result.status,
    output: result.output ? JSON.parse(result.output) : null
  });
});

export default handle(app);
```

The `POST` route starts an execution and returns its ARN; the `GET` route reports its status and output. Attach
`startOrder` to an [HTTP API Gateway](/resources/networking/http-api-gateway) to call them.

## Express workflows and other settings

The resource exposes only `definition`. Set anything else, such as the workflow type or logging, with
[overrides](/configuration/overrides-and-escape-hatches) of the `AWS::StepFunctions::StateMachine` child resource.

An Express workflow suits high-volume, short work: it runs for at most five minutes and bills per request and duration
instead of per transition. [`stacktape synth`](/cli/synth) shows the child resource's logical name; the example assumes
`OrderWorkflowStateMachine`.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, StateMachine } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const orderWorkflow = new StateMachine({
    definition: {
      StartAt: 'Process',
      States: {
        Process: { Type: 'Pass', End: true }
      }
    },
    overrides: {
      OrderWorkflowStateMachine: {
        StateMachineType: 'EXPRESS'
      }
    }
  });

  return { resources: { orderWorkflow } };
});
```

## Basic example


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Bastion } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const bastion = new Bastion({});

  return {
    resources: { bastion }
  };
});
```


The deploy starts one Amazon Linux 2023 instance in a public subnet. Nothing on the internet can connect to it; Systems Manager
sessions go out from the instance.

Open a tunnel from your machine to a private database:

```bash
stacktape bastion tunnel mainDatabase --stage production
```

The command prints a `localhost` address for each endpoint of the resource. Point `psql`, DBeaver, TablePlus or your
ORM at it and keep the command running while you work. A Redis cache with a reader endpoint gets two local ports.

## Instance size

`instanceSize` is `t3.micro` by default, which is enough for tunnels and admin work. Pick a larger size only if you run
heavy scripts on the bastion.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Bastion } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const bastion = new Bastion({
    instanceSize: 't3.small'
  });

  return {
    resources: { bastion }
  };
});
```

## Startup commands

`runCommandsAtLaunch` runs commands as root when the instance starts, for example to install database clients. The
instance runs Amazon Linux 2023, so use `dnf` or `yum`.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Bastion } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const bastion = new Bastion({
    runCommandsAtLaunch: ['yum install -y postgresql15', 'yum install -y redis6']
  });

  return {
    resources: { bastion }
  };
});
```


> **Warning:** Changing `runCommandsAtLaunch` replaces the instance on the next deploy, and files on the old one are lost. Don't keep
anything on the bastion you can't recreate.

## Logging

The bastion sends three system logs to CloudWatch, each with its own retention:

| Log type | Source | Default retention | Contains |
|---|---|---|---|
| `messages` | `/var/log/messages` | 30 days | Startup, kernel and service messages |
| `secure` | `/var/log/secure` | 180 days | Sign-ins and authentication events |
| `audit` | `/var/log/audit/audit.log` | 365 days | Linux audit events |


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Bastion } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const bastion = new Bastion({
    logging: {
      messages: { retentionDays: 14 },
      secure: { retentionDays: 365 },
      audit: { disabled: true }
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { bastion }
  };
});
```


Keep `secure` and `audit` longer when you need an audit trail, and shorten or turn them off for development stacks.
Each log type also supports [log forwarding](/observability/log-forwarding).

## Basic example

A pool needs no properties; Stacktape creates the Cognito user pool and an app client. Most apps set at least how new
users verify their email or phone:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, UserAuthPool } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const auth = new UserAuthPool({
    userVerificationType: 'email-code'
  });

  return {
    resources: { auth }
  };
});
```


New users verify their email with a one-time code. They can sign in with their email or phone number
(`allowEmailAsUserName` and `allowPhoneNumberAsUserName` are `true` by default). The deploy prints nothing to open; your
app uses the pool through `connectTo` or the [Hosted UI](#hosted-ui).

### Admin-only accounts

For internal tools, B2B dashboards, or any app where you do not want public sign-ups, set `allowOnlyAdminsToCreateAccount: true`. Accounts must then be created through an admin flow (using the Cognito `AdminCreateUser` API from your backend).


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, UserAuthPool } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const auth = new UserAuthPool({
    userVerificationType: 'email-code',
    allowOnlyAdminsToCreateAccount: true,
    unusedAccountValidityDays: 7
  });

  return {
    resources: { auth }
  };
});
```


Use `unusedAccountValidityDays` to control how long an unused admin-created account stays valid if the user never signs in. Setting it to `7` means the temporary password and account expire after 7 days. The default is 31 days. This is separate from `passwordPolicy.temporaryPasswordValidityDays`, which controls how long a temporary password is valid before it must be changed on first sign-in.

## Connecting to resources

To use the user pool from a [Lambda function](/resources/compute/lambda-function), [web service](/resources/compute/web-service), or any other compute resource, add it to the resource's [`connectTo`](/configuration/connecting-resources) list. For a `UserAuthPool`, `connectTo` grants full-control permissions and injects `ID`, `CLIENT_ID`, and `ARN` environment variables.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  UserAuthPool,
  LambdaFunction,
  HttpApiGateway,
  HttpApiIntegration,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const auth = new UserAuthPool({
    userVerificationType: 'email-code'
  });

  const gateway = new HttpApiGateway({});

  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts'
    }),
    connectTo: [auth],
    events: [
      new HttpApiIntegration({
        httpApiGatewayName: 'gateway',
        method: '*',
        path: '/{proxy+}'
      })
    ]
  });

  return {
    resources: { auth, gateway, api }
  };
});
```


For a resource named `auth`, the following environment variables are injected by `connectTo`:

| Variable | Description |
|---|---|
| `STP_AUTH_ID` | Cognito User Pool ID |
| `STP_AUTH_CLIENT_ID` | User Pool Client ID |
| `STP_AUTH_ARN` | User Pool ARN |

`$ResourceParam('auth', 'domain')` gives the Hosted UI domain.

Your application code can pass the injected pool ID, client ID, and ARN to AWS SDKs or Cognito-compatible libraries. For example, backend code commonly uses Cognito APIs for admin user creation or authentication flows. Libraries like `amazon-cognito-identity-js` or `@aws-sdk/client-cognito-identity-provider` accept the pool ID and client ID directly.

## Protecting API routes

A Stacktape user authentication pool integrates with [HTTP API Gateway](/resources/networking/http-api-gateway) to protect routes using JWT validation. With a `cognito` authorizer on an HTTP trigger, API Gateway checks the bearer token on every request before your code
runs, and answers `401` when it's missing or invalid.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  UserAuthPool,
  LambdaFunction,
  HttpApiGateway,
  HttpApiIntegration,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const auth = new UserAuthPool({
    userVerificationType: 'email-code'
  });

  const gateway = new HttpApiGateway({});

  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts'
    }),
    connectTo: [auth],
    events: [
      new HttpApiIntegration({
        httpApiGatewayName: 'gateway',
        method: 'GET',
        path: '/me',
        authorizer: {
          type: 'cognito',
          properties: {
            userPoolName: 'auth'
          }
        }
      })
    ]
  });

  return {
    resources: { auth, gateway, api }
  };
});
```


The `cognito` authorizer type validates JWTs issued by the referenced user pool. Stacktape uses `userPoolName` to set the expected audience to the user pool client ID and build the issuer URL from the user pool and AWS region. By default, the token is read from the `Authorization` header. You can customize this with the `identitySources` property on the authorizer to read from a different header, query parameter, or stage variable using the `$request.*` syntax.

Stacktape also supports a `lambda` authorizer type, where a [Lambda function](/resources/compute/lambda-function) runs custom authorization logic for each request. Use it for checks beyond the JWT, for example API keys, permissions from a database or another identity provider. See [HTTP triggers](/resources/triggers/http-triggers) for details on both authorizer types.

## Identity providers

A Stacktape user authentication pool supports six types of external identity providers, allowing users to sign in with existing accounts instead of creating new credentials:

| Provider type | Use case |
|---|---|
| `Google` | Consumer and business apps |
| `Facebook` | Consumer apps, especially social or e-commerce |
| `SignInWithApple` | Required for iOS apps that offer other social logins |
| `LoginWithAmazon` | E-commerce or Alexa-integrated apps |
| `OIDC` | Any OpenID Connect provider (Okta, Azure AD, etc.) |
| `SAML` | Enterprise SSO (Active Directory, Ping Identity, etc.) |

Every provider needs a `clientId` and a `clientSecret`. For social and OIDC providers, obtain them from the provider's developer console. For SAML and OIDC providers, advanced provider-specific options can be passed through `providerDetails` as Cognito `ProviderDetails` values. Store secrets using the [`$Secret` directive](/configuration/directives) to avoid exposing them in your config. See [secrets](/configuration/secrets) or [`stacktape secret set`](/cli/secret-set) for setup.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, UserAuthPool } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const auth = new UserAuthPool({
    userVerificationType: 'email-code',
    enableHostedUi: true,
    hostedUiDomainPrefix: 'myapp-login',
    callbackURLs: ['https://myapp.com/auth/callback'],
    logoutURLs: ['https://myapp.com/logged-out'],
    identityProviders: [
      {
        type: 'Google',
        clientId: "$Secret('google-credentials', 'clientId')",
        clientSecret: "$Secret('google-credentials', 'clientSecret')"
      }
    ]
  });

  return {
    resources: { auth }
  };
});
```


When you add an identity provider, Stacktape creates separate `AWS::Cognito::UserPoolIdentityProvider` resources and registers them in the user pool client's `SupportedIdentityProviders`. If you do not specify `attributeMapping`, Stacktape defaults to mapping `email` from the provider to the Cognito `email` attribute. You can override this with the `attributeMapping` property to map additional attributes like `given_name` or `picture`.

If you omit `authorizeScopes`, Stacktape uses a reasonable default per provider. Set `authorizeScopes` explicitly when your app needs specific provider scopes, for example requesting access to a user's Google Calendar or Facebook friend list.

Set `allowOnlyExternalIdentityProviders: true` only after configuring at least one `identityProviders` entry. Stacktape then removes `COGNITO` from the user pool client's `SupportedIdentityProviders`, so users must sign in through the configured external providers.

## Lambda hooks

Cognito triggers let you run custom logic at key points in the authentication lifecycle. The `hooks` property connects [Lambda function](/resources/compute/lambda-function) ARNs to specific trigger points. Use [`$ResourceParam`](/configuration/referenceable-parameters) to reference a Lambda function from your stack:

| Hook | When it runs | Common use case |
|---|---|---|
| `preSignUp` | Before a new user is created | Validate input, auto-confirm trusted domains, block sign-ups |
| `postConfirmation` | After account confirmation | Create records in your database, provision resources |
| `preAuthentication` | Before credential validation | Block sign-in by IP, device, or user state |
| `postAuthentication` | After successful sign-in | Update last-login timestamps, record analytics |
| `preTokenGeneration` | Before tokens are issued | Add custom claims (roles, permissions) to JWTs |
| `customMessage` | Before sending email/SMS | Customize message content, localize text |
| `userMigration` | When an unknown user signs in | Import users on-the-fly from a legacy system |
| `createAuthChallenge` | During custom auth flow | Generate a custom challenge (e.g., OTP) |
| `defineAuthChallenge` | During custom auth flow | Decide whether a challenge is needed |
| `verifyAuthChallengeResponse` | During custom auth flow | Validate the user's challenge response |

Each hook value must be a Lambda function ARN. Stacktape maps the configured hooks to the Cognito user pool `LambdaConfig`.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  UserAuthPool,
  LambdaFunction,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const createProfile = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/create-profile.ts'
    })
  });

  const auth = new UserAuthPool({
    userVerificationType: 'email-code',
    hooks: {
      postConfirmation: "$ResourceParam('createProfile', 'arn')"
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { createProfile, auth }
  };
});
```


Here `createProfile` runs after each user confirms their account, for example to add the user to your own database. The `$ResourceParam('createProfile', 'arn')` directive resolves to the Lambda ARN at deploy time.

## Multi-factor authentication

MFA adds a second verification step during sign-in, making it harder for attackers to access accounts even if they know a user's password. Stacktape supports three MFA factor types:

| Factor | How it works | Requirements |
|---|---|---|
| `SOFTWARE_TOKEN` | Time-based one-time codes from an authenticator app (Google Authenticator, Authy) | None |
| `SMS` | One-time codes sent via text message | Requires an SNS role so Cognito can send SMS |
| `EMAIL_OTP` | One-time codes sent via email | Requires `emailConfiguration.sesAddressArn` with a verified SES identity |

The `mfaConfiguration.status` property controls enforcement: `ON` requires MFA for all users, `OPTIONAL` lets users opt in, and `OFF` disables MFA entirely. For most apps, start with `OPTIONAL` and move to `ON` once your users have enrolled their second factor.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, UserAuthPool } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const auth = new UserAuthPool({
    userVerificationType: 'email-code',
    mfaConfiguration: {
      status: 'OPTIONAL',
      enabledTypes: ['SOFTWARE_TOKEN']
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { auth }
  };
});
```


`SOFTWARE_TOKEN` is the best default: no per-message cost, works everywhere and needs no SES or SNS setup. Use `SMS` only when your users might not have access to authenticator apps, keeping in mind the per-message SMS cost. Use `EMAIL_OTP` when you already have SES configured and want a low-friction second factor that does not require a separate authenticator app.

## Hosted UI

Cognito provides a pre-built, hosted login and registration page so you do not have to build your own auth screens. Enable it with `enableHostedUi: true`. The Hosted UI is useful when you want authentication screens outside your main app. If you use OAuth redirects, configure `callbackURLs` and `logoutURLs` explicitly.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, UserAuthPool } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const auth = new UserAuthPool({
    enableHostedUi: true,
    hostedUiDomainPrefix: 'myapp-auth',
    userVerificationType: 'email-code',
    callbackURLs: ['https://myapp.com/auth/callback'],
    logoutURLs: ['https://myapp.com/logged-out'],
    allowedOAuthFlows: ['code'],
    allowedOAuthScopes: ['email', 'openid', 'profile']
  });

  return {
    resources: { auth }
  };
});
```


The `hostedUiDomainPrefix` sets the first part of the Hosted UI URL: `https://<prefix>.auth.<region>.amazoncognito.com`. Choose a prefix that matches your project name. Cognito accepts 1 to 63 lowercase letters, digits and hyphens, starting and ending with a letter or digit, and rejects a prefix that contains `aws`, `amazon` or `cognito`; Stacktape checks this before deploying. Without `hostedUiDomainPrefix`, the prefix is `<stack name>-<pool name>`. When Cognito would reject that, for example for a pool named `cognitoPool`, a new pool gets the same name without the rejected parts and with a short hash. A deployed pool keeps the prefix it has. Use `hostedUiCSS` to provide custom CSS for the Hosted UI, applied via Cognito's `AWS::Cognito::UserPoolUICustomizationAttachment` resource.

**When to enable the Hosted UI:** Use it when you want to ship authentication quickly without building custom UI. It is especially useful with social login providers, since the Hosted UI handles the OAuth redirect flow for you. **When to skip it:** For full control of the sign-in screens, build your own UI on the Cognito SDK against the same pool.

### Custom domain

By default, the Hosted UI is served from a Cognito-managed subdomain. To use your own domain (e.g., `auth.example.com`), configure `customDomain`:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, UserAuthPool } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const auth = new UserAuthPool({
    enableHostedUi: true,
    userVerificationType: 'email-code',
    callbackURLs: ['https://example.com/auth/callback'],
    logoutURLs: ['https://example.com/logged-out'],
    customDomain: {
      domainName: 'auth.example.com'
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { auth }
  };
});
```


Use your own domain when users see the sign-in URL, for example in login redirects. Otherwise the Cognito subdomain
from `hostedUiDomainPrefix` needs no DNS or certificate.

Cognito serves custom domains through CloudFront, so the certificate must be in `us-east-1`. Run
[`stacktape domain add`](/resources/networking/custom-domains#set-up-the-domain) before the first deploy; it creates one
there. Cognito also requires the parent domain (`example.com` for `auth.example.com`) to have an A record. Stacktape
creates the DNS record for `customDomain` unless you set `disableDnsRecordCreation: true`, and `customCertificateArn`
picks a specific certificate.

## Password policy

Stacktape lets you configure password strength requirements through the `passwordPolicy` property. You can set `minimumLength` and require specific character classes (`requireLowercase`, `requireUppercase`, `requireNumbers`, `requireSymbols`).


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, UserAuthPool } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const auth = new UserAuthPool({
    userVerificationType: 'email-code',
    passwordPolicy: {
      minimumLength: 12,
      requireNumbers: true,
      requireUppercase: true,
      requireLowercase: true,
      temporaryPasswordValidityDays: 3
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { auth }
  };
});
```


The `temporaryPasswordValidityDays` inside `passwordPolicy` controls how long a temporary password issued to a new user is valid before it must be changed on first sign-in. This is distinct from the top-level `unusedAccountValidityDays`, which controls when the entire unused admin-created account expires if the user never signs in at all.

Without `passwordPolicy`, Cognito's own defaults apply. For production, set at least `minimumLength: 12` with
`requireNumbers` and `requireUppercase`.

## Custom attributes

The `schema` property lets you define custom attributes stored on each user record. These are useful for data like `role`, `plan`, `tenantId`, or `companyName` that you want attached to the user profile and optionally included in JWT claims (via the `preTokenGeneration` hook).


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, UserAuthPool } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const auth = new UserAuthPool({
    userVerificationType: 'email-code',
    schema: [
      {
        name: 'role',
        attributeDataType: 'String',
        mutable: true,
        stringMinLength: 1,
        stringMaxLength: 50
      },
      {
        name: 'tenantId',
        attributeDataType: 'String',
        required: true,
        mutable: false,
        stringMinLength: 1,
        stringMaxLength: 128
      },
      {
        name: 'loginCount',
        attributeDataType: 'Number',
        mutable: true
      }
    ]
  });

  return {
    resources: { auth }
  };
});
```


Each attribute specifies a `name`, `attributeDataType` (e.g., `String`, `Number`), and optional constraints like `required`, `mutable`, `stringMinLength`, and `stringMaxLength`. Attributes with `developerOnlyAttribute: true` are readable only from your backend, not by users.


> **Warning:** Cognito user pool custom attribute schemas are difficult to change after creation. Plan the schema carefully before deploying to production.

## Firewall

Stacktape user authentication pools can be protected by a [web application firewall](/resources/security/web-application-firewall) that inspects requests to the Hosted UI and Cognito token endpoints. `useFirewall` is the Stacktape resource name of a `web-app-firewall` resource; it is not an inline WAF rule definition. Set it to the name of a `WebAppFirewall` resource in your stack.

`useFirewall` creates a `WebACLAssociation` between the user pool and the referenced firewall. AWS requires a regional WAF web ACL for Cognito user pool associations, so the referenced `WebAppFirewall` resource must use `scope: 'regional'`. A CDN-scoped firewall targets CloudFront and cannot be associated with a Cognito user pool. See the [web application firewall](/resources/security/web-application-firewall) page for firewall scope configuration.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, UserAuthPool, WebAppFirewall } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const firewall = new WebAppFirewall({
    scope: 'regional'
  });

  const auth = new UserAuthPool({
    userVerificationType: 'email-code',
    useFirewall: 'firewall'
  });

  return {
    resources: { firewall, auth }
  };
});
```


**When to enable a firewall:** Use it when your Hosted UI is publicly accessible and you want to protect against credential stuffing, bot traffic, or IP-based attacks. A firewall costs about $7 a month with the default rules, plus $0.60 per million requests. **When to skip it:** For internal-only apps or apps that use custom UI with backend-only Cognito API calls, the firewall adds cost without significant benefit.

## Basic example


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebAppFirewall } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const firewall = new WebAppFirewall({
    scope: 'regional'
  });

  return {
    resources: { firewall }
  };
});
```


With no `rules`, the firewall uses two AWS-managed rule groups, `AWSManagedRulesCommonRuleSet` and
`AWSManagedRulesKnownBadInputsRuleSet`, and lets through every request they don't block. Attach it by name from the
resource it protects:

| Scope | Attach it with | To |
|---|---|---|
| `regional` | `useFirewall` | [Application Load Balancer](/resources/networking/application-load-balancer), [web service](/resources/compute/web-service) with an Application Load Balancer, [user auth pool](/resources/security/user-auth-pool) |
| `cdn` | `cdn.useFirewall` or `useFirewall` | [CDN](/resources/networking/cdn), the CDN of a load balancer, HTTP API Gateway, function or bucket, [static hosting](/resources/frontend/static-hosting) and the SSR frontends |

`scope` is required. A `cdn` firewall works at CloudFront locations and stops bad requests before they reach your
region; Stacktape creates it in `us-east-1`, where AWS keeps CloudFront firewalls. A firewall can't change scope after
its first deploy, so create a new one instead.

HTTP API Gateways and Network Load Balancers can't take a firewall directly. Put a CDN in front of an HTTP API Gateway
and attach a `cdn` firewall to it.

### Managed rule groups

Managed rule groups are rule sets maintained by AWS or by vendors on AWS Marketplace. For AWS groups, set
`vendorName: 'AWS'`.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebAppFirewall } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const firewall = new WebAppFirewall({
    scope: 'regional',
    rules: [
      {
        type: 'managed-rule-group',
        properties: {
          name: 'AWSManagedRulesCommonRuleSet',
          vendorName: 'AWS',
          priority: 1
        }
      },
      {
        type: 'managed-rule-group',
        properties: {
          name: 'AWSManagedRulesKnownBadInputsRuleSet',
          vendorName: 'AWS',
          priority: 2
        }
      },
      {
        type: 'managed-rule-group',
        properties: {
          name: 'AWSManagedRulesSQLiRuleSet',
          vendorName: 'AWS',
          priority: 3
        }
      }
    ]
  });

  return {
    resources: { firewall }
  };
});
```


| Rule group | Blocks |
|---|---|
| `AWSManagedRulesCommonRuleSet` | Cross-site scripting, path traversal, file inclusion, oversized requests |
| `AWSManagedRulesKnownBadInputsRuleSet` | Known exploit payloads |
| `AWSManagedRulesSQLiRuleSet` | SQL injection in the query, body, cookies and headers |
| `AWSManagedRulesAmazonIpReputationList` | IP addresses Amazon threat intelligence flags |
| `AWSManagedRulesBotControlRuleSet` | Bots, by category (extra monthly and per-request fees) |

AWS changes the rules inside these groups over time; see the
[AWS list of managed rule groups](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/waf/latest/developerguide/aws-managed-rule-groups-list.html).

To try a group without blocking anything, set `overrideAction: 'Count'`: matching requests are only counted. `'None'`
enforces the group. If one rule of a group blocks legitimate requests, list it in `excludedRules`; you find its name in
the sampled requests in the AWS WAF console.

### Rate-based rules

A rate-based rule limits how many requests one IP address can send in a rolling five-minute window.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebAppFirewall } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const firewall = new WebAppFirewall({
    scope: 'regional',
    rules: [
      {
        type: 'managed-rule-group',
        properties: {
          name: 'AWSManagedRulesCommonRuleSet',
          vendorName: 'AWS',
          priority: 1
        }
      },
      {
        type: 'rate-based-rule',
        properties: {
          name: 'rateLimiter',
          priority: 10,
          limit: 2000,
          action: 'Block'
        }
      }
    ]
  });

  return {
    resources: { firewall }
  };
});
```


`limit` takes 100 to 20,000,000. 2,000 means about 6 to 7 requests per second, sustained. `action` is `'Block'` (a 403
response) by default; `'Count'` only counts, which helps you find the right limit, and `'Captcha'` or `'Challenge'`
make the client prove it's a browser.

Behind a proxy or CDN, every request comes from the proxy's address. Set `aggregateBasedOn: 'FORWARDED_IP'` and
`forwardedIPConfig` with the header (usually `X-Forwarded-For`) and a `fallbackBehavior` for requests without it
(`'MATCH'` or `'NO_MATCH'`).

### Custom rule groups

A custom rule group uses a WAF rule group that already exists in your account, by ARN, for example one another team
maintains. It takes `overrideAction` like a managed group.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebAppFirewall } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const firewall = new WebAppFirewall({
    scope: 'regional',
    rules: [
      {
        type: 'custom-rule-group',
        properties: {
          name: 'teamSharedRules',
          priority: 5,
          arn: 'arn:aws:wafv2:eu-west-1:123456789012:regional/rulegroup/my-shared-rules/abc123'
        }
      }
    ]
  });

  return {
    resources: { firewall }
  };
});
```

## Custom response bodies

A blocked request gets a plain 403 by default. `customResponseBodies` defines named bodies, for example a JSON error
for an API or an HTML page for a website. `contentType` is `application/json`, `text/plain` or `text/html`.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, WebAppFirewall } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const firewall = new WebAppFirewall({
    scope: 'regional',
    customResponseBodies: {
      blockedResponse: {
        contentType: 'application/json',
        content: '{"error": "Request blocked by firewall", "code": "WAF_BLOCKED"}'
      }
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { firewall }
  };
});
```


The rule types here don't select a body per rule. For that and other WAF settings Stacktape doesn't expose, use
[overrides](/configuration/overrides-and-escape-hatches).

## Basic example

A file system needs no properties:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, EfsFilesystem } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const sharedStorage = new EfsFilesystem({});

  return {
    resources: { sharedStorage }
  };
});
```


It uses elastic throughput. Mount it into a workload with `volumeMounts`:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  WorkerService,
  EfsFilesystem,
  JsBundleImagePackaging
} from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const sharedData = new EfsFilesystem({});

  const worker = new WorkerService({
    packaging: new JsBundleImagePackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/worker.ts' }),
    resources: { cpu: 0.5, memory: 1024 },
    connectTo: [sharedData],
    volumeMounts: [
      {
        type: 'efs',
        properties: {
          efsFilesystemName: 'sharedData',
          mountPath: '/mnt/shared',
          rootDirectory: '/workers'
        }
      }
    ]
  });

  return { resources: { sharedData, worker } };
});
```


The worker sees the file system at `/mnt/shared`, limited to its `/workers` directory. Every container that mounts it
sees the same files. Web services, private services, multi-container workloads and
[Lambda functions](/resources/compute/lambda-function#storage) (with `joinDefaultVpc: true`) mount it the same way.
`stacktape dev` doesn't mount EFS, so test file access on a deployed stage.

### Provisioned

A fixed rate regardless of how much data is stored, for example `provisionedThroughputInMibps: 100` for 100 MiB/s. It
suits video pipelines or analytics that move a lot of data all the time. You pay for the provisioned rate even when
idle. You can change it any time.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, EfsFilesystem } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const videoStorage = new EfsFilesystem({
    throughputMode: 'provisioned',
    provisionedThroughputInMibps: 100
  });

  return {
    resources: { videoStorage }
  };
});
```

## Backups

`backupEnabled: true` turns on daily AWS Backup backups kept for 35 days. They are incremental, so you pay for the
changed data. Turn it on for data you can't recreate, such as user uploads. The schedule and retention can't be
changed here.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, EfsFilesystem } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const sharedStorage = new EfsFilesystem({
    backupEnabled: true
  });

  return {
    resources: { sharedStorage }
  };
});
```

## Basic example

A bucket needs no properties:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Bucket } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const uploads = new Bucket({});

  return {
    resources: { uploads }
  };
});
```


The bucket is private: only workloads you connect with [`connectTo`](#connecting-to-a-bucket) and IAM identities with
permission can read or write it.

## Uploading files on deploy

The `directoryUpload` property syncs a local directory to the bucket on every deployment. Stacktape mirrors the directory exactly — files in the bucket that are not in the local directory get deleted.


> **Warning:** Directory upload replaces all existing bucket contents. Do not use this for buckets that store user-uploaded or application-generated files. It is designed for deploying build output, static assets, or configuration files.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Bucket } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const assets = new Bucket({
    directoryUpload: {
      directoryPath: './dist',
      headersPreset: 'single-page-app'
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { assets }
  };
});
```


The `headersPreset` property sets optimized HTTP caching headers for your framework. Available presets:

| Preset | Behavior | Best for |
|---|---|---|
| `single-page-app` | HTML never cached, hashed assets cached forever | React, Vue, Angular SPAs |
| `static-website` | CDN-cached, never browser-cached | Generic static sites |
| `astro-static-website` | Hashed build assets cached forever, HTML always fresh | Astro static builds |
| `sveltekit-static-website` | Hashed build assets cached forever, HTML always fresh | SvelteKit static builds |
| `nuxt-static-website` | Hashed build assets cached forever, HTML always fresh | Nuxt static builds |
| `gatsby-static-website` | Supported Gatsby preset | Gatsby builds |

For more control, use `fileOptions` to set custom HTTP headers and tags on files matching specific glob patterns. For example, set a long `Cache-Control` header on image files while keeping HTML files uncached. Use `excludeFilesPatterns` to skip specific files during upload.

S3 Transfer Acceleration is enabled by default for faster uploads from distant locations. Disable it with `disableS3TransferAcceleration: true` to avoid the small per-GB surcharge.

## Access control

Bucket access control determines who can read from and write to the bucket. The `accessibility.accessibilityMode` property supports three modes:

| Mode | Who can read | Who can write | Default |
|---|---|---|---|
| `private` | Only resources with IAM permissions | Only resources with IAM permissions | Yes |
| `public-read` | Anyone on the internet | Only resources with IAM permissions | No |
| `public-read-write` | Anyone on the internet | Anyone on the internet | No |

Most buckets should stay `private`. Use [`connectTo`](/configuration/connecting-resources) to grant your compute resources access — `connectTo` grants bucket read, write, and delete object permissions automatically. Use `public-read` only when you need to serve files directly to browsers without signed URLs. Avoid `public-read-write` unless you have a specific reason — it allows anyone on the internet to write to your bucket.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Bucket } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const publicAssets = new Bucket({
    accessibility: {
      accessibilityMode: 'public-read'
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { publicAssets }
  };
});
```


For fine-grained access control, use `accessibility.accessPolicyStatements` for advanced access configuration with bucket policy IAM role statements.

## CORS

CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) configuration is needed when browsers make requests directly to your S3 bucket, for example uploading files via presigned URLs from a frontend app. If your application only accesses the bucket from server-side code (Lambda functions, containers), you don't need CORS.

Set `cors.enabled: true` without `corsRules` to use permissive defaults (`*` origins, all methods). Add `corsRules` to replace those defaults with explicit browser access rules for production.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Bucket } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const uploads = new Bucket({
    cors: {
      enabled: true,
      corsRules: [
        {
          allowedOrigins: ['https://myapp.com'],
          allowedMethods: ['GET', 'PUT'],
          allowedHeaders: ['*'],
          maxAge: 3600
        }
      ]
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { uploads }
  };
});
```


When `corsRules` are provided, they define the explicit browser access rules for your bucket. Each rule specifies `allowedOrigins`, `allowedMethods`, `allowedHeaders`, optional `exposedResponseHeaders` (headers accessible to browser JavaScript), and `maxAge` (how long in seconds browsers can cache preflight responses). Rules are evaluated in order; the first matching rule wins for each preflight request. Allowed methods can be `GET`, `POST`, `PUT`, `PATCH`, `DELETE`, `HEAD`, or `*` for any.

## CDN

Placing a CDN (Amazon CloudFront) in front of a bucket reduces download latency for global users, lowers bandwidth costs (CloudFront data transfer is cheaper than direct S3 transfer), and enables edge caching for frequently accessed files. Configure it with the `cdn` property.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Bucket } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const spa = new Bucket({
    directoryUpload: {
      directoryPath: './dist',
      headersPreset: 'single-page-app'
    },
    cdn: {
      enabled: true,
      rewriteRoutesForSinglePageApp: true
    }
  });

  return {
    resources: { spa }
  };
});
```


The `rewriteRoutesForSinglePageApp` option routes requests without a file extension (e.g., `/about`, `/dashboard`) to `index.html`, letting your SPA handle client-side routing. Requests with extensions (`.js`, `.css`, `.png`) serve the actual file. Enable `rewriteRoutesForSinglePageApp` only for browser-routed SPAs where URLs like `/dashboard` should load `index.html`. Leave it disabled for ordinary file buckets or assets where extensionless keys should be looked up as stored objects.

Bucket CDN URL normalization is enabled by default for clean HTML URLs. Disable it with `disableUrlNormalization: true` only when your bucket object keys intentionally rely on exact URL paths.

Enable it when browsers download files from the bucket, especially users far from your region. Buckets that only your
backend reads don't need it. To serve the files at your own domain, set `cdn.customDomains`; see
[Custom domains](/resources/networking/custom-domains).

The bucket `cdn` property adds bucket-specific options `rewriteRoutesForSinglePageApp` and `disableUrlNormalization` on top of the shared CDN configuration. See the [CDN resource page](/resources/networking/cdn) for shared CDN options.


> **Tip:** If you're building a static website or SPA with CDN, client-side routing, and custom domains, a [HostingBucket](/resources/frontend/static-hosting) bundles all of this in a single resource type.

## Lifecycle rules

Lifecycle rules automate storage cost management by expiring objects, transitioning them to cheaper storage classes, or cleaning up incomplete uploads. Configure them with the `lifecycleRules` array.

Stacktape supports five lifecycle rule types:

| Rule type | What it does |
|---|---|
| `expiration` | Delete objects after a set number of days since upload |
| `non-current-version-expiration` | Delete old object versions after a set number of days (requires `versioning: true`) |
| `class-transition` | Move objects to a cheaper storage class after a set number of days |
| `non-current-version-class-transition` | Move old versions to a cheaper storage class after they become non-current; use with `versioning: true` |
| `abort-incomplete-multipart-upload` | Clean up incomplete multipart uploads after a set number of days |


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Bucket } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const dataLake = new Bucket({
    lifecycleRules: [
      {
        type: 'class-transition',
        properties: {
          daysAfterUpload: 30,
          storageClass: 'STANDARD_IA',
          prefix: 'logs/'
        }
      },
      {
        type: 'expiration',
        properties: {
          daysAfterUpload: 365,
          prefix: 'logs/'
        }
      },
      {
        type: 'abort-incomplete-multipart-upload',
        properties: {
          daysAfterInitiation: 7
        }
      }
    ]
  });

  return {
    resources: { dataLake }
  };
});
```


This example moves log files to cheaper storage after 30 days, deletes them after a year, and cleans up failed multipart uploads after 7 days. Each rule supports optional `prefix` and `tags` filters to target specific objects.

## Versioning and encryption

**Versioning** keeps previous versions of overwritten or deleted objects, letting you recover from accidental changes. Enable it with `versioning: true`. Versioning is a prerequisite for `non-current-version-expiration` and `non-current-version-class-transition` lifecycle rules. Enable versioning for buckets where accidental overwrites would be costly — user uploads, application data, configuration files. Skip it for ephemeral data like logs or build caches where old versions have no value. Note that versioning increases storage costs since all object versions are retained until explicitly expired by a lifecycle rule.

**Encryption** sets `encryption: true` to configure AES-256 server-side encryption at rest on the bucket. The source JSDoc describes this as "Encrypt stored objects at rest (AES-256)." If you need different encryption (e.g., AWS KMS customer-managed keys), use [overrides](/configuration/overrides-and-escape-hatches) on the underlying CloudFormation resource.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Bucket } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const secureBucket = new Bucket({
    versioning: true,
    encryption: true,
    lifecycleRules: [
      {
        type: 'non-current-version-expiration',
        properties: {
          daysAfterVersioned: 30
        }
      }
    ]
  });

  return {
    resources: { secureBucket }
  };
});
```


When versioning is enabled, pair it with a `non-current-version-expiration` rule to automatically clean up old versions and avoid unbounded storage growth. In this example, old versions are deleted 30 days after they become non-current (i.e., after a newer version replaces them).

## Event-driven workflows

Stacktape buckets support two approaches for reacting to object changes:

**S3 event triggers** invoke a [Lambda function](/resources/compute/lambda-function) directly for configured S3 event types — commonly object-created, object-removed, and object-restore events. The trigger type also supports replication-related and reduced-redundancy lost-object events through the [S3 trigger configuration](/resources/triggers/s3-events). This is the simplest approach for processing uploads, for example resizing images or parsing CSV files. Configure S3 triggers on the Lambda function side.

**EventBridge notifications** send bucket events to [EventBridge](/resources/messaging/event-bus), where multiple consumers can react to the same event independently. Enable with `enableEventBusNotifications: true` (defaults to `false`). Use EventBridge notifications when bucket events should be available on EventBridge for other parts of your architecture. For direct upload processing, an S3 trigger on a Lambda function is usually simpler.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Bucket } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const uploads = new Bucket({
    enableEventBusNotifications: true
  });

  return {
    resources: { uploads }
  };
});
```


For most use cases, an S3 event trigger on a Lambda function is simpler and sufficient. Choose EventBridge when you need fan-out to multiple consumers or complex event routing rules.

## Connecting to a bucket

Add the bucket to a workload's [`connectTo`](/configuration/connecting-resources). The workload can then read, write and
delete objects and gets the bucket's name and ARN as environment variables.


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, Bucket, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const uploads = new Bucket({});

  const processor = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/processor.ts'
    }),
    connectTo: [uploads]
  });

  return {
    resources: { uploads, processor }
  };
});
```


The `processor` function receives these environment variables at runtime:

| Variable | Description |
|---|---|
| `STP_UPLOADS_NAME` | The S3 bucket name |
| `STP_UPLOADS_ARN` | The S3 bucket ARN |

Use the bucket name with any AWS S3 SDK to read and write objects:

```typescript
import { S3Client, PutObjectCommand } from '@aws-sdk/client-s3';

const s3 = new S3Client({});

export default async () => {
  await s3.send(new PutObjectCommand({
    Bucket: process.env.STP_UPLOADS_NAME,
    Key: 'example.txt',
    Body: 'Hello from Stacktape'
  }));

  return { statusCode: 200, body: JSON.stringify({ message: 'Uploaded' }) };
};
```

For uploads from a browser, generate a presigned URL in your backend and let the browser upload straight to S3, so large
files don't pass through your API. That needs [CORS](#cors) on the bucket. To run code when a file arrives, see
[Event-driven workflows](#event-driven-workflows).

## Basic example

The alarm lives on the resource it watches. The trigger names it by the resource's name, `alarms` and the alarm's
position in that list:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/api.ts' }),
    alarms: [
      {
        description: 'API error rate above 5%',
        trigger: { type: 'lambda-error-rate', properties: { thresholdPercent: 5 } }
      }
    ]
  });

  const onApiErrors = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/on-api-errors.ts' }),
    events: [
      {
        type: 'cloudwatch-alarm',
        properties: { alarmName: 'api.alarms.0' }
      }
    ]
  });

  return {
    resources: { api, onApiErrors }
  };
});
```


`api.alarms.0` is the first alarm in the `alarms` list of the `api` resource. An alarm that you defined in the Console
for a resource is `<resource>.alarms.<alarm name>`. The alarm must be in the same stack.

Several functions can reference the same alarm. Each one gets its own EventBridge rule and runs independently.

## Handling the event

The function receives the CloudWatch "Alarm State Change" event from EventBridge. It runs only when the alarm moves
into `ALARM`, not when it recovers.

```typescript
type AlarmEvent = {
  detail: {
    alarmName: string;
    state: { value: string; reason: string; timestamp: string };
    previousState: { value: string };
  };
};

export default async (event: AlarmEvent) => {
  const { alarmName, state } = event.detail;
  console.info(`${alarmName} is ${state.value}: ${state.reason}`);
  // remediation or paging logic
};
```

The [AWS event reference](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/monitoring/cloudwatch-and-eventbridge.html)
lists every field.

The event arrives asynchronously. If the function throws, Lambda retries it twice, so make side effects idempotent: an
alarm that pages twice for one incident is noise. To keep events that still fail, set
[`destinations.onFailure`](/resources/compute/lambda-function#api-ref_lambda-function-props--destinations) on the
function.

## Basic example

This function receives the `ERROR` lines of another function in the stack:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import { defineConfig, LambdaFunction, JsBundleLambdaPackaging, $ResourceParam } from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/api.ts' })
  });

  const errorWatcher = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/error-watcher.ts' }),
    events: [
      {
        type: 'cloudwatch-log',
        properties: {
          logGroupArn: $ResourceParam('api', 'logGroupArn'),
          filter: 'ERROR'
        }
      }
    ]
  });

  return {
    resources: { api, errorWatcher }
  };
});
```


`logGroupArn` takes the ARN of any log group. Lambda functions in the stack expose theirs as the `logGroupArn`
[parameter](/configuration/referenceable-parameters). For a log group outside the stack, paste its ARN.

The payload arrives base64-encoded and gzipped. Decode it before you read it:

```typescript
import { gunzipSync } from 'node:zlib';

export default async (event: { awslogs: { data: string } }) => {
  const logData = JSON.parse(gunzipSync(Buffer.from(event.awslogs.data, 'base64')).toString());
  // logData.logGroup, logData.logStream
  for (const logEvent of logData.logEvents) {
    console.info(logEvent.id, new Date(logEvent.timestamp).toISOString(), logEvent.message);
  }
};
```

One invocation carries a batch of log events. CloudWatch decides the batch size; the trigger has no setting for it.
Always loop over `logEvents`.

## Basic example

Turn on the table's stream with `streamType`, then pass its ARN to the trigger:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  LambdaFunction,
  DynamoDbTable,
  DynamoDbIntegration,
  $ResourceParam,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const ordersTable = new DynamoDbTable({
    primaryKey: {
      partitionKey: { name: 'id', type: 'string' }
    },
    streamType: 'NEW_AND_OLD_IMAGES'
  });

  const streamProcessor = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/process-stream.ts'
    }),
    events: [
      new DynamoDbIntegration({
        streamArn: $ResourceParam('ordersTable', 'streamArn')
      })
    ]
  });

  return {
    resources: { ordersTable, streamProcessor }
  };
});
```


The table has a `streamArn` parameter only when `streamType` is set. For a table outside the stack, pass its stream ARN
as a string; the function's role gets read access to that stream.

`streamType` decides what each record contains:

| `streamType` | Record contains |
|---|---|
| `KEYS_ONLY` | The item's key |
| `NEW_IMAGE` | The item after the change |
| `OLD_IMAGE` | The item before the change |
| `NEW_AND_OLD_IMAGES` | Both, for diffs and audit logs |

Each record has an `eventName` (`INSERT`, `MODIFY` or `REMOVE`) and the images in DynamoDB's attribute format
(`{ S: 'hello' }`). `unmarshall` from `@aws-sdk/util-dynamodb` turns them into plain objects:

```typescript
import { unmarshall } from '@aws-sdk/util-dynamodb';

export default async (event: { Records: Array<{ eventName: string; dynamodb: { NewImage?: any; OldImage?: any } }> }) => {
  for (const record of event.Records) {
    const before = record.dynamodb.OldImage && unmarshall(record.dynamodb.OldImage);
    const after = record.dynamodb.NewImage && unmarshall(record.dynamodb.NewImage);
    console.info(record.eventName, before, after);
  }
};
```

After `stacktape deploy`, write an item and read the function's logs. AWS can take a minute or two to start polling a
new trigger.

```bash
aws dynamodb put-item --table-name <table-name> --item '{"id":{"S":"order-1"}}'
```

```bash
stacktape logs streamProcessor --stage dev
```

`stacktape param get ordersTable name --stage dev --region eu-west-1` prints the table name.

`startingPosition` is `TRIM_HORIZON` by default: on the first deploy the function reads every change still in the
stream. Set `LATEST` to read only changes made after the trigger exists.

## Failures and retries

If the function throws, Lambda retries the whole batch, including records it already processed, and doesn't move past
it. By default it keeps retrying until the records expire after 24 hours, and the shard waits behind them. Make the
handler idempotent, for example by keying writes on the item key, and give the trigger a way out:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  LambdaFunction,
  DynamoDbTable,
  DynamoDbIntegration,
  SqsQueue,
  $ResourceParam,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const ordersTable = new DynamoDbTable({
    primaryKey: { partitionKey: { name: 'id', type: 'string' } },
    streamType: 'NEW_AND_OLD_IMAGES'
  });
  const failedChanges = new SqsQueue({});

  const streamProcessor = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({ entryfilePath: './src/process-stream.ts' }),
    events: [
      new DynamoDbIntegration({
        streamArn: $ResourceParam('ordersTable', 'streamArn'),
        maximumRetryAttempts: 3,
        bisectBatchOnFunctionError: true,
        onFailure: { arn: $ResourceParam('failedChanges', 'arn'), type: 'sqs' }
      })
    ]
  });

  return { resources: { ordersTable, failedChanges, streamProcessor } };
});
```


- `maximumRetryAttempts` stops retrying a batch after this many attempts.
- `bisectBatchOnFunctionError` splits a failing batch in two before each retry, which narrows it down to the bad record.
- `reportBatchItemFailures: true` lets the handler return `{ batchItemFailures: [{ itemIdentifier }] }` with the
  sequence number of the first record it couldn't process. Lambda retries from that record instead of the whole batch.
- `onFailure` names an SQS queue or SNS topic for batches that still fail.

The `onFailure` destination receives a description of the failed batch, not the changes: the shard, the sequence number
range and the error. You can read the records from the stream by sequence number until they expire 24 hours after the
write. If you need the changed items kept longer, have the handler save failed records to S3 or a queue itself.

## Basic example


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  LambdaFunction,
  EventBus,
  EventBusIntegration,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';
export default defineConfig(() => {
  const orderBus = new EventBus({});

  const orderProcessor = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/order-processor.ts'
    }),
    events: [
      new EventBusIntegration({
        eventBusName: 'orderBus',
        eventPattern: {
          source: ['my-app.orders'],
          'detail-type': ['OrderPlaced']
        }
      })
    ]
  });

  return {
    resources: { orderBus, orderProcessor }
  };
});
```


The function runs for every `OrderPlaced` event from `my-app.orders`. It receives the whole EventBridge event; your data
is in `detail`. After `stacktape deploy`, publish a test event:

```bash
aws events put-events --entries '[{"EventBusName":"<bus-name>","Source":"my-app.orders","DetailType":"OrderPlaced","Detail":"{\"orderId\":\"42\"}"}]'
```

```bash
stacktape logs orderProcessor --stage dev
```

`stacktape param get orderBus arn --stage dev --region eu-west-1` prints the bus ARN; the bus name is its last part.

## Change what the function receives

Set one of these to send something other than the whole event:

- `inputPath: '$.detail'` sends only your payload. This is the common one.
- `input` sends a fixed JSON value.
- `inputTransformer` picks values with `inputPathsMap` and places them into an `inputTemplate`.

```typescript
new EventBusIntegration({
  eventBusName: 'orderBus',
  eventPattern: { 'detail-type': ['OrderPlaced'] },
  inputTransformer: {
    inputPathsMap: { orderId: '$.detail.orderId', customer: '$.detail.customerName' },
    inputTemplate: { message: 'Order <orderId> placed by <customer>.' }
  }
})
```

For an order `12345` by `Jane`, the function receives `{"message":"Order 12345 placed by Jane."}`.

## HTTP API Gateway

Define the gateway as a resource and point the function's integration at it:


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  LambdaFunction,
  HttpApiGateway,
  HttpApiIntegration,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const api = new HttpApiGateway({});

  const app = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/handler.ts'
    }),
    events: [
      new HttpApiIntegration({
        httpApiGatewayName: 'api',
        method: '*',
        path: '/{proxy+}'
      })
    ]
  });

  return {
    resources: { api, app }
  };
});
```


`method: '*'` with `path: '/{proxy+}'` sends every request to one function. That suits an app with its own router,
such as Hono, Express or Fastify. The deploy prints the gateway URL; `stacktape param get api url --stage dev --region
eu-west-1` prints it again. Call it with `curl <url>/anything`.

## Application Load Balancer


Example (TypeScript):

```typescript
import {
  defineConfig,
  LambdaFunction,
  ApplicationLoadBalancer,
  ApplicationLoadBalancerIntegration,
  JsBundleLambdaPackaging
} from 'stacktape';

export default defineConfig(() => {
  const alb = new ApplicationLoadBalancer({});

  const usersApi = new LambdaFunction({
    packaging: new JsBundleLambdaPackaging({
      entryfilePath: './src/users.ts'
    }),
    events: [
      new ApplicationLoadBalancerIntegration({
        loadBalancerName: 'alb',
        priority: 10,
        paths: ['/users', '/users/*'],
        methods: ['GET', 'POST']
      })
    ]
  });

  return {
    resources: { alb, usersApi }
  };
});
```


The function handles `GET` and `POST` on `/users` and everything under it. A request must match every condition you
set; within a list such as `paths`, any value matches.

| Condition | Matches |
|---|---|
| `paths` | URL paths, case-sensitive, with `*` and `?` wildcards |
| `methods` | HTTP methods |
| `hosts` | The `Host` header, with wildcards such as `*.example.com` |
| `headers` | A `headerName` and a list of `values` |
| `queryParams` | A `paramName` and a list of `values` |
| `sourceIps` | Client IPs in CIDR notation |

`priority` is required. The load balancer checks rules from the lowest number up and the first match handles the
request, so a broad rule with a low number hides more specific rules behind it. Numbers must be unique per listener.
Leave gaps (10, 20, 30) so you can add rules later. Set `listenerPort` only when the load balancer has custom
listeners.

